(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["mixed",{"l:mixed/why-does-mixing-topics-in-practice-beat-doing-th":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inside a block of problems from one module you know the technique before you read the statement, so you practise executing it but never practise \u003cb>choosing\u003c/b> it. Mixing problems from different modules (\u003cb>interleaving\u003c/b>) forces the choice, and spreading them over days (\u003cb>spacing\u003c/b>) forces you to rebuild the idea from memory instead of copying it from the last problem. Both feel slower while you practise, and both win clearly on tests weeks later.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture learning to drive only in an empty car park, one manoeuvre at a time: twenty hill starts, then twenty reverse parks. Each one goes well because you know what is coming. On a real road nobody announces the manoeuvre; recognising which one the situation needs is half the skill. A problem list filed under \"Sliding window\" is the car park: the label answers the hardest question for you.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Algorithm problems are the same. \"Longest substring with at most k distinct letters\" and \"count the subarrays whose sum is k\" look alike, yet the first is a window and the second needs prefix sums. If you only ever meet them inside their own modules, you never practise telling them apart, and the first time you must, it is in an interview.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Three schedules\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>blocked      W W W W   P P P P   T T T T     \"which idea?\" is answered by the module title\ninterleaved  W P T P   T W P W   P T W T     every problem starts with \"which idea?\"\nspaced       day 1: W P T      day 4: P W T      day 11: T W P\n             (W = window, P = prefix sums, T = two pointers)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>What the evidence says\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Interleaving builds strategy choice.\u003c/b> Rohrer, Dedrick and Stershic gave 126 seventh-grade students the same maths problems over three months, arranged either in blocks or interleaved. Interleaving won on a surprise test a day later (effect size d = 0.42) and by more thirty days later (d = 0.79). Their explanation is the one above: with blocked practice, students know the strategy before they read the problem.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It holds in real classrooms.\u003c/b> In a preregistered trial with 54 classes over four months, the interleaved group scored 61% against 38% on an unannounced test a month later (Rohrer and colleagues, 2020).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mix look-alikes, not everything.\u003c/b> A 2019 meta-analysis (Brunmair and Richter) found the benefit is largest when the categories are easy to confuse. That is why this guide pairs confusable techniques: window and prefix sums, BFS and Dijkstra, greedy and DP.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spacing beats more of the same.\u003c/b> Spreading ten problems over two sessions virtually doubled scores four weeks later, while doing nine problems instead of three in one sitting made no difference at one or four weeks (Rohrer and Taylor, 2006).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>It feels worse while it works better.\u003c/b> In a study summarised by Dunlosky, students who practised in blocks scored higher during practice, yet those who interleaved did three times better on the test a week later. Expect mixed practice to feel slow; that is the effect, not a sign of failure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>How this page builds it in\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>The \u003cb>review sets\u003c/b> in this module have no topic labels. Each opens when you have marked every card of the module it follows as done: set 1 after Prefix sums, set 2 after Monotonic stacks, set 3 after Tries, set 4 after Weighted shortest paths, set 5 after Greedy, set 6 after Bits and number theory. A set only uses ideas you have met.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Today's session\u003c/b> has one review slot. It offers the oldest problem or card you marked \u003cb>Brute only\u003c/b> or \u003cb>Stuck\u003c/b> that has become due (three days after you marked it). When nothing is due and a set is open, it offers the next unsolved problem of the earliest open set. One mixed problem a day spreads the sets out on their own.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The hints open one at a time: what to notice, then a nudge, then the key insight, which starts with the \u003cb>Pattern\u003c/b> line. Name the technique yourself, in writing, before you open anything.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Interleaving costs no extra problems; it changes the order. The real costs are two. Each problem takes longer, because the first two to five minutes go on choosing an idea. And your success rate during practice drops, which feels like going backwards. Budget one mixed problem per session: a Medium has a 30-minute timebox, so five sessions a week is about two and a half hours of mixed practice. Compare that with twenty window problems in one weekend: after the first three or four, the extra problems add almost nothing a month later (the overlearning result above).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>After you have met at least two techniques that can be confused. A brand-new technique is learnt best from a worked example and a few problems in a row (the cards and their Labs), and only then mixed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep short blocked bursts where they help: two or three problems right after a card get the mechanics into your hands. Then move on.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Do this\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You have just read a card\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Its 2-3 Lab problems, in a row\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A module is finished\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Its review set opens: one mixed problem per session\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Preparing for interviews\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mostly mixed problems, timed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A technique you keep failing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One short blocked burst, then back to mixed\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turning three topic pools into one interleaved week with the template below: always take the pool with the most problems left, unless it was the one just used.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: three pools; nothing chosen yet\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w1 w2 w3    prefix: p1 p2    twoptr: t1 t2\norder:  (empty)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">window has the most left (3), so take {{w1}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w2 w3    prefix: p1 p2    twoptr: t1 t2\norder:  \u003cmark>w1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">All pools have 2 left; window was just used anyway and ties go by name, so {{p1}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w2 w3    prefix: p2    twoptr: t1 t2\norder:  w1 \u003cmark>p1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">window and twoptr have 2 left; take {{t1}} (window would also do; ties go by name)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w2 w3    prefix: p2    twoptr: t2\norder:  w1 p1 \u003cmark>t1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">window has the most left (2): {{w2}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: w3    prefix: p2    twoptr: t2\norder:  w1 p1 t1 \u003cmark>w2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">One each left: {{p2}}, {{t2}}, then {{w3}}; no two neighbours share a topic\u003c/p>\u003cpre>order:  w1 p1 t1 w2 \u003cmark>p2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>t2\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>w3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\n\ndef interleave(pools):\n    \"\"\"pools: topic -&gt; list of problems. Returns [(topic, problem)] with no two neighbours from the\n    same topic whenever that is possible: take the largest remaining pool that is not the last topic.\"\"\"\n    queues = {t: list(v) for t, v in pools.items() if v}\n    heap = [(-len(v), t) for t, v in queues.items()]\n    heapq.heapify(heap)\n    out, last = [], None\n    while heap:\n        left, topic = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if topic == last and heap:\n            other = heapq.heappop(heap)\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (left, topic))\n            left, topic = other\n        out.append((topic, queues[topic].pop(0)))\n        last = topic\n        if left + 1 &lt; 0:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, (left + 1, topic))\n    return out\n\n\nweek = interleave({\"window\": [\"w1\", \"w2\", \"w3\"], \"prefix\": [\"p1\", \"p2\"], \"twoptr\": [\"t1\", \"t2\"]})\n# [('window', 'w1'), ('prefix', 'p1'), ('twoptr', 't1'), ('window', 'w2'), ('prefix', 'p2'), ...]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use it to build your own mixed sets from the problem banks of finished modules. It keeps each topic's order, and a clean result (no neighbours sharing a topic) exists exactly when no pool holds more than half of all problems, rounded up.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Peeking at the label.\u003c/b> The problem link opens the judge, which may show topic tags; do not scroll to them before you have chosen. The Solutions and Video links name the technique too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Grading generously.\u003c/b> Needing the nudge means the choice was not yours. Mark Brute only or Stuck so the problem comes back.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Binge-solving a set.\u003c/b> Five mixed problems in one evening is blocked practice again, just with variety; the spacing is gone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Struggling too long.\u003c/b> Difficulty helps only while it stays achievable. If after ten minutes you cannot name even two candidate ideas, open the first hint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixing only unrelated topics.\u003c/b> The gain comes from look-alikes. A set of a heap problem, a trie problem and a bit trick trains less choice than a window problem next to a prefix-sum problem.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">During a week of blocked practice your accuracy is 90%; in a week of mixed practice it is 60%. Which week taught you more, and why is the accuracy misleading?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Very likely the mixed week. Blocked accuracy is inflated because the block tells you the technique, so you never had to choose; the studies above show blocked groups doing better during practice and much worse weeks later. Mixed accuracy measures the skill you need, including the choice.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does this guide interleave window problems with prefix-sum problems rather than with, say, trie problems?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Interleaving helps most when the categories are easy to confuse (Brunmair and Richter's meta-analysis). Window and prefix-sum problems share their surface (\"subarray\", \"sum\", \"longest\"), so mixing them forces you to find the deciding feature; a trie problem is rarely mistaken for either.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Set 1 is open. You marked one problem Brute only on Monday evening and nothing else is marked. What does the review slot of Today's session offer on Tuesday, and what on Thursday evening?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On Tuesday nothing is due yet (an item becomes due three days after you marked it), so the slot offers the next unsolved problem of set 1. By Thursday evening the Monday item is due, so it is offered first, as a re-solve from a blank file.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You have three free hours this weekend and the DP modules are fresh. Plan A: twelve DP problems on Saturday. Plan B: four problems on Saturday and four on Sunday, mixing DP with earlier modules. Which do you choose?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Plan B. Spreading practice over two sessions roughly doubled retention four weeks later in Rohrer and Taylor's study, while extra problems in one sitting added nothing; mixing in earlier modules also trains the choice between DP and its look-alikes (greedy, backtracking).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do the first three problems of review set 1, one per session if you can. Before writing any code, write down the idea you think applies and a one-line reason; compare it with the Pattern line only after solving.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>219 Contains Duplicate II.\u003c/b> Expected: false for \u003ccode>nums = [4, 1, 2, 4], k = 2\u003c/code> (the two 4s are three apart) and true for \u003ccode>[4, 1, 4, 2]\u003c/code>; target O(n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1679 Max Number of K-Sum Pairs.\u003c/b> Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>nums = [2, 2, 3, 3, 3], k = 5\u003c/code>, and 2 for \u003ccode>[1, 1, 1, 1], k = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>930 Binary Subarrays With Sum.\u003c/b> Expected: 6 for \u003ccode>nums = [1, 0, 0, 1], goal = 1\u003c/code>, and 6 for \u003ccode>[0, 1, 0, 1], goal = 1\u003c/code>; target O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Expected for the whole Lab: you named an idea within about two minutes for each problem, and your written guess matched the Pattern line at least twice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know why mixed, spaced practice beats blocks, and how this page's review sets and Today's session schedule it for you.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/which-technique-does-a-problem-statement-point-t":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Read a statement for three things, in this order: the \u003cb>constraints\u003c/b> (which running times are even allowed), the \u003cb>shape of the answer\u003c/b> (one number, a count, every solution, a structure) and the \u003cb>structure of the input\u003c/b> (sorted, contiguous, pairs, grid, graph, stream). Together they usually leave two candidates; a \u003cb>counter-cue\u003c/b> decides between them. The atlas below lists the cues and counter-cues for every technique in this guide.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A doctor does not test for every disease. Symptoms suggest a short list, and one deciding test rules out the look-alike. Statement cues are the symptoms (\"longest contiguous\", \"fewest moves\", \"every way\") and counter-cues are the deciding tests (\"values can be negative\", \"moves cost different amounts\").\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The two-minute routine\u003c/h5>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Constraints.\u003c/b> Turn n into a target complexity (the template below). n &lt;= 20 invites exponential search; n = 10^5 demands O(n log n); n = 10^9 means you cannot even read n items.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Answer shape.\u003c/b> Every solution listed points to search; a count or a best value over choices points to DP or greedy; a yes/no on a number that only flips once points to binary search on the answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Input structure.\u003c/b> Sorted? Contiguous? Intervals? A grid or links? A stream?\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Atlas row, then its counter-cue.\u003c/b> If the counter-cue fires, move to the row it names.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Nothing fits?\u003c/b> Write the brute force and climb from it (the module Deriving solutions to unfamiliar problems).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch5>The atlas\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Statement cue\u003c/th>\u003cth>Technique\u003c/th>\u003cth>Module\u003c/th>\u003cth>Counter-cue, then use\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No idea at all, n tiny\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Brute force with itertools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Solving problems you have not seen\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Always a valid first step and your test oracle\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Seen this value or its complement before?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Set or dict lookup\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hash tables, sets and counting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Need order, ranges or nearest values: sort or an ordered map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count, group, anagram, duplicates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Counter or a canonical key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hash tables, sets and counting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only the top k counts: add a heap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Values in 1..n, O(1) extra space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Array as its own index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Values unbounded: hashing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rotate, transpose, spiral, simulate on a matrix\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Index arithmetic and reversals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewest moves across the grid: BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Brackets, nesting, undo, most recent first\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Linked lists, stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Next greater\": monotonic stack\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Next pointers; cycle, middle, k-th from the end\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pointer rewiring, fast and slow pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Linked lists, stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Random access needed: copy to a list\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted input, pair or triple with a target\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers from both ends\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unsorted and indices wanted: hash map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Remove, compact or partition in place\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Read and write pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Extra space allowed and order kept: build a new list\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Longest or shortest contiguous stretch under a condition\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Variable sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Negative values or an exact target: prefix sums\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Every window of length k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Window maximum: monotonic deque\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count subarrays with sum, remainder or balance exactly k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums plus a count map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums and difference arrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Non-negative values and \"at most\": a window works too\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many range sums on unchanging data\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1D or 2D prefix sums\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums and difference arrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Updates between queries: Fenwick tree\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many range additions, one read at the end\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Difference array\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums and difference arrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reads between updates: Fenwick tree\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted or rotated array, find a position\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search on the index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search and searching the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Unsorted, one query: just scan\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Minimise the maximum, smallest x that works\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search on the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search and searching the answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check not monotone in x: DP or search\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Next greater or smaller, \"until a taller one\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>k-th greater rather than next: ordered structure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Maximum or minimum of every window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic deque\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A single window: one scan\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum of the minimum over all subarrays\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contribution counting with a monotonic stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stacks and queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>n &lt;= 1000: running minimum over all subarrays\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Order would make it easy: neighbours, groups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sort first, then one pass\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sorting, partitioning and selection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Data arrives over time: heap or ordered map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>k-th smallest of fixed data, asked once\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quickselect, heapq.nsmallest or sort\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sorting, partitioning and selection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many queries or a stream: heap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Repeatedly take the best while items arrive\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heaps and priority queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One query on fixed data: sort or select\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Merge k sorted sources; running median\u003c/td>\u003ctd>k-way merge; two heaps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heaps and priority queues\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Arbitrary deletions: ordered map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Start and end pairs: merge, overlap, free time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sort by start and merge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals and sweep line\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Peak overlap: sweep events\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Rooms needed, peak load\u003c/td>\u003ctd>+1/-1 event sweep or a heap of end times\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals and sweep line\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weighted intervals: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Most non-overlapping intervals\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Earliest end first\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals and sweep line\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Intervals carry values: DP with bisect\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Root and children; answer combines subtrees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recursion returning values up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary trees and tree recursion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Answer depends on the path from the root: pass state down\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Level by level, nearest to the root\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Breadth-first traversal by levels\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary trees and tree recursion\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Path-shaped questions: DFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted data with inserts and deletes; floor, ceiling, rank\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ordered map: SortedList or bisect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Binary search trees and ordered maps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No updates: sorted list plus bisect\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many words sharing prefixes; words in a grid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Trie\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact lookups only: a set\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Grid cells or linked entities; reachability, components\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS or DFS flood fill\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Links only added, queries online: union-find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fewest moves, every move costs the same\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS, multi-source if many starts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Moves cost different amounts: Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prerequisites, dependency order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Topological sort\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Undirected: union-find or parent tracking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two groups with \"must differ\" pairs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two-colouring\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Graph modelling and traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Must be together\" pairs: union-find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Same group?\" while links are added\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find and minimum spanning trees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Need paths or distances: BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Connect everything at least total cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Kruskal or Prim\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find and minimum spanning trees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One cheapest route: shortest paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weighted edges, cheapest route, weights &gt;= 0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weighted shortest paths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Negative edges: Bellman-Ford; costs 0 or 1: 0-1 BFS\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>At most k stops\u003c/td>\u003ctd>k rounds of Bellman-Ford, or Dijkstra on (node, stops)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weighted shortest paths\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No hop limit: Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>List all, every, generate; n around 20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Backtracking with pruning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Complete search and backtracking\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only a count or best: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count ways or optimise over choices; states repeat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memoised recursion, then a table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One choice provably safe: greedy\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Modes: holding or not, cooldown\u003c/td>\u003ctd>State-machine DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No modes: take or skip\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Two sequences: subsequence, edit, align\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two-sequence DP table\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on grids, strings and knapsacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contiguous only: window or hashing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Right and down moves on a grid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Grid DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on grids, strings and knapsacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Moves in all directions: graph search\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Items, weights, capacity or target sum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Knapsack DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP on grids, strings and knapsacks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Huge capacity, n &lt;= 40: meet in the middle\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>A local choice looks safe; reach, fuel, balance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greedy with an exchange argument\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A small counterexample breaks it: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Burst or merge over a range; subsets of &lt;= 20; digits of N\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interval, bitmask or digit DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Advanced dynamic programming\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple choices: ordinary DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Implement a class\" with costs per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hash map plus a list, heap or sorted list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Designing data structures\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One operation only: one structure\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Find a pattern in a long text; repeats\u003c/td>\u003ctd>KMP or rolling hash\u003c/td>\u003ctd>String algorithms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short text: str.find\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Once while others appear twice; subsets as masks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>XOR and bitmasks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bits and number theory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Other multiplicities: count bits per position\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Modulo 10^9 + 7, gcd, primes, nCr\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Modular arithmetic, sieve, combinations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bits and number theory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small values: compute directly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Range queries mixed with updates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fenwick or segment tree\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Range queries: Fenwick and segment trees\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No updates: prefix sums or a sparse table\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The routine takes about two minutes. A wrong technique typically costs 20 to 30 minutes before you notice, so even one avoided miss per week pays for the habit many times over. The template's estimate is O(1) per call: seven formulas.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On every unfamiliar statement, before writing code; and after every solve, to add or sharpen a row in your own copy. A row only suggests: the counter-cue and a hand-worked example decide. When two rows match, the problem is often both: sort then sweep, Dijkstra then DP over distances, BFS inside binary search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Classifying a statement nobody labelled: \"n &lt;= 10^5 positive integers; find the length of the shortest contiguous stretch whose sum is at least T.\"\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Constraints first: n = 10^5, so the budget allows {{O(n log n)}}; all pairs (5 * 10^9) is out\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 10^5   -&gt;   target O(n log n) or better\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Answer shape: one length, a best value over many stretches\u003c/p>\u003cpre>shape: \u003cmark>optimise over ranges\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input structure: one array, the stretch must be {{contiguous}}, values all {{positive}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>structure: contiguous, positive\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Atlas rows that match: \"shortest contiguous stretch\" (window) and \"sum\" (prefix sums)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>candidates: \u003cmark>variable window\u003c/mark>  |  prefix sums\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Counter-cue check: values are positive, so adding grows the sum and removing shrinks it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window counter-cue (\"negative values\") does \u003cmark>not\u003c/mark> fire\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Decision: variable window, O(n). Change one word, \"integers may be negative\", and the counter-cue fires\u003c/p>\u003cpre>positive -&gt; \u003cmark>window\u003c/mark>\nmay be negative -&gt; \u003cmark>prefix sums + monotonic deque\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\n\nCLASSES = [\n    (\"O(n!)\", lambda n: math.factorial(n) if n &lt;= 20 else math.inf),\n    (\"O(2^n)\", lambda n: 2.0 ** n if n &lt;= 1000 else math.inf),\n    (\"O(n^3)\", lambda n: float(n) ** 3),\n    (\"O(n^2)\", lambda n: float(n) ** 2),\n    (\"O(n sqrt n)\", lambda n: n * math.sqrt(n)),\n    (\"O(n log n)\", lambda n: n * max(1.0, math.log2(n))),\n    (\"O(n)\", lambda n: float(n)),\n]\n\n\ndef target_complexity(n, budget=10**7):\n    \"\"\"Slowest complexity class whose operation count fits the budget (about one second of simple\n    Python operations). Anything faster also fits; anything slower will not.\"\"\"\n    for name, ops in CLASSES:\n        if ops(n) &lt;= budget:\n            return name\n    return \"O(log n) or O(1): you cannot even read n items\"\n\n\n# n = 8: O(n!)   20: O(2^n)   1000: O(n^2)   5000: O(n sqrt n)   10^5: O(n log n)\n# 10^6: O(n)     10^9: O(log n) or O(1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The budget of 10^7 simple operations per second is a rule of thumb for pure Python; work done inside C built-ins (\u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sum\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> operations) runs 10 to 100 times faster.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Matching surface words.\u003c/b> \"Subarray\" does not always mean a window, and a \"tree\" of dependencies may be a general graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ignoring small constraints.\u003c/b> n &lt;= 20 is an invitation: try every subset or permutation, or use bitmasks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stopping at the first row.\u003c/b> Always check the counter-cue; it exists because the look-alike is common.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting combinations.\u003c/b> Many Mediums are two rows at once: sort then two pointers, Dijkstra then DP.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>target_complexity(2000)\u003c/code> return, and is an O(n^2 log n) idea safe at n = 2000?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\"O(n^2)\": n^3 = 8·10^9 is far over the budget and n^2 = 4·10^6 fits. n^2 log n is about 4.4·10^7 simple operations, several seconds in pure Python, so it is risky; look for O(n^2) or move the inner work into C built-ins.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague classifies \"fewest knight moves between two squares of a chessboard\" as grid DP. Which cue did they miss?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Grid DP needs moves in a fixed direction (right and down), so the cells form an acyclic order. A knight moves in all directions, so the board is a graph with cycles and every move costs one: BFS.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does \"values may be negative\" rule out the variable window for \"shortest stretch with sum at least T\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The window relies on monotone validity: removing an element from the left must never make an invalid window valid. With negatives, dropping a negative element raises the sum, so the window may stop shrinking too early (or never become valid) and miss shorter answers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each problem write the atlas row you used and its counter-cue before coding, then compare with the Pattern line.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>438 Find All Anagrams in a String.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[0, 1, 3]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>s = \"cbacab\", p = \"abc\"\u003c/code>; target O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>189 Rotate Array.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[4, 5, 1, 2, 3]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3], k = 4\u003c/code> (k larger than n); O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1657 Determine if Two Strings Are Close.\u003c/b> Expected: true for \u003ccode>(\"aab\", \"bba\")\u003c/code>, false for \u003ccode>(\"aabbc\", \"abccc\")\u003c/code> and false for \u003ccode>(\"ab\", \"aa\")\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to classify a statement from its constraints, answer shape and input structure, and check the counter-cue before you commit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-do-you-tell-look-alike-techniques-apart":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Most wrong-technique mistakes come from six pairs that share their surface: sliding window or prefix sums, BFS or Dijkstra, greedy or DP, heap or sort or quickselect, backtracking or DP, union-find or BFS. Each pair has one \u003cb>deciding question\u003c/b>, such as \"does the condition stay true when I shrink the range?\". When you are still unsure, write the simpler idea and let a random tester compare it with a brute force: a two-minute \u003cb>counterexample hunt\u003c/b> beats twenty minutes of arguing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Identical twins share a face; you tell them apart by one feature, a scar or a voice. You do not compare everything, you check the one thing that differs. Each pair below has such a feature, and it is always a property of the problem, never a word in the statement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Six pairs and their deciding questions\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pair\u003c/th>\u003cth>What they share\u003c/th>\u003cth>Deciding question\u003c/th>\u003cth>Yes\u003c/th>\u003cth>No\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding window or prefix sums\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Contiguous subarrays, sums\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does shrinking a valid range keep it valid (and growing an invalid one keep it invalid)?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Window: O(n), O(1) space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums plus a hash map: exact targets, negative values, counting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>BFS or Dijkstra\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewest or cheapest moves\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does every move cost the same?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS, O(V + E)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dijkstra if costs &gt;= 0; 0-1 BFS for costs 0 and 1; Bellman-Ford for negatives\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Greedy or DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Optimise over a sequence of choices\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Can you prove one choice is always safe, and does a brute force fail to break it?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greedy, usually sort plus one pass\u003c/td>\u003ctd>DP over the choices\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Heap, sort or quickselect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Smallest, largest, top k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Does data arrive or change between questions?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Heap: O(log n) per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixed data: sort for full order, heapq.nsmallest or quickselect for top k\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Backtracking or DP\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Choices, \"ways\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Must every solution be listed, or only counted or optimised?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>List them: backtracking (the output can be exponential)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Count or best, with repeating states: DP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Union-find or BFS\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Connectivity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Are links only added and is the relation symmetric, with no paths or distances needed?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Union-find, near O(1) per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>BFS or DFS: directed reach, distances, paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two problems from this module's review sets show the first pair from both sides: 1004 (longest run of 1s with at most k flips) is a window, because a stretch with too many zeros stays bad as it grows; 523 (a stretch whose sum is a multiple of k) is prefix sums, because \"multiple of k\" is not monotone at all. And 2101 (bombs triggering bombs) looks like union-find but is not: a large bomb can reach a small one that cannot reach back, so the relation is not symmetric.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Settle it with a counterexample hunt\u003c/h5>\u003cp>When the deciding question is hard to answer by thought alone, test it. Write the candidate (fast, maybe wrong), an obviously correct brute force, and a generator of small random inputs; run thousands of cases and stop at the first disagreement. Sort the cases so the smallest failing input is found first: a three-element counterexample explains itself, a fifty-element one does not.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A hunt costs about five minutes to write the brute force, two for the generator, and seconds to run 10,000 small cases. A wrong submission or a wrong interview answer costs far more. The techniques themselves:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time\u003c/th>\u003cth>Extra space\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Window / prefix sums with a map\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) / O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) or O(alphabet) / O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>BFS / Dijkstra with heapq\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V + E) / O((V + E) log V)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(V) / O(V + E)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Top k of n by sort / nsmallest / quickselect\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n) / O(n log k) / O(n) average\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n) / O(k) / O(1) in place\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Ask the deciding question whenever two atlas rows match. Run a counterexample hunt whenever your idea is greedy, or a window over values that might be negative, or any \"this feels right\" step you cannot prove in two sentences. Skip the hunt when a short proof exists: an exchange argument you can state is better evidence than any number of random tests.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The window idea for \"shortest stretch with sum at least k\" on \u003ccode>[1, -1, 1, 3]\u003c/code> with k = 4. The correct answer is 2 (\u003ccode>[1, 3]\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 0: add 1, sum = 1 &lt; 4, nothing to record\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  -1   1   3      window [0, 0], sum 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 1: add -1, sum = 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   1  \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   1   3      window [0, 1], sum 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 2: add 1, sum = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   1  -1  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   3       window [0, 2], sum 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">right = 3: add 3, sum = 4 &gt;= 4: record length 4, then drop nums[0] = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   1  -1   1  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>       window [0, 3], sum 4, best = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">sum is now 3 &lt; 4, so the window stops shrinking and the loop ends\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   .  \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   1   3      window [1, 3], sum 3, best = 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">But dropping the -1 would have raised the sum back to 4 with length 2: the window answered {{4}}, the truth is {{2}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums   .   .  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>    [1, 3] sums to 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The random hunt below finds an even smaller case on its own: \u003ccode>([-3, 3], 1)\u003c/code>, where the window never becomes valid and answers -1 although \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code> alone works.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import math\nimport random\n\n\ndef find_counterexample(candidate, oracle, gen, trials=10_000, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"Compare a fast idea with a slow, obviously correct oracle on random small inputs.\n    Returns the smallest disagreeing input found, or None.\"\"\"\n    rnd = random.Random(seed)\n    cases = sorted((gen(rnd) for _ in range(trials)), key=lambda c: len(repr(c)))\n    for case in cases:\n        if candidate(*case) != oracle(*case):\n            return case\n    return None\n\n\ndef shortest_at_least_window(nums, k):\n    \"\"\"Window idea: valid only when every value is non-negative.\"\"\"\n    best, s, left = math.inf, 0, 0\n    for right, x in enumerate(nums):\n        s += x\n        while s &gt;= k:\n            best = min(best, right - left + 1)\n            s -= nums[left]\n            left += 1\n    return best if best &lt; math.inf else -1\n\n\ndef shortest_at_least_brute(nums, k):\n    best = math.inf\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        s = 0\n        for j in range(i, len(nums)):\n            s += nums[j]\n            if s &gt;= k:\n                best = min(best, j - i + 1)\n                break\n    return best if best &lt; math.inf else -1\n\n\ndef gen_signed(rnd):\n    return ([rnd.randint(-3, 4) for _ in range(rnd.randint(1, 6))], rnd.randint(1, 6))\n\n\nprint(find_counterexample(shortest_at_least_window, shortest_at_least_brute, gen_signed))\n# ([-3, 3], 1); with values drawn from 0..4 instead it prints None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same harness compares a greedy coin-change function with a DP one: on random coin systems that include 1 it reports \u003ccode>((1, 4, 5), 8)\u003c/code>, where greedy pays 5 + 1 + 1 + 1 (four coins) and DP pays 4 + 4 (two).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trusting the examples.\u003c/b> Statement examples are chosen to explain, not to break; they rarely contain the negative value or the tie that kills a wrong idea.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Union-find on a directed relation.\u003c/b> It merges both ways, so it answers \"same group\" for pairs that only reach one way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>BFS with almost equal weights.\u003c/b> Costs of 1 and 2 are not equal costs; use Dijkstra, or split each cost-2 edge into two cost-1 edges.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A hunt with a buggy oracle.\u003c/b> Check the brute force on the statement's examples first, or the hunt will \"find\" correct code wrong.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">\"Longest stretch whose sum is at most k, all values non-negative\": window or prefix sums? Give the reason in one sentence.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A window: with non-negative values, removing an element from a valid stretch keeps the sum at most k, and adding to an invalid stretch keeps it above k, so validity is monotone and each end only moves forward.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Friendships are added one at a time. After each addition you must answer either (a) \"are these two people connected?\" or (b) \"how many links separate them?\". Which structure for each?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(a) union-find: links are only added, the relation is symmetric and only membership is asked, at near O(1) per operation. (b) BFS: union-find stores no paths or distances, so you need a traversal of the graph (from one endpoint, O(V + E) per question).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Someone finds the median of a fixed list by pushing every element onto a heap and popping n/2 times. Is it correct, what does it cost, and what would you use instead?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is correct and costs O(n log n), no better than sorting. For one median of fixed data, sort once (\u003ccode>sorted(a)[len(a) // 2]\u003c/code>) or use quickselect for O(n) on average; the two-heap method earns its keep only when values arrive over time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1004 Max Consecutive Ones III.\u003c/b> Before coding, answer the window-or-prefix-sums question in writing. Expected: 4 for \u003ccode>nums = [1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0], k = 1\u003c/code>, and 0 for \u003ccode>[0, 0, 0], k = 0\u003c/code>; target O(n), O(1) space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>523 Continuous Subarray Sum.\u003c/b> Same question first. Expected: true for \u003ccode>[2, 4, 1], k = 7\u003c/code>, false for \u003ccode>[3, 1], k = 5\u003c/code>, true for \u003ccode>[5, 0, 0], k = 3\u003c/code> (the two zeros form a valid stretch).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>646 Maximum Length of Pair Chain.\u003c/b> Write a greedy version and an O(n^2) DP version, then run \u003ccode>find_counterexample\u003c/code> between them. Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[[1, 3], [2, 4], [5, 6], [4, 5]]\u003c/code>, 3 for \u003ccode>[[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\u003c/code>, and the hunt returns None.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know the deciding question for six look-alike pairs and how to settle a doubtful idea with a counterexample hunt.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-does-the-same-problem-change-at-n-20-10-3-10":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The constraints are part of the problem: they tell you which algorithm the setter wants. The same story needs brute force at n = 20, a quadratic or simple DP at 10^3, an O(n log n) or O(n) idea at 10^5, and at 10^9 something that never touches every item, such as a formula, a binary search or fast exponentiation. Read n (and the value range, and the number of queries) before you design anything.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Delivering 5 parcels, you can try every route. Delivering 5,000, you need a rule such as \"nearest next\". Delivering to every house in a country, you no longer plan routes at all; you plan depots and use averages. The task did not change, its size did, and each size has its own tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>One story at four sizes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A robot climbs n steps, moving 1, 2 or 3 steps at a time. How many different routes reach the top, modulo 1,000,000,007? Let f(n) be the answer: the last move was 1, 2 or 3 steps, so f(n) = f(n - 1) + f(n - 2) + f(n - 3), with f(0) = 1 and f(negative) = 0.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>n\u003c/th>\u003cth>Budget allows\u003c/th>\u003cth>What works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why the previous version stops working\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>20\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exponential\u003c/td>\u003ctd>List every route explicitly: 121,415 routes, about 0.2 s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(nothing yet)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n^2)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A loop over the recurrence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Listing is hopeless (about 10^264 routes), and memoised recursion is 1,000 calls deep: Python's default recursion limit is 1,000, so it raises RecursionError\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^5\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The same loop with three rolling variables, taking \u003ccode>% MOD\u003c/code> every step\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Without the modulus the exact count has 26,465 digits and every addition gets slower\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10^9\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raise the 3 x 3 step matrix to the n-th power by repeated squaring: about 30 squarings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The O(n) loop managed 10^7 steps in 2.7 s, so 10^9 would take about 4.5 minutes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Other stories, the same lesson\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Story\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 20\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^3\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^5\u003c/th>\u003cth>n = 10^9\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Count pairs i &lt; j with a[i] + a[j] &lt;= T\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All pairs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All pairs (5·10^5)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sort, then two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The values are 1..n: count by arithmetic, O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Can a subset reach the sum T?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Try all 2^20 subsets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subset-sum DP if T &lt;= 10^4 (O(nT))\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only with special structure; in general it is NP-hard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Values 1..n: every T from 0 to n(n + 1)/2 is reachable\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The second table carries a warning: when a problem that is hard in general appears with a large n, the constraints are hiding structure (small values, a special sequence) that you are meant to find.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting the work for the climbing story: listing routes costs one step per route, about 1.84^n; the loop costs n iterations of three additions; the matrix method costs one or two 3 x 3 multiplications (27 multiply-adds each) per bit of n, so about 2 · 30 · 27 = 1,620 operations at n = 10^9. Same answer, from minutes down to under a millisecond.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Always, as the first two minutes of a problem: n, the value range, the number of queries q, and the time limit. Check the product of the sizes, not each one alone: n = 10^5 with q = 10^5 queries rules out O(n) per query. When the constraints are tiny, enjoy it: a clean brute force is the intended solution, not a lack of ambition.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>routes_matrix(10)\u003c/code> processes the bits of 10 = 0b1010 from the lowest, keeping \u003ccode>result\u003c/code> (the product so far) and \u003ccode>step\u003c/code> (the matrix squared once per bit).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start: result = M^0 (the identity), step = M^1; bits of 10 from the right: 0, 1, 0, 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 1010 (binary)   result = M^0   step = M^1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 0 is {{0}}: leave result, square step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 101             result = M^0   step = \u003cmark>M^2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 1 is {{1}}: result = result * step, then square step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 10              result = \u003cmark>M^2\u003c/mark>   step = \u003cmark>M^4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 2 is {{0}}: square step only\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 1               result = M^2   step = \u003cmark>M^8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Bit 3 is {{1}}: result = M^2 * M^8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>n = 0               result = \u003cmark>M^10\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">f(10) is the top-left entry of M^10: {{274}}, matching 1, 1, 2, 4, 7, 13, 24, 44, 81, 149, 274\u003c/p>\u003cpre>f(0..10) = 1 1 2 4 7 13 24 44 81 149 \u003cmark>274\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>MOD = 1_000_000_007\n\n\ndef routes_loop(n):\n    \"\"\"n up to about 10^6: bottom-up with three rolling variables. O(n) time, O(1) space.\"\"\"\n    a, b, c = 0, 0, 1                     # f(i - 3), f(i - 2), f(i - 1), starting from f(0) = 1\n    for _ in range(n):\n        a, b, c = b, c, (a + b + c) % MOD\n    return c\n\n\ndef mat_mult(X, Y):\n    return [[sum(X[i][t] * Y[t][j] for t in range(3)) % MOD for j in range(3)] for i in range(3)]\n\n\ndef routes_matrix(n):\n    \"\"\"n up to 10^18: (f(i), f(i-1), f(i-2)) = M (f(i-1), f(i-2), f(i-3)), so apply M^n to\n    (f(0), f(-1), f(-2)) = (1, 0, 0). O(log n) matrix products.\"\"\"\n    result = [[1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0], [0, 0, 1]]\n    step = [[1, 1, 1], [1, 0, 0], [0, 1, 0]]\n    while n:\n        if n &amp; 1:\n            result = mat_mult(result, step)\n        step = mat_mult(step, step)\n        n &gt;&gt;= 1\n    return result[0][0]\n\n\nassert routes_loop(10) == routes_matrix(10) == 274\nassert routes_loop(10**5) == routes_matrix(10**5)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>The recursion limit.\u003c/b> Memoised recursion is an O(n) algorithm that still crashes at n = 1,000 in Python; go bottom-up, or raise the limit only with care.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the modulus.\u003c/b> Python's integers never overflow, so nothing warns you; the numbers just grow until every operation is slow. In C++ or Java they overflow silently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reading \"values up to 10^9\" as \"n up to 10^9\".\u003c/b> A large value range forbids arrays indexed by value (use a dict or coordinate compression) but says nothing about n.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ignoring the number of test cases.\u003c/b> \"Up to 10^4 test cases, sum of n up to 2·10^5\" is a different budget from \"10^4 test cases of n = 2·10^5\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">\u003ccode>routes_loop(10**7)\u003c/code> took 2.7 s. Roughly how long would \u003ccode>routes_loop(10**8)\u003c/code> take, and what does that tell you about n = 10^8 in Python?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>About 27 s, ten times as long, because the loop is linear. Even an O(n) loop of simple Python operations is too slow around 10^8; you need either C-level built-ins (or NumPy) or an algorithm below O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the memoised version fail at n = 1,000 but succeed at n = 600?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each pending call f(i) waits on f(i - 1), so the call stack grows to about n frames before anything returns. Python's default recursion limit is 1,000 frames, so n = 600 fits and n = 1,000 raises RecursionError, although the algorithm itself is fine.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A problem has n &lt;= 300 and asks for something you can do in O(n^3) or, with a clever trick, in O(n). Which do you write first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The O(n^3) version if it is simpler: 2.7·10^7 simple operations is a few seconds in Python, so check whether the inner loop can be cut to O(n^2) (9·10^4 operations, instant). With n this small the setter expects a cubic or quadratic idea; the O(n) trick is a bonus, not the target.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write down the target complexity from each problem's constraints before choosing an idea.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1442 Count Triplets That Can Form Two Arrays of Equal XOR\u003c/b> (n &lt;= 300). Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[2, 2, 2]\u003c/code> and 2 for \u003ccode>[1, 3, 2]\u003c/code>; an O(n^2) solution is comfortably within the budget.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1823 Find the Winner of the Circular Game\u003c/b> (n &lt;= 500). Solve it once by simulation and once in O(n). Expected: 5 for \u003ccode>n = 6, k = 2\u003c/code>, and 4 for \u003ccode>n = 4, k = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2101 Detonate the Maximum Bombs\u003c/b> (n &lt;= 100). Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[[0, 0, 2], [3, 0, 1], [1, 0, 1]]\u003c/code>; the constraint permits O(n^3).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to read the constraints first and match the algorithm to the size, including when n is too large to touch every item.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/when-should-you-re-solve-a-problem-and-how-does-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Solving a problem once proves you could do it that day. Re-solving it \u003cb>from a blank file\u003c/b> after a gap, then after longer gaps (a few days, a week, three weeks, two months), makes it something you can do months later under pressure. Grade every attempt honestly: \u003cb>Solved\u003c/b> only if you wrote the brute force and the optimal solution yourself; \u003cb>Brute only\u003c/b> if you reached a correct but slow solution or needed a nudge; \u003cb>Stuck\u003c/b> if you needed the key insight or the solution. Brute only and Stuck come back in Today's session three days later; the longer rungs are yours to schedule.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Memory fades fastest just after learning. Pulling an idea back out when it has started to fade (but is not gone) strengthens it far more than rereading it while it is fresh: this is the testing effect combined with spacing. One successful re-solve after two months is worth more than three re-solves in the same week, and a failed re-solve tells you something a fresh solve cannot: which part you only understood while it was in front of you.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The ladder\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Rung\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gap after the last attempt\u003c/th>\u003cth>Who schedules it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 days, after Brute only or Stuck\u003c/td>\u003ctd>This page: Today's session\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 week, after a clean solve\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You: your journal\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 weeks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>3\u003c/td>\u003ctd>2 months\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Off\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(none)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The problem retires to the bank pool\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The rule is one line: a clean solve climbs one rung; anything else drops back to rung 0. The gaps expand because, as Cepeda's data shows, the best gap is a sizeable fraction of how long you want to remember, so each successful retrieval earns a longer one.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The blank-file rule\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A re-solve starts from an empty file with the statement only: no old code, no notes. Keep the page's timebox (Easy 20, Medium 30, Hard 45 minutes). Write the brute force first, then the optimal version, then test both against each other on small random inputs. Retyping code you remember is not a re-solve; it tests your typing, not your reasoning.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Grading honestly\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>What happened\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mark\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Brute force and optimal written alone, within about 1.5x the timebox; you can state the cost and why it is correct\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Solved\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Only a correct slow solution, or you needed \"What to notice\" or \"A nudge\", or you went far over the timebox\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Brute only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>You needed \"Key insight\" or read a solution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stuck\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>How Today's session runs rung 0\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>Marking \u003cb>Brute only\u003c/b> or \u003cb>Stuck\u003c/b> records the time; three days later the item becomes due.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The review slot holds one item per session: the oldest due item; if nothing is due, the next problem of the earliest open review set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>After the re-solve, mark the new result. \u003cb>Solved\u003c/b> takes it out of the queue. If the result is the same status as before, clear it and set it again so the date updates (clicking a selected status clears it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>The \u003cb>Review\u003c/b> view lists everything currently marked Brute only or Stuck, for a longer catch-up session.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The page does not schedule rungs 1 to 3; the template computes those dates for your journal.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Derive the weekly load before you commit to it. If you add N new problems a week and each climbs r rungs, then once the pipeline is full you face about N x r re-solves a week. With N = 5 and all four rungs that is 20 re-solves a week; at 15 to 20 minutes each (re-solves are faster than first solves), five to seven hours, which is too much. So be selective: give every Brute only or Stuck problem the full ladder, give clean first solves only rungs 1 and 3, and cap reviews at about a third of your practice time. That brings N = 5 down to roughly 8 to 12 re-solves a week.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use the full ladder for core problems of each module, for anything you marked Brute only or Stuck, and for interview staples. Skip it for bank problems that taught you nothing new, and for problems you can only \"solve\" by remembering code: rewrite their cards instead, then re-solve. If the queue grows beyond one or two re-solves per session, stop adding new problems for a few days; review debt only grows.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One problem's history through the ladder (dates from the template below).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">2 Nov: first attempt, needed the key insight: {{Stuck}}. Rung 0: due in 3 days\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: Stuck                         next: \u003cmark>5 Nov\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">5 Nov (Today's session offers it): reached only the slow version: {{Brute only}}. Back to rung 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: Stuck, Brute                  next: \u003cmark>8 Nov\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">8 Nov: clean solve from a blank file: {{Solved}}. Rung 1: one week\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: Stuck, Brute, Solved          next: \u003cmark>15 Nov\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">15 Nov: clean again. Rung 2: three weeks\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: ..., Solved, Solved           next: \u003cmark>6 Dec\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">6 Dec: clean again. Rung 3: two months\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: ..., Solved x3                next: \u003cmark>4 Feb\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">4 Feb: clean after two months: off the ladder\u003c/p>\u003cpre>history: ..., Solved x4                next: \u003cmark>none\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from datetime import date, timedelta\n\nLADDER = [3, 7, 21, 60]          # days until the next re-solve, by rung\n\n\ndef next_resolve(history):\n    \"\"\"history: [(date, result)] oldest first, result in {\"solved\", \"brute\", \"stuck\"}.\n    A clean solve climbs one rung; anything else drops to rung 0 (a re-solve in 3 days).\n    Returns the next re-solve date, or None once the problem has climbed off the ladder.\"\"\"\n    rung = 0\n    for _, result in history:\n        rung = rung + 1 if result == \"solved\" else 0\n    if rung &gt;= len(LADDER):\n        return None\n    return history[-1][0] + timedelta(days=LADDER[rung])\n\n\nh = [(date(2026, 11, 2), \"stuck\"), (date(2026, 11, 5), \"brute\"), (date(2026, 11, 8), \"solved\")]\nprint(next_resolve(h))                                  # 2026-11-15\nprint(next_resolve([(date(2026, 11, 2), \"solved\")]))    # 2026-11-09: a clean first solve starts at rung 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Retyping instead of re-deriving.\u003c/b> If you remember the code, write the brute force first anyway, and say the invariant aloud before the optimal version.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-solving the same day.\u003c/b> It feels productive and measures short-term memory only.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Generous grading.\u003c/b> A Solved mark removes the problem from review; if you hesitated at the key step, it was not Solved.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Letting the queue grow.\u003c/b> One or two re-solves per session is sustainable; ten is a backlog you will skip.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-solving favourites.\u003c/b> The problems you dislike are usually the ones that need it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A history reads \u003ccode>[(1 Mar, solved), (8 Mar, stuck)]\u003c/code>. When is the next re-solve, and why not later?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>11 Mar. The Stuck result drops the problem back to rung 0, three days after the failed attempt; the earlier clean solve does not count any more, because the failure shows the idea had not stuck.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do the gaps grow (3, 7, 21, 60 days) instead of staying at one week?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each successful retrieval makes the memory more durable, and the best gap is a fraction of how long you want to remember (Cepeda and colleagues: roughly 20-40% of a one-week horizon, 5-10% of a one-year horizon). Fixed short gaps spend time re-proving what you already hold; growing gaps test you near the point of forgetting, which is where retrieval helps most.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You re-solve 946 cleanly but it takes 50 minutes against a 30-minute timebox. What do you mark, and what happens next?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Brute only: past about 1.5x the timebox the solution is not yet fluent, even if it is correct. Today's session brings it back three days later; once a re-solve is clean and on time, mark Solved and put the one-week date in your journal.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve each problem today, record the attempt as \u003ccode>(date, result)\u003c/code>, and compute its next date with \u003ccode>next_resolve\u003c/code>. Expected: three dates in your journal (three days out for Brute only or Stuck, one week out for a clean solve).\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>946 Validate Stack Sequences.\u003c/b> Expected: false for \u003ccode>pushed = [1, 2, 3], popped = [3, 1, 2]\u003c/code> and true for \u003ccode>popped = [2, 3, 1]\u003c/code>; O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>240 Search a 2D Matrix II.\u003c/b> Expected: true for target 6 and false for target 10 in \u003ccode>[[1, 4, 7], [2, 5, 8], [3, 6, 9]]\u003c/code>; O(m + n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>328 Odd Even Linked List.\u003c/b> Expected: 1 3 5 2 4 6 from the list 1 to 6, and a single node 7 unchanged; O(1) extra space.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to run a spaced re-solve ladder from a blank file, grade attempts honestly, and combine it with Today's session.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-do-you-keep-an-error-log-that-actually-reduc":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A problem journal records what you learnt; an \u003cb>error log\u003c/b> records how you fail. One line per mistake: date, problem, \u003cb>category\u003c/b>, what happened. Categories (misread, wrong technique, broken invariant, off-by-one, complexity, missed edge case, Python cost trap) let you review by kind of mistake, so one drill fixes a whole class of future bugs instead of one problem.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Aviation checklists and blameless post-mortems exist because people repeat the same few failure modes. A post-mortem does not ask \"who was careless?\" but \"which check would have caught this?\". Your log asks the same question of your code, and after a month the answer is usually one or two categories that account for most of your losses.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The categories\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Category\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical symptom\u003c/th>\u003cth>Drill that targets it\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>misread\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Solved a different problem; missed a constraint\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Restate the problem in one sentence and list the constraints before coding\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>technique\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Right code for the wrong idea\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two classification sprints; add the missed cue to your atlas\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>invariant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pointers or windows drift; wrong on some inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Write the invariant as a comment first, then assert it while testing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>off-by-one\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fails at the boundaries; empty or size-one inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Half-open ranges; test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>complexity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Correct but times out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compute the target from n before designing; time a worst-case input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>edge-case\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fails on duplicates, negatives, zeros, extremes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run the edge-case catalogue before submitting\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>python-cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hidden O(n) inside a loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Check the cost sheet: \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>x in list\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>s +=\u003c/code> in loops, slicing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>The line and the weekly review\u003c/h5>\u003cp>One line, written right after the attempt: \u003ccode>2026-11-04 | 930 | off-by-one | did not seed the counts with prefix 0\u003c/code>. Once a week, count by category, take the largest, and apply its drill to every problem for the next week. At the next review, check whether that category's count fell. Keep the categories few: seven is enough to separate the causes without agonising over the label.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>About 30 seconds per entry and ten minutes per weekly review. Over a month that is under an hour, and it typically removes the single most expensive habit you have (often off-by-one or skipping the constraints), which costs far more than an hour.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Log every Brute only or Stuck result and every wrong submission, including the ones you fixed in two minutes: the cheap mistakes recur most. Do not log typos that the interpreter flagged instantly unless the same typo keeps coming back. When a mistake fits two categories, choose the earliest step of the solving loop that went wrong (a misread that led to the wrong technique is a misread).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turning a failed attempt into one log line.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">1283 fails a hidden test: the answer is too small by one\u003c/p>\u003cpre>symptom: \u003cmark>wrong answer\u003c/mark>, off by one\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reproduce with the smallest input that fails, found with a brute force\u003c/p>\u003cpre>nums = [4, 4], threshold = 2   expected 4, got \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Find the line: the check summed x // d, which rounds down; the statement rounds each quotient up\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum(x // d)  \u003cmark>should be sum((x + d - 1) // d)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Which check would have caught it? Restating the statement in my own words: \"each quotient rounded up\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>category: \u003cmark>misread\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write the line\u003c/p>\u003cpre>2026-11-06 | 1283 | misread | rounded quotients down, not up\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At the weekly review off-by-one has three entries, the most: next week's drill is {{test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose}}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>off-by-one 3   technique 2   edge-case 1   ...\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\nDRILLS = {\n    \"misread\": \"Restate the problem in one sentence and list the constraints before coding.\",\n    \"technique\": \"Do two classification sprints; add the missed cue to your atlas.\",\n    \"invariant\": \"Write the invariant as a comment first, then assert it while testing.\",\n    \"off-by-one\": \"Use half-open ranges; test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose.\",\n    \"complexity\": \"Compute the target from n before designing; time a worst-case input.\",\n    \"edge-case\": \"Run the edge-case catalogue: empty, one, duplicates, negatives, extremes.\",\n    \"python-cost\": \"Check the cost sheet: list.pop(0), x in list, s += in loops, slicing.\",\n}\n\n\ndef review(log_lines, top=3):\n    \"\"\"log_lines: 'date | problem | category | what happened'. Returns the commonest categories,\n    each with its count, its drill and the problems that showed it.\"\"\"\n    by_cat = defaultdict(list)\n    for line in log_lines:\n        day, problem, category, note = [part.strip() for part in line.split(\"|\", 3)]\n        by_cat[category].append(problem)\n    ranked = sorted(by_cat.items(), key=lambda kv: -len(kv[1]))[:top]\n    return [(cat, len(probs), DRILLS.get(cat, \"new category: define a drill\"), probs)\n            for cat, probs in ranked]\n\n\nLOG = \"\"\"2026-11-02 | 1004 | technique | tried prefix sums; the window was enough\n2026-11-03 | 523 | edge-case | forgot that the stretch needs length &gt;= 2\n2026-11-04 | 930 | off-by-one | did not seed the counts with prefix 0\n2026-11-06 | 1283 | misread | rounded the quotients down; the statement rounds up\n2026-11-07 | 2300 | complexity | nested loop, 10^10 pairs\n2026-11-09 | 1944 | technique | reached for a heap; a stack was the idea\n2026-11-10 | 1209 | python-cost | rebuilt the string with += inside the loop\n2026-11-12 | 1248 | off-by-one | atMost(k - 1) computed with the wrong window bound\n2026-11-13 | 2125 | off-by-one | skipped the last non-empty row\"\"\".splitlines()\nprint(review(LOG)[0])\n# ('off-by-one', 3, 'Use half-open ranges; test sizes 0, 1, 2 on purpose.', ['930', '1248', '2125'])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vague categories.\u003c/b> \"Bug\" or \"silly mistake\" cannot be drilled. Name the check that would have caught it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Logging only disasters.\u003c/b> The two-minute fixes are the most frequent and the cheapest to eliminate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Never reviewing.\u003c/b> A log nobody reads is a diary. Put the weekly review in your calendar.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Too many categories.\u003c/b> Twenty categories split the counts so thin that nothing stands out.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Blame instead of process.\u003c/b> \"I was careless\" changes nothing; \"I skip the size-one test\" leads to a drill.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your solution passed every example but timed out on n = 10^5 because you used \u003ccode>nums.pop(0)\u003c/code> inside a loop. Which category, and why not \"complexity\"?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>python-cost. The algorithm was linear on paper; the hidden O(n) cost of \u003ccode>list.pop(0)\u003c/code> (every element shifts left) made it quadratic. The drill is the Python cost sheet (use \u003ccode>collections.deque.popleft\u003c/code>), not a new algorithm.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why review by category rather than re-reading each problem's notes?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Mistakes cluster by kind, not by problem. Ten off-by-one errors in ten different problems look unrelated problem by problem, but by category they become one habit with one drill (half-open ranges, size 0-2 tests) that prevents the next ten as well.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A log line reads \u003ccode>1004 | bug | forgot something\u003c/code>. What is wrong with it, and how would you rewrite it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Neither the category nor the note says which check failed, so it cannot be counted or drilled. Rewrite it with a real category and a precise note, for example \u003ccode>2026-11-02 | 1004 | invariant | moved left before decrementing the zero count\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve the three problems and log every slip, including ones your own tests caught. Then run \u003ccode>review\u003c/code> on your lines. Expected: either at least one category with two or more entries, or an empty log you can honestly defend.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1283 Find the Smallest Divisor Given a Threshold.\u003c/b> Expected: 6 for \u003ccode>nums = [2, 7, 11], threshold = 5\u003c/code>, and 4 for \u003ccode>[4, 4], threshold = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1248 Count Number of Nice Subarrays.\u003c/b> Expected: 4 for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 1, 2, 1], k = 2\u003c/code>, and 0 for \u003ccode>[2, 4], k = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2300 Successful Pairs of Spells and Potions.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[1, 3]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>spells = [2, 5], potions = [1, 2, 3, 4], success = 8\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to log mistakes by category and turn the most frequent one into a drill that prevents a whole class of bugs.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/what-belongs-on-a-dsa-flashcard-and-what-does-no":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A good card asks for \u003cb>one fact you need to rebuild a solution\u003c/b>: the cue that points to a technique, the invariant that makes it correct, the recurrence, the cost with its reason, or the bug to avoid. A card holding a whole solution trains you to recognise code you have seen, not to produce it. Write four or five cards per solved problem from your journal, review them for a few minutes a day, and let a scheduler space them out.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A card is an interview question you can answer in one breath: \"why is the monotonic stack linear?\", \"what must the prefix-count map start with?\". Problems test whether you can build a solution; cards keep the parts sharp between problems, the way scales keep a pianist's fingers ready for pieces.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Five kinds of card\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>Front\u003c/th>\u003cth>Back\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cue to technique\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Count subarrays with an exact sum, values may be negative: which idea?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix sums with a count map\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Invariant\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Monotonic stack for next greater: what is true of the stack?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Values strictly decrease from bottom to top, and every element on it is still waiting for its answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recurrence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fewest coins for amount a: recurrence and base case?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>best[a] = 1 + min(best[a - c]) over coins c &lt;= a; best[0] = 0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost with reason\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Why is the monotonic stack O(n) overall?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each index is pushed once and popped at most once\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pitfall\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prefix-count map: what must it contain before the loop?\u003c/td>\u003ctd>{0: 1}, so stretches that start at index 0 are counted\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>What makes a card bad\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Whole solutions.\u003c/b> \"Solve 1209\" with twenty lines of code on the back: you will recognise the code and still fail to write it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Vague fronts.\u003c/b> \"What is dynamic programming?\" has no checkable answer, so you cannot grade yourself honestly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two facts at once.\u003c/b> \"Cost and invariant of a monotonic stack\" gets graded as right when you knew one of them. Split it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Facts you never understood.\u003c/b> A card cannot create understanding; work the problem first, then write the card.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Scheduling\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Use a spaced-repetition app such as Anki (its scheduler is based on SuperMemo's SM-2 algorithm, or the newer FSRS), or a Leitner box: cards move up a box each time you answer correctly and back to the first box when you miss, and higher boxes are reviewed less often. Anki imports plain text files with one card per line and fields separated by tabs, which the template below produces.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Say you solve five problems a week and write four cards each: 20 new cards a week. With gaps that roughly double after each correct answer, a card costs about six to eight reviews in its first year, so each week's batch costs about 140 reviews spread over the year. In steady state that is about 20 reviews a day; at roughly ten seconds each, three to four minutes a day.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write cards right after the journal entry for a solved problem, and from each module's \"Spot it\" cues. Review them as a warm-up at the start of a session. Do not let cards replace problems: cards keep the parts ready, but only re-solving from a blank file proves you can assemble them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Turning one journal entry (for 930, counting 0/1 subarrays with a given sum) into cards with the template.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The journal entry: problem, cue, technique, invariant, cost, pitfall\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cue: count subarrays with an exact sum, zeros allowed\ntechnique: prefix sums with a count map\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 1, cue to technique: the front is the {{cue}}, the back names the idea\u003c/p>\u003cpre>count subarrays with an exact sum, zeros allowed: which idea?\n-&gt; \u003cmark>prefix sums with a count map\u003c/mark> (930 Binary Subarrays With Sum)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 2, invariant: one sentence that must hold at every step\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix sums with a count map: what stays true at every step?\n-&gt; seen[s] = number of \u003cmark>earlier\u003c/mark> prefixes equal to s\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 3, cost with its reason\u003c/p>\u003cpre>-&gt; \u003cmark>O(n)\u003c/mark>: one pass, O(1) expected per dict operation\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Card 4, the pitfall\u003c/p>\u003cpre>930: the bug to avoid?  -&gt; \u003cmark>seed the map\u003c/mark>: seen = {0: 1}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Export: one line per card, a tab between front and back, ready for Anki's text import\u003c/p>\u003cpre>front &lt;TAB&gt; back\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def cards_from_entry(entry):\n    \"\"\"entry: dict with problem, cue, technique, invariant, cost, pitfall.\n    Returns (front, back) pairs, one fact each. Never the whole solution.\"\"\"\n    p = entry[\"problem\"]\n    return [\n        (f\"{entry['cue']}: which idea?\", f\"{entry['technique']} ({p})\"),\n        (f\"{entry['technique']}: what stays true at every step?\", entry[\"invariant\"]),\n        (f\"{entry['technique']} on {p}: target cost and why?\", entry[\"cost\"]),\n        (f\"{p}: the bug to avoid?\", entry[\"pitfall\"]),\n    ]\n\n\ndef to_tsv(cards):\n    \"\"\"Tab-separated front/back lines, which Anki's plain-text importer reads.\"\"\"\n    clean = lambda s: s.replace(\"\\t\", \" \").replace(\"\\n\", \" \")\n    return \"\\n\".join(f\"{clean(f)}\\t{clean(b)}\" for f, b in cards)\n\n\nentry = dict(problem=\"930 Binary Subarrays With Sum\",\n             cue=\"count subarrays with an exact sum, zeros allowed\",\n             technique=\"prefix sums with a count map\",\n             invariant=\"seen[s] = number of earlier prefixes equal to s, before adding the current prefix\",\n             cost=\"O(n): one pass, O(1) expected per dict operation\",\n             pitfall=\"start with seen = {0: 1} so subarrays that begin at index 0 count\")\nopen(\"cards.tsv\", \"w\").write(to_tsv(cards_from_entry(entry)) + \"\\n\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cards that are too big.\u003c/b> If the answer takes more than one breath, split the card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ambiguous fronts.\u003c/b> If two different answers are both right, add context to the front (\"for non-negative values\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reviewing instead of solving.\u003c/b> Cards support practice; they do not replace re-solves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keeping bad cards.\u003c/b> A card you keep failing for the same reason is badly written: rewrite or delete it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Rewrite the card \"Explain the sliding window technique\" as two good cards.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For example: \"Longest stretch with at most k distinct letters: which idea, and what makes it valid?\" with the back \"Variable window; validity is monotone: shrinking a valid window keeps it valid\". And: \"Variable window: why is it O(n) although it has a loop inside a loop?\" with the back \"Each index enters once (right end) and leaves at most once (left end)\". Each asks for one checkable fact.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is putting the solution code for 1209 on a card a poor investment?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reviewing it trains recognition: you see the code and think \"yes, that one\", which feels like knowing it. Producing a solution needs the cue, the idea (a stack of runs) and the invariant, each retrieved on its own; cards for those transfer to other problems, a code card transfers to nothing.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In \u003ccode>to_tsv\u003c/code>, why are tabs inside a card replaced by spaces?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The importer splits each line on tabs: a tab inside the front or back would create an extra field and shift the text into the wrong place. Newlines are replaced for the same reason, since each line is one card.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve each problem, then write four cards for it with the template. Expected: no card longer than two short lines, none containing more than one line of code, and at least one cue card and one invariant card per problem.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>1209 Remove All Adjacent Duplicates in String II.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>\"z\"\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>s = \"xyyyxxz\", k = 3\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>\"\"\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>\"aabbaa\", k = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1019 Next Greater Node In Linked List.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[4, 4, 5, 5, 0]\u003c/code> for the list 3, 1, 4, 2, 5.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>16 3Sum Closest.\u003c/b> Expected: 2 for \u003ccode>[-2, 0, 1, 3]\u003c/code> with target 2, and 3 for \u003ccode>[1, 1, 1, 0]\u003c/code> with target 100.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to write small single-fact cards and schedule them, so the parts of every solution stay ready between problems.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:mixed/how-do-you-find-your-weak-spots-from-your-tracke":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Your statuses are data. For each module, the share of tried problems you marked Brute only or Stuck shows where your understanding is thin, and the error log shows why. Pick the worst one or two modules, re-read their cards, redo their Stuck problems from a blank file, and add a few fresh bank problems from them to your practice. Measure again in two weeks.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You do not optimise a program by speeding up every line; you profile it and fix the hot spot. Practice time works the same way. Spreading effort evenly over thirty modules feels fair, but most of the gain is in the two or three where you keep getting stuck.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>What the page already shows\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>Module headers and the rail show solved counts per module; the stage map shows lessons and problems per stage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The status filter shows only problems with a chosen status, and the \u003cb>Review\u003c/b> view lists everything marked Brute only or Stuck.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The progress menu has \u003cb>Export all progress as a JSON file\u003c/b>. Problem statuses sit under \u003ccode>trackers[\"dsa-status\"]\u003c/code>, each \u003ccode>{\"status\": s, \"updatedAt\": ms}\u003c/code> with 1 Solved, 2 Brute only, 3 Stuck.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Three numbers per module\u003c/h5>\u003cp>For each module count the problems you have \u003cb>tried\u003c/b> (any status), and from them the \u003cb>help rate\u003c/b> (Brute only plus Stuck, divided by tried) and the \u003cb>stuck rate\u003c/b> (Stuck divided by tried).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Signal\u003c/th>\u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\u003cth>Next two weeks\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>High stuck rate in one module\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The core idea did not land\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Re-read its cards, redo its Stuck problems from a blank file, then three bank problems\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>High help rate in mixed sets, low in module practice\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recognition, not mechanics\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Classification sprints, the atlas, look-alike pairs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mostly Brute only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You find a solution but not the optimal one\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Target complexity first; the optimisation ladder in the Deriving solutions module\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>One error-log category dominates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A habit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That category's drill on every problem\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The export and the analysis take about ten minutes, every two weeks. The script runs in time linear in the number of tracked items, instant even for the full bank of about 900 problems.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every two weeks, after finishing a stage, and before choosing what to revise for an interview. Do not do it after every problem: with three or four attempts per module, one bad evening looks like a weakness. Require at least three tried problems before you trust a module's rate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>weak_spots\u003c/code> on a small export: seven tried problems in two modules.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Load: statuses from the export, and problem -&gt; module from the page's data\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window: 3 1 , 76 2 , 424 1 , 1004 1     prefix: 560 1 , 930 2 , 523 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Tally the window module: four tried, three Solved, one Brute only\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window  tried \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  solved 3  brute 1  stuck 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Tally the prefix module: three tried, one of each status\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix  tried \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>  solved 1  brute 1  stuck 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Help rates: (brute + stuck) / tried\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window  1/4 = \u003cmark>0.25\u003c/mark>      prefix  2/3 = \u003cmark>0.67\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Both modules reach the three-attempt minimum, so rank by help rate\u003c/p>\u003cpre>0.67 Prefix sums ...   0.25 Sliding window ...\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Plan: prefix sums first: re-read the count-map card, redo {{523}} (Stuck) from a blank file, add three bank problems\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom collections import Counter, defaultdict\n\n\ndef weak_spots(status, module_of, names, min_tried=3):\n    \"\"\"status: problem id -&gt; 0..3 (1 Solved, 2 Brute only, 3 Stuck). module_of: id -&gt; module key.\n    Returns (help rate, module, tried, solved, brute only, stuck), worst first.\"\"\"\n    tally = defaultdict(Counter)\n    for pid, s in status.items():\n        if s and pid in module_of:\n            tally[module_of[pid]][s] += 1\n    rows = []\n    for key, c in tally.items():\n        tried = sum(c.values())\n        if tried &gt;= min_tried:\n            rows.append((round((c[2] + c[3]) / tried, 2), names[key], tried, c[1], c[2], c[3]))\n    return sorted(rows, reverse=True)\n\n\ndef load(progress_file_text, page_html_text):\n    \"\"\"progress: the exported JSON file; page: the saved dsa.html, whose data sits in a\n    &lt;script id=\"guide-data\"&gt; element.\"\"\"\n    entries = json.loads(progress_file_text)[\"trackers\"][\"dsa-status\"]\n    status = {pid: e[\"status\"] for pid, e in entries.items()}\n    start = page_html_text.index('id=\"guide-data\"&gt;') + len('id=\"guide-data\"&gt;')\n    data = json.loads(page_html_text[start:page_html_text.index(\"&lt;/script&gt;\", start)])\n    module_of, names = {}, {}\n    for m in data[\"modules\"]:\n        names[m[\"key\"]] = m[\"name\"]\n        for row in m.get(\"practice\", []):\n            module_of[row[0]] = m[\"key\"]\n        for pid in m.get(\"bank\", []):\n            module_of[pid] = m[\"key\"]\n    return status, module_of, names\n\n\n# status, module_of, names = load(open(\"study-progress.json\").read(), open(\"dsa.html\").read())\n# for row in weak_spots(status, module_of, names): print(row)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Card statuses (Done, Needs practice, Revisit) live in the same export under ids that are not problem numbers; \u003ccode>module_of\u003c/code> only maps problems, so cards are ignored here. Read their Needs practice marks separately.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small samples.\u003c/b> Two attempts make a rate of 0, 0.5 or 1; wait for at least three.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No data is not strength.\u003c/b> A module you never practised has no rate; check the untried ones too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixed problems.\u003c/b> All review-set problems belong to this module in the data, so a high rate here means \"recognition\"; use the Pattern line and your error log to see which techniques they were.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Gaming the metric.\u003c/b> Generous Solved marks make the numbers look good and hide exactly the weaknesses you are looking for.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A module has six tried problems: three Solved, two Brute only, one Stuck. What are its help rate and stuck rate, and is it a weak spot?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Help rate (2 + 1) / 6 = 0.5 and stuck rate 1/6, about 0.17. Half the attempts needed help, so it is a candidate; the low stuck rate suggests mostly Brute only results, so work on optimisation (target complexity, the ladder) rather than re-learning the idea.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your mixed review sets show a help rate of 0.6 while every module's own practice is below 0.2. What is the diagnosis?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You can execute each technique when the module tells you which one to use, but you struggle to choose it unlabelled: a recognition weakness. Spend the next two weeks on classification sprints, the atlas and the look-alike pairs rather than on re-reading technique cards.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your two weakest modules by rate are Advanced dynamic programming (optional) and Sliding window, and an interview is three weeks away. Which comes first, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sliding window: it is essential, appears in a large share of interview problems and is fast to fix with a few re-solves. Advanced DP is optional and rarely asked; plan it after the interview.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Solve the three problems, then export your progress and run \u003ccode>weak_spots\u003c/code>. Expected: a list of rows \u003ccode>(help rate, module, tried, solved, brute only, stuck)\u003c/code>, worst first, and a written two-week plan for the top row.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>2966 Divide Array Into Arrays With Max Difference.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[[1, 2, 3], [7, 8, 9]]\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 7, 8, 9], k = 2\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code> for the same array with \u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1944 Number of Visible People in a Queue.\u003c/b> Expected: \u003ccode>[2, 1, 2, 1, 0]\u003c/code> for heights \u003ccode>[5, 3, 4, 1, 2]\u003c/code>; O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2125 Number of Laser Beams in a Bank.\u003c/b> Expected: 6 for \u003ccode>[\"101\", \"000\", \"011\", \"010\"]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to turn your statuses and error log into a ranked list of weak spots and a two-week practice plan.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:mixed/classification-sprint-twenty-statements-in-thirt":"\u003cp>Recognition is a skill you can drill without writing code. This sprint uses twenty short statements written for this guide: each is a disguised version of a common problem shape, and several come in look-alike pairs on purpose.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Set a timer for 30 minutes. For each statement below write four things on paper, without opening any answer: the \u003cb>technique\u003c/b>, the \u003cb>module\u003c/b>, the \u003cb>deciding cue\u003c/b> (the phrase or constraint that decided it) and the \u003cb>target complexity\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do all twenty before checking anything. If you are stuck on one for more than two minutes, write your best guess and move on: guessing under time pressure is part of the drill.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When the timer ends, open the answers one by one. Score one point for the technique and one for the deciding cue (a different cue that also decides it counts).\u003c/li>\u003cli>For every miss, add a line to your error log with the category \u003ccode>technique\u003c/code> and the cue you missed, and add the cue to your atlas or as a card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Repeat the sprint in two weeks with the order reversed, and compare scores and times.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>The twenty statements\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">1. A sensor log holds up to 10^5 readings, some of them negative. Count the stretches of consecutive readings that add up to exactly T.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Prefix sums with a hash map of counts (Prefix sums and difference arrays). Deciding cue: an exact target with negative values, so a window cannot decide when to shrink; count earlier running totals equal to total - T. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">2. Given a string of up to 10^5 lowercase letters, find the length of the longest stretch that uses at most three distinct letters.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Variable sliding window with letter counts (Sliding window). Deciding cue: longest contiguous stretch under a condition that stays true when the stretch shrinks (\"at most three distinct\"). O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">3. A sorted list of prices and a budget B: find two different items whose prices add up to exactly B, using O(1) extra memory.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two pointers from both ends (Two pointers). Deciding cue: sorted input, a pair with a target, and O(1) extra space, which rules out a hash set. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">4. Parcels with weights must ship in the given order over d days; each day's load cannot exceed the truck's capacity. Find the smallest capacity that ships everything within d days (n up to 10^5).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Binary search on the answer with a greedy feasibility check (Binary search and searching the answer). Deciding cue: \"smallest capacity that works\", and a larger capacity never hurts, so feasibility is monotone. O(n log(sum of weights)).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">5. For each day in a list of share prices, report how many days you would wait for a strictly higher price (0 if it never comes).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Monotonic stack (Monotonic stacks and queues). Deciding cue: \"the next strictly higher value to the right\" for every element. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">6. Jobs arrive at a server over time, each with a priority. At every tick the server runs the waiting job with the highest priority. Simulate 10^5 arrivals.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Heap (Heaps and priority queues). Deciding cue: repeatedly take the best item while new items keep arriving. O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">7. Given 10^5 exam scores that never change, report the 100th highest score once.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Selection: \u003ccode>heapq.nlargest(100, scores)[-1]\u003c/code>, quickselect or a sort (Sorting, partitioning and selection). Deciding cue: one question on fixed data; a heap kept up to date over time is not needed. O(n log k) with nlargest, O(n) on average with quickselect.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">8. Given the start and end times of many meetings, find the smallest number of rooms so that no two overlapping meetings share a room.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Sweep line over +1/-1 events, or a min-heap of end times (Intervals and sweep line). Deciding cue: start and end pairs, and the answer is the peak overlap. O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">9. A family tree lists each person's wealth. Find the largest total wealth of any person together with all of their descendants.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tree recursion returning subtree sums up (Binary trees and tree recursion). Deciding cue: each answer combines a node with the answers of its children. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">10. Store 10^5 file paths, then answer many queries of the form \"how many stored paths begin with this prefix?\".\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Trie with a count at each node (Tries). Deciding cue: many prefix queries over a large shared vocabulary. O(length) per query. (A sorted list with two bisects also works for static data.)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">11. In a city grid with walls, find the fewest moves from the entrance to any exit; every move takes one minute.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Breadth-first search, multi-source from all exits or single-source from the entrance (Graph modelling and traversal). Deciding cue: fewest moves when every move costs the same. O(cells).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">12. Roads between depots have different travel times, all positive. Find the quickest time from the main depot to every shop.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Dijkstra with a heap (Weighted shortest paths). Deciding cue: weighted edges with non-negative costs, single source. O((V + E) log V).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">13. Courses have prerequisites. Produce an order in which to take all of them, or report that no order exists.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Topological sort with Kahn's algorithm or DFS (Graph modelling and traversal). Deciding cue: dependency order, and \"no order\" means a cycle. O(V + E).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">14. Friendship links are added one at a time. After each addition, report how many separate friend groups exist.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Union-find with a component counter (Union-find and minimum spanning trees). Deciding cue: links only added, and only group membership is asked; each successful union lowers the count by one. Near O(1) per link.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">15. From at most 12 distinct numbers, list every selection whose sum is exactly T.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Backtracking with pruning (Complete search and backtracking). Deciding cue: \"list every\" and a tiny n; the output itself can be exponential. O(2^n · n) in the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">16. Given up to 100 positive numbers, each at most 100, count the selections whose sum is exactly T (T up to 10^4), modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>0/1 knapsack counting DP (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks). Deciding cue: only a count is asked, with a small target sum, so states (items used, sum) repeat; loop the sums downwards so each number is used once. O(n · T).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">17. Daily prices: make as many buy-then-sell trades as you like, but after selling you must wait one day before buying again. Maximise the profit.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>State-machine DP with states holding, just sold and resting (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables). Deciding cue: a mode that changes what you may do next (the cooldown). O(n), O(1) space.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">18. Children each need a biscuit of at least their own appetite size; each biscuit goes to at most one child. Maximise the number of satisfied children.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Greedy after sorting both lists, matching the smallest adequate biscuit to each child in order (Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments). Deciding cue: an exchange argument shows giving the smallest biscuit that fits is never worse. O(n log n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">19. Build a cache with get and put in O(1) that removes the least recently used key when it is full.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Hash map plus a doubly linked list, or OrderedDict with move_to_end (Designing data structures). Deciding cue: \"implement a class\" with O(1) for several operations at once, including an order-based eviction.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">20. In a list where every value appears exactly twice except one, find the lone value using O(1) extra memory.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>XOR of all values (Bits and number theory). Deciding cue: pairs cancel and O(1) memory rules out a set; x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x. O(n).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A first attempt usually takes 25 to 35 minutes; under 20 means you are ready for the review sets at full speed, over 35 means more atlas work first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Most misses come from the look-alike pairs placed on purpose: 1 and 2 (prefix sums or window), 6 and 7 (heap or selection), 11 and 12 (BFS or Dijkstra), 15 and 16 (backtracking or DP), 17 and 18 (DP or greedy). If you missed one side of a pair, re-read its row in the look-alike card.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The error log gains one \u003ccode>technique\u003c/code> line per miss, each naming the cue you overlooked. On the repeat sprint two weeks later the score should rise and the time fall; if a pair is still missed, make a card for its deciding question.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:219":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an integer array and a nonnegative distance \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, determine whether two different indices hold equal values and differ by at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 3\u003c/code>, return true; with \u003ccode>k = 2\u003c/code>, return false. The indices, not the values, determine the distance.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The array can contain many repeated and negative values. A value may occur more than twice, and \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> may be zero or larger than the array. Only existence is requested; the actual indices are not required. A quadratic scan is costly for a large array.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Report whether some value repeats with its two positions at most k apart\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you meet a value, which earlier occurrence of it is the only one worth comparing with?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Hash map of last positions, or a set over the last k values (Hash tables, sets and counting)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Only the most recent previous index of each value matters, because it is the closest. Keep a dict from value to last index and check i - last[x] &lt;= k before updating it; or keep a set of the last k values and remove the one that falls out of range.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(min(n, k)) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every pair of indices \u003ccode>i &lt; j\u003c/code>, checking both equality and \u003ccode>j - i &lt;= k\u003c/code>. Stop when a pair passes both tests. There are O(n²) pairs in the worst case and O(1) auxiliary space. Limiting the inner scan to the next k positions improves the bound but can still be quadratic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each index, remember the most recent index for each value. Among earlier equal values, the most recent is closest to the current index. If that distance is too large, all older occurrences are too far away as well. If it is small enough, the stored occurrence is a valid witness. Update the index after checking so the current element is never paired with itself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, 2, 1], k = 2; initially last = {}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>index 0 stores 1 -&gt; 0; index 1 stores 2 -&gt; 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">index 2 sees 1 last at 0; distance 2 &lt;= 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The map describes only positions strictly before the current one. Replacing an old position by a newer one preserves every possible future answer because the newer one is never farther away.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def contains_nearby_duplicate(nums, k):\n    last = {}\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        if value in last and i - last[value] &lt;= k:\n            return True\n        last[value] = i\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expected O(n) time with hash-table operations and O(min(n, u)) space, where u is the number of distinct values. The implementation retains every distinct value, even when its last position is older than k.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>k = 0\u003c/code> cannot match two different indices; equality at exactly distance k is allowed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check before updating the index, or every element matches itself.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty and singleton arrays return false. A later repeat may be close even if an earlier repeat was not.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Maintain a set of only the previous k elements for a bounded-size window.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return a pair of qualifying indices instead of a Boolean.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1679":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an integer array and a target \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, repeatedly remove two remaining elements whose sum is \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. Return the maximum number of operations. Each occurrence may be used only once. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>k = 5\u003c/code>, two operations are possible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The same number may appear many times, so positions are separate resources even when their values agree. The official input has positive integers, but the reasoning also works for zero or negative values. The number of operations, not the chosen pairs, is requested. Large arrays make checking all pairings impractical.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove as many disjoint pairs whose sum is exactly k as possible\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For a value x only one partner value works. How many pairs can x and its partner form together?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting complements (Hash tables, sets and counting), or sort and two pointers (Two pointers)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Different value classes never compete: x can only pair with k - x. With a Counter add min(count[x], count[k - x]) for x &lt; k - x, and count[x] // 2 when 2x = k. Sorting and moving two pointers inwards also works: if the sum is too big, the largest value has no partner left.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time with a Counter, O(n log n) with sorting\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all possible first legal pairs, remove each pair in turn, and recursively find the best continuation; also return zero when no pair remains. This explores exponentially many removal sequences and needs linear recursion depth plus copies if arrays are copied. The same remaining multiset can be reached by many different orders.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each value has exactly one possible partner, \u003ccode>k - value\u003c/code>. Therefore one complement class cannot steal a useful partner from another class. While scanning, keep counts of previously seen occurrences that have not yet been paired. If the current value's complement is available, consume one; otherwise store the current value for a future complement. Forming a pair immediately is safe: exchanging it with any later pair using that same complement leaves the same number of possible pairs. This also handles a value equal to its own complement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [3, 1, 3, 4, 3], k = 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>store 3, then store 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next 3 consumes the waiting 3; 4 is stored\u003c/p>\u003cpre>final 3 waits; answer = \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>After each item, the map represents unused seen occurrences. The counter need not delete zero entries because availability is checked by count, not membership.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, four copies of half the target make two pairs, while three copies make only one. The scan consumes them two at a time without requiring a separate special case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef max_operations(nums, k):\n    waiting = defaultdict(int)\n    answer = 0\n    for value in nums:\n        partner = k - value\n        if waiting[partner] &gt; 0:\n            waiting[partner] -= 1\n            answer += 1\n        else:\n            waiting[value] += 1\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expected O(n) time with hashing and O(n) auxiliary space for counts. Each element is processed once; \u003ccode>defaultdict\u003c/code> may also create entries for queried complements that never appear. The returned integer uses constant space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>When \u003ccode>2 * value == k\u003c/code>, one operation needs two distinct occurrences, not one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Decrement the partner count after matching, or the same occurrence can be reused.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty array, no complementary values, or a single element yields zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sort a copy and use two pointers to obtain O(n log n) time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the actual index pairs by storing lists of waiting indices.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:930":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary array and an integer \u003ccode>goal\u003c/code>, count its nonempty contiguous subarrays whose sum equals \u003ccode>goal\u003c/code>. Different start or end indices count separately, even if their values are identical. For \u003ccode>[1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\u003c/code> and goal 2, there are four qualifying subarrays.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every entry is zero or one and the requested sum is nonnegative. Zeros can extend a matching subarray without changing its sum. The answer can be quadratic in n even though an efficient algorithm need not enumerate it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the subarrays of a 0/1 array whose sum is exactly goal\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A subarray's sum is the difference of two running totals. For the current total, which earlier totals complete a subarray with the right sum?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums with a count map (Prefix sums and difference arrays)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a Counter of running totals seen so far, seeded with {0: 1}; at each index add count[total - goal], then record the total. Because the values are 0 or 1, atMost(goal) - atMost(goal - 1) with a shrinking window also works.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) extra with the window version)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each starting index, extend an ending index to the right, accumulating the sum and counting each match. There are O(n²) intervals and each extension costs O(1), so time is O(n²) and auxiliary space is O(1). Recomputing each sum from scratch would take even longer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let the prefix total before an interval be \u003ccode>p\u003c/code> and the total through its end be \u003ccode>t\u003c/code>. Its sum is \u003ccode>t - p\u003c/code>, so each earlier prefix equal to \u003ccode>t - goal\u003c/code> starts a valid interval. Finding just one matching window per endpoint is not enough. Store how often each prefix total has occurred. Seed the empty prefix with frequency one so intervals starting at index zero are counted. Query before recording the current prefix to rule out empty intervals, especially when goal is zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, 0, 1], goal = 1; counts = {0: 1}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first total 1 finds one earlier 0; answer 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">second total 1 finds another 0; answer 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>third total 2 finds two earlier totals 1; answer \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The two occurrences of prefix total one correspond to different starts; their multiplicity must be retained. Prefix sums also work for negative values, although this problem supplies binary values.\u003c/p>\u003cp>At each endpoint the lookup counts all valid starts at once, rather than choosing a single window. This distinction is especially important when consecutive zeros give several prefixes the same total.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef num_subarrays_with_sum(nums, goal):\n    counts = defaultdict(int)\n    counts[0] = 1\n    total = answer = 0\n    for value in nums:\n        total += value\n        answer += counts[total - goal]\n        counts[total] += 1\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expected O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space for the frequency map. The largest count can be n(n + 1)/2 for all zeros and goal zero; Python integers grow as necessary.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Initialising the empty prefix is necessary for matches starting at zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store frequencies, not just distinct totals; several zero entries create repeated totals.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty input gives zero; goal zero on \u003ccode>[0, 0]\u003c/code> gives three, not two.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For nonnegative entries, subtract the number of sums at most goal minus those at most goal minus one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace binary entries with arbitrary signed integers; prefix frequencies still apply.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:438":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given lowercase strings \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> and nonempty \u003ccode>p\u003c/code>, return every starting index in \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> where the substring of length \u003ccode>len(p)\u003c/code> is a rearrangement of \u003ccode>p\u003c/code>. Return indices in increasing order. For \u003ccode>s = \"cbaebabacd\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>p = \"abc\"\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[0, 6]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The alphabet has exactly 26 lowercase English letters. A match must have the same length and the same count of every letter; the order inside it is irrelevant. If the pattern is longer than the text, there are no candidates. Overlapping matches are allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find every start position where a stretch of s is a rearrangement of p\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every match has exactly len(p) letters. What changes when the stretch moves one place to the right?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Fixed-size sliding window with letter counts (Sliding window)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Slide a window of length len(p) over s with 26 letter counts: add the entering letter, remove the leaving one, and record the start whenever the counts equal those of p. Tracking how many of the 26 counts currently match makes each step O(1).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space (26 counts)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every possible start, slice the candidate substring and sort it and the pattern before comparing. If n is the text length and m the pattern length, this costs O(n m log m) time in a straightforward implementation and O(m) temporary space. Most of the work is repeated between overlapping candidates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All candidates have the same length. The fixed alphabet makes each frequency table a fixed size. Keep one array of letter counts for the pattern and another for the current text window. Add the new rightmost character and remove the character that has just fallen out when the window grows beyond m. Once the window has length m, equal arrays mean equal multisets and hence an anagram. Comparing 26 entries is constant work because the alphabet size is fixed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = \"abab\", p = \"ab\"; first window \"ab\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>counts agree, record 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">slide to \"ba\", then \"ab\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>counts agree at both starts; return \u003cmark>[0, 1, 2]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Removing the old character before comparison ensures that exactly m text characters contribute. Equal length by itself is insufficient: each of the 26 counts must agree.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a one-character pattern, the same loop checks every individual text character. The code assumes the stated nonempty pattern; an empty pattern would require a separately defined convention.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def find_anagrams(s, p):\n    m = len(p)\n    if m &gt; len(s):\n        return []\n    target = [0] * 26\n    window = [0] * 26\n    for ch in p:\n        target[ord(ch) - ord('a')] += 1\n    answer = []\n    for i, ch in enumerate(s):\n        window[ord(ch) - ord('a')] += 1\n        if i &gt;= m:\n            window[ord(s[i - m]) - ord('a')] -= 1\n        if i &gt;= m - 1 and window == target:\n            answer.append(i - m + 1)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Time is O(n + m) because comparing 26 counts costs O(1); auxiliary space is O(1) for the fixed alphabet, excluding up to O(n) returned indices. If the alphabet were variable, each full comparison would cost O(alphabet size).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not compare incomplete windows, or report end indices instead of start indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Repeated letters must be counted; sets cannot distinguish \u003ccode>aab\u003c/code> from \u003ccode>abb\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A longer pattern returns an empty list; overlapping answers are not skipped.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track the number of matching count positions to avoid comparing all entries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generalise to arbitrary characters with a map and track the number of nonzero differences.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:189":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rotate an integer array to the right by \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> positions, wrapping elements that leave the end back to the front. Modify the supplied array in place and return no value. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 2\u003c/code>, the array becomes \u003ccode>[4, 5, 1, 2, 3]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The number of shifts is nonnegative and may exceed the array length. Values may repeat and must retain their relative cyclic order. In-place means the original list object must be changed, not merely a local variable reassigned.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Shift every element k places to the right, wrapping around, in place\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After the shift the array is two blocks that have swapped places. Which simple in-place operation on a range reverses the order of things?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-place reversals (Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Set k %= n, reverse the whole array, then reverse the first k elements and the remaining n - k. The full reversal puts the two blocks in the right places but each one backwards; the two partial reversals fix their insides.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeat k times: save the final value, move every other element one position right, and put the saved value at the front. This uses O(1) auxiliary space and O(nk) time. Reducing k modulo n helps but may still leave quadratic work when k is near n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A full cycle of n shifts changes nothing, so only the remainder matters. A right rotation cuts the sequence into an initial block A and a final block B, then produces B followed by A. Reverse the entire list to obtain reverse(B) followed by reverse(A). Reversing each of those two ranges restores the internal order while keeping their new positions. Each reversal swaps its endpoints until the pointers meet, using no copied slices.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">[1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2; reverse all\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[5, 4, 3, 2, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">reverse positions 0..1, then 2..4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>[4, 5, 1, 2, 3] = \u003cmark>rotated array\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The helper accepts inclusive bounds; an empty range such as \u003ccode>0..-1\u003c/code> requires no swaps. The explicit empty-array guard avoids taking a remainder modulo zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The three reversals are a composition, not three separate rotations. Each swap modifies the original list directly. In particular, when k is zero the full reversal and the final reversal cancel, which preserves the input ordering without requiring a special branch.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def rotate(nums, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    if not n:\n        return\n    k %= n\n\n    def reverse(left, right):\n        while left &lt; right:\n            nums[left], nums[right] = nums[right], nums[left]\n            left += 1\n            right -= 1\n\n    reverse(0, n - 1)\n    reverse(0, k - 1)\n    reverse(k, n - 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each reversal touches at most n positions, so time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The small helper uses a constant number of local variables and does not allocate an array proportional to n.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reduce k modulo n only after handling an empty array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reverse both blocks, not just the first, to restore their order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Zero shifts and a one-element array leave the original object unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Rotate left by reversing the corresponding blocks in the opposite arrangement.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use cyclic replacement and track how many positions have been written.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1657":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two lowercase words are called close if one can become the other using any number of position swaps and global exchanges of two existing letters. A global exchange changes every occurrence of each chosen letter into the other. Decide whether the given words are close. For \u003ccode>\"abbccc\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"cccbba\"\u003c/code>, return true.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Only the 26 lowercase letters occur. A position swap exchanges two characters at selected positions. A global exchange replaces each occurrence of one selected existing letter by the other, in both directions. Word lengths and counts may be large, so exploring every sequence of operations is not practical.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Decide whether two words can be made equal by swapping letters and by exchanging the roles of two letters everywhere\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List what the two operations can never change about a word.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Comparing invariants with counts (Hash tables, sets and counting)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Swaps keep the multiset of letters; exchanging two letters swaps their counts. So the words are close exactly when they use the same set of letters and their sorted lists of letter counts are equal (a letter missing from one word can never appear, since exchanges only use existing letters).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) extra space (26 counts)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Treat each distinct word arrangement as a state and perform a graph search over all swaps and allowed global exchanges. This is finite but can have exponentially many states; with n positions even the number of distinct arrangements can approach n!. Storing visited strings also takes substantial space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two invariants completely describe feasibility: the set of letters present, and the multiset of their positive frequencies. A position swap cannot alter counts; a global exchange cannot introduce a new letter. Position swaps change neither invariant; global exchanges permute the frequencies among the existing letters. Conversely, if both invariants agree, exchange letter identities until every letter has the desired frequency. Then position swaps arrange the resulting multiset into the target word. Equal total length follows from equal positive frequency multisets, though a direct length check is also useful.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">word1 = \"abbccc\": counts a:1, b:2, c:3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>word2 = \"aaabbc\": counts a:3, b:2, c:1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">both use {a, b, c}, with frequencies [1, 2, 3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>exchange a and c; then reorder positions =&gt; \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Matching only sorted counts would wrongly accept words whose alphabets differ. Matching only alphabets would miss incompatible multiplicities.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For instance, \u003ccode>aab\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>abb\u003c/code> are close because exchanging the roles of a and b swaps their counts. By contrast, \u003ccode>aab\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>aac\u003c/code> cannot be close because c is absent from the first word.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\ndef close_strings(word1, word2):\n    if len(word1) != len(word2):\n        return False\n    first = Counter(word1)\n    second = Counter(word2)\n    return (first.keys() == second.keys()\n            and sorted(first.values()) == sorted(second.values()))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting takes O(n + m) time for word lengths n and m. Sorting at most 26 counts is constant work under the fixed-alphabet constraint, and the counters use O(1) auxiliary space. For an unrestricted alphabet of u distinct characters, sorting instead costs O(u log u) and space is O(u).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Equal frequency multisets alone do not allow replacing a letter that was absent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Zero counts need not be sorted because equality of present-letter sets is checked separately.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Different lengths fail immediately; identical words always pass.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compare histograms of frequencies rather than sorting them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Construct an explicit sequence of relabellings and position swaps when a witness is required.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1004":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary array and nonnegative \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, find the greatest length of a contiguous run of ones obtainable by changing at most k zeros to ones. For \u003ccode>[1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>k = 1\u003c/code>, the answer is 3. No actual changes need to be returned.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The chosen entries must remain contiguous, and fewer than k flips are allowed. Large inputs call for avoiding repeated work over many possible intervals. If k covers every zero, the entire array is feasible.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Longest run of 1s if you may flip at most k zeros\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Restate it without flipping: which stretches of the array are acceptable as they are?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Variable sliding window (Sliding window)\u003c/p>\u003cp>A stretch with at most k zeros can be turned into all 1s, so find the longest such stretch. Grow the right end, count zeros, and advance the left end while the count exceeds k; a stretch with too many zeros stays bad as it grows, so the left end never moves back.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every interval and count its zeros while extending the right endpoint from each fixed left endpoint. Record the longest interval with at most k zeros. This takes O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space; recomputing counts from scratch for each interval would be O(n³).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A candidate interval is feasible precisely when it contains at most k zeros. Grow a window from left to right and track its number of zeros. Whenever the count exceeds k, remove elements from its left until it becomes valid again. Removing a leftmost zero decreases the count; removing a one does not. Any earlier left endpoint was invalid for the current right endpoint, since it included all zeros in the invalid window. Thus the restored window is the longest feasible one ending at that right endpoint.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">[1, 0, 1, 0, 1], k = 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first three positions contain one zero; best = 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">second zero makes count 2; remove 1 and then 0 from left\u003c/p>\u003cpre>window [1, 0, 1] is valid; best stays \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both endpoints move only forward. The maximum over all right endpoints is therefore the overall maximum, including intervals ending at the last element.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When removing elements, the left endpoint may pass several ones before reaching a zero. The count changes only when that zero leaves, and the window can temporarily be empty when k is zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_ones(nums, k):\n    left = zeros = best = 0\n    for right, value in enumerate(nums):\n        if value == 0:\n            zeros += 1\n        while zeros &gt; k:\n            if nums[left] == 0:\n                zeros -= 1\n            left += 1\n        best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each index enters once and leaves at most once, so time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The nested while loop is amortised linear, not quadratic, because the left pointer never moves backward.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Shrink until valid, not merely by one position, for the stated window invariant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>k = 0\u003c/code> finds the longest existing run of ones; all zeros may produce zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty array returns zero; all ones return the array length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the endpoints of a longest feasible interval as well as its length.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count intervals with at most k zeros by adding the current valid window length at every endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:523":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given an array of nonnegative integers and a positive integer \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, decide whether some contiguous subarray of length at least two has a sum divisible by \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>[23, 2, 4, 6, 7]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 6\u003c/code>, the answer is true because \u003ccode>[2, 4]\u003c/code> sums to 6. A zero sum is divisible by any positive k.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The array can contain zeros and the sum need not equal k: any multiple qualifies. The minimum length rules out a single element even if it is itself divisible by k. Large arrays make checking all intervals expensive. The official k is positive, so remainder by zero is not part of this contract.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Is there a stretch of at least two numbers whose sum is a multiple of k?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When do two running totals differ by a multiple of k?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums modulo k with a first-index map (Prefix sums and difference arrays)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two running totals with the same remainder mod k enclose a multiple of k. Store the first index of each remainder, seeding remainder 0 at index -1, and report success when a remainder reappears at least two positions later; keeping only the first index makes each candidate stretch as long as possible.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(min(n, k)) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each start, extend an end index and accumulate the sum; test divisibility whenever at least two values have been included. There are O(n²) candidate intervals, giving O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space. This repeats work across starts despite maintaining each running sum incrementally.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The difference of two prefix sums is divisible by k exactly when the sums have equal remainders modulo k. Record the earliest index for each remainder. A repeat at index i encloses a divisible interval after that earlier index; it is long enough when the distance is at least two. Keeping the earliest occurrence maximises that distance, so later occurrences should not overwrite it. Seed remainder zero at index -1 to represent the empty prefix and allow a subarray beginning at zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [23, 2, 4], k = 6; first = {0: -1}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefix remainders at indices 0 and 1 are 5 and 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">index 2 gives remainder 5, first seen at index 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>distance 2, so [2, 4] qualifies: \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A repeated remainder at adjacent indices is not enough; keep its original index so a later repeat may still produce a valid length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def check_subarray_sum(nums, k):\n    first = {0: -1}\n    remainder = 0\n    for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n        remainder = (remainder + value) % k\n        if remainder in first:\n            if i - first[remainder] &gt;= 2:\n                return True\n        else:\n            first[remainder] = i\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expected O(n) time for hash operations and O(min(n, k)) auxiliary space, up to a constant for the seeded entry. There are only k possible remainders. Python's integer arithmetic avoids fixed-width sum overflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not overwrite an earlier index after seeing a remainder again.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two zeros qualify; one zero alone does not. An array shorter than two always fails.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The empty prefix has index -1, not zero; include distance exactly two.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a witness interval using the stored first index.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a zero divisor under a different specification, look for a length-two-or-more zero-sum subarray using exact prefix sums instead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:16":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose three elements at distinct indices whose sum is closest to a given target; return that sum. The input has at least three integers. For \u003ccode>[-1, 2, 1, -4]\u003c/code> and target 1, return 2. The original task guarantees a unique closest sum, so a tie-breaking policy is unnecessary there.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Values may be negative, duplicated, or positive, and the sum may be either side of the target. Exactly three occurrences are required, not three different values. The input need not be ordered. A cubic enumeration can become expensive as the input grows.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Choose three numbers whose sum is as close as possible to a target\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Put the numbers in order first. With one number fixed, if the other two add up to too much, which of them should change?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort, fix one, two pointers (Two pointers)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort, fix i, and move lo and hi inwards: a sum above the target can only get closer by lowering hi, one below it by raising lo. Track the closest sum seen; an exact match can return at once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time, O(1) extra space beyond sorting\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all index triples \u003ccode>i &lt; j &lt; l\u003c/code>, compute each sum, and retain the one with smallest absolute difference from the target. There are O(n³) triples, so time is O(n³) and auxiliary space is O(1), apart from the input. This is straightforward and useful as a test oracle for small arrays.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort a copy of the numbers and fix the first index, leaving the caller's input unchanged. For the two remaining indices, start at opposite ends. If the current sum is below target, moving the upper pointer left would only decrease it further; after recording this sum, no discarded pair using the current lower pointer and a smaller upper pointer can improve it. Move the lower pointer right instead. Symmetrically, if the sum is above target, move the upper pointer left. An exact match cannot be improved.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">sorted [-4, -1, 1, 2], target 1; fix -4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>-4 + -1 + 2 = -3; move lower pointer right\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">-4 + 1 + 2 = -1; next fix -1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>-1 + 1 + 2 = 2, closest sum = \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The initial best is a real triple, so the algorithm has a valid answer even if every sum is far from the target.\u003c/p>\u003cp>No special duplicate skipping is necessary for correctness: repeated values can lead to redundant comparisons but never cause an invalid triple, since the pointers always refer to different positions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def three_sum_closest(nums, target):\n    values = sorted(nums)\n    best = sum(values[:3])\n    for i in range(len(values) - 2):\n        left, right = i + 1, len(values) - 1\n        while left &lt; right:\n            current = values[i] + values[left] + values[right]\n            if abs(current - target) &lt; abs(best - target):\n                best = current\n            if current == target:\n                return current\n            if current &lt; target:\n                left += 1\n            else:\n                right -= 1\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting costs O(n log n); n fixed indices each have a linear two-pointer scan, for O(n²) time total. The sorted copy uses O(n) space. The pointer variables themselves use O(1) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep three different indices even if values repeat; stop when pointers meet.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare absolute differences, not sums themselves. If ties are allowed, this code retains its first best sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Exactly three elements still provide one valid candidate.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the three values along with the closest sum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find a closest pair by using the same two-pointer scan without fixing a third element.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2125":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A bank is a rectangular grid of \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> characters; a \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> is a security device. A beam connects each pair of devices in different rows when every row strictly between them contains no device. Count all such beams. In \u003ccode>[\"101\", \"000\", \"011\", \"010\"]\u003c/code>, the answer is 6.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Columns and horizontal distance do not restrict a beam. A row can have zero, one, or many devices, and empty rows can occur anywhere. Multiple devices in the same row do not connect to one another. The grid may be large, so it is important to avoid testing every device pair separately.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count connections between devices on different rows when every row between them is empty\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Do the empty rows matter at all? Which pairs of rows actually connect?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Row aggregation on a grid (Arrays, dynamic arrays, strings and grids)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Drop the empty rows; each remaining row connects only with the next non-empty one, and every pair of devices across them is one beam. Sum count[r] * count[next] over consecutive non-empty rows.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>List all devices, inspect every pair in different rows, and scan the rows between them to check whether they are empty. With D devices and R rows this can take O(D²R) time; storing the device list takes O(D) space. It checks the same intervening rows repeatedly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Only consecutive nonempty rows can have beams between them. Any pair with another nonempty row between is blocked, while consecutive nonempty rows have no intervening devices. If those rows contain a and b devices, each of the a devices connects to each of the b, giving a times b beams. Scan rows in order and remember the previous nonempty count; empty rows do not replace it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">rows \"101\", \"000\", \"011\", \"010\"\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first count 2; skip the empty row\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next count 2 contributes 2 * 2 = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>final count 1 contributes 2 * 1 = 2; total \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The stored count always belongs to the most recent row with a device, so every legal row pair is counted once and no blocked pair is considered.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, two devices in one row and three in the next nonempty row contribute six regardless of their columns. Leading and trailing empty rows contribute nothing to the total.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def number_of_beams(bank):\n    previous = answer = 0\n    for row in bank:\n        count = row.count('1')\n        if count:\n            answer += previous * count\n            previous = count\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For R rows and C columns, counting devices scans O(RC) characters and uses O(1) auxiliary space. The input itself is not copied. The answer can be much larger than the number of cells; Python integers support large counts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not reset the previous nonempty count when an empty row appears.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include the last nonempty row's contribution; a single nonempty row yields zero.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Devices in the same row do not form beams, and columns do not have to align.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the beam contribution for each consecutive pair of nonempty rows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If devices are given as row counts directly, scan those counts instead of the full grid.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:946":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given two sequences of distinct values, \u003ccode>pushed\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>popped\u003c/code>, decide whether a stack starting empty can push all values in the first order and pop all values in the second order. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[4, 5, 3, 2, 1]\u003c/code>, the answer is true.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The official sequences have the same length and consist of the same distinct values. Pushes must follow their specified order, but pushes and pops may interleave. A stack can pop only its current top. Merely checking that the sequences contain the same values cannot establish a valid ordering.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Given a push order and a pop order of distinct values, could one stack have produced both?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Replay the operations. After each push, when is a pop forced, and why is it never wrong to pop as early as possible?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Stack simulation (Linked lists, stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Simulate: push each value, then pop while the top equals the next value of the pop order. Popping early is safe because a top value that is due next can never be popped later once something is pushed over it. Valid exactly when the stack ends empty.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Explore every legal choice to push the next value or pop the current top, maintaining the remaining orders. An attempted pop is legal only if the top is the next required value. The number of interleavings can be exponential, with O(n) stack and recursion space per path. Many choices are unnecessary because an available required pop can be done immediately.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Replay pushes in order. After each push, repeatedly pop while the stack top equals the next required output. Doing that pop immediately is safe: if a valid schedule delayed it, moving the pop earlier does not disturb any already required output, and later pushes still occur in their original order. If the top is not the next output, a pop cannot help, so the only possible progress is another push. Success means every requested pop was produced.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pushed [1, 2, 3], popped [2, 3, 1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push 1, push 2; top equals 2, pop it\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">push 3, pop 3, then pop 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>all required values produced: \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The index into \u003ccode>popped\u003c/code> is the count already produced. Guarding that index prevents reading beyond the end, including for empty sequences.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If all pushes have been processed while some requested pop remains, no legal push is left to reveal a different stack top. The simulation has therefore ruled out every possible schedule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def validate_stack_sequences(pushed, popped):\n    if len(pushed) != len(popped):\n        return False\n    stack = []\n    j = 0\n    for value in pushed:\n        stack.append(value)\n        while stack and j &lt; len(popped) and stack[-1] == popped[j]:\n            stack.pop()\n            j += 1\n    return j == len(popped)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each value is pushed once and popped at most once, so time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(n) for the simulated stack. The while loop cannot perform more than n pops over the whole scan.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A single push can expose several consecutive required pops, so use a while loop.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stop comparing when all requested pops are consumed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Two empty sequences are valid. A different length is rejected before simulation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Produce the sequence of push and pop operations as a witness.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If repeated values are allowed, simulate the same procedure but distinguish occurrences only when the specification requires their identities.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:240":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a rectangular matrix whose rows are nondecreasing left to right and whose columns are nondecreasing top to bottom, determine whether it contains a target. For \u003ccode>[[1, 4, 7], [2, 5, 8], [3, 6, 9]]\u003c/code>, searching for 6 returns true and searching for 10 returns false.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rows and columns are individually ordered, but the first element of one row need not exceed the last element of the previous row. Duplicate values are possible, and ranges of values from different rows can overlap.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Search a matrix whose rows and columns are each sorted ascending, where rows do not continue one another\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find a corner where one comparison with the target rules out a whole row or a whole column.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Staircase search on a sorted matrix (Binary search and searching the answer)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Start at the top-right corner. If the value is larger than the target, everything below it in that column is larger too, so move left; if smaller, everything to its left in that row is smaller, so move down. Each step discards a row or a column: at most m + n steps.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m + n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Examine every cell until the target is found or the matrix is exhausted. For R rows and C columns this takes O(RC) time and O(1) auxiliary space. Searching each row independently with binary search improves this to O(R log C), but does not exploit both orderings at once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The matrix cannot generally be treated as one globally sorted flattened array. Instead, start at the top-right cell of the remaining rectangle. If its value is too large, all values below it in that column are at least as large, so discard that column by moving left. If it is too small, all values to its left in that row are at most as small, so discard that row by moving down. Equality succeeds. Each comparison removes an entire row or column without removing any possible match.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">matrix [[1, 4, 7], [2, 5, 8], [3, 6, 9]], target 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>at 7: too large, move left to 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">4 and then 5 are too small, move down twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>reach 6 and return \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The candidate region is the rows at or below the current row and columns at or left of the current column. When either coordinate leaves the matrix, the candidate region is empty.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An equality check must come before discarding a row or column; equal values may appear in several positions, but the question only asks whether at least one exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def search_matrix(matrix, target):\n    if not matrix or not matrix[0]:\n        return False\n    row, col = 0, len(matrix[0]) - 1\n    while row &lt; len(matrix) and col &gt;= 0:\n        value = matrix[row][col]\n        if value == target:\n            return True\n        if value &gt; target:\n            col -= 1\n        else:\n            row += 1\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each step reduces the number of remaining rows or columns, so time is O(R + C) and auxiliary space is O(1). The empty-matrix guard also supports an empty input beyond the usual nonempty-grid constraint.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start at a corner where the two available directions have opposite order; the top-left corner does not provide that property.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not binary-search the flattened array without a global ordering guarantee.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One-row and one-column matrices work, as do repeated values and absent targets.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start at the bottom-left and move up or right symmetrically.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count values at most a target by accumulating discarded column portions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1944":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>People stand in a line with distinct heights. For each person, count the people to their right whom they can see. Person i sees person j when every person strictly between them is shorter than both endpoints. Return one count per person. For \u003ccode>[10, 6, 8, 5, 11, 9]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2, 1, 1, 0]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Heights are distinct in the official problem; equality would need an explicitly specified blocking rule and different handling. A person always sees the immediate neighbour to the right, but a tall intermediate person can hide many others. The output has n entries, and large n rules out a separate full scan from each person in the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each person in a line, count the people to their right they can see over everyone in between\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If a taller person stands closer, who behind them disappears from view for everyone further left?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack (Monotonic stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan from the right keeping a stack of heights still visible, decreasing from bottom to top. For person i, pop every shorter height (each is visible to i and hidden by i from everyone further left) and count it; if the stack is still non-empty the next taller person is visible too, so add one. Then push i.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each i, inspect every j to its right while tracking the maximum height strictly between i and j. Count j exactly when that maximum is less than both endpoint heights. Update the maximum after the comparison so the target person is not included among the intervening people. Each pair is inspected once, giving O(n²) time, O(1) working space, and O(n) output space. This direct definition is valuable for small independent tests.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process people from right to left, keeping a stack of candidates visible from positions still farther left. Its heights decrease from bottom to top. For a new height h, the top is the nearest remaining candidate. If it is shorter than h, it is visible: everyone between h and that candidate is shorter than that candidate. Pop it and count it. It is now hidden from every future person to the left by h, which is closer and taller. Continue until the stack is empty or its top is taller than h. A remaining taller top is also visible, but blocks every candidate behind it for h; count it once and stop. Push h as a future candidate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why can a popped candidate never matter again? Any observer left of h who might see the popped shorter person must also look past h. Because h exceeds that person's height, the strict visibility condition fails regardless of the observer's own height. Conversely, candidates not popped retain the same relative blocking relationships. This establishes the invariant inductively, beginning with an empty stack at the right edge.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">heights [10, 6, 8, 5, 11, 9], scan from the right\u003c/p>\u003cpre>9 sees nobody; 11 pops 9 and sees one; stack [11]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">5 sees 11; 8 pops 5 and sees 11: count 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>6 sees 8; stack from bottom is [11, 8, 6]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">10 pops 6 and 8, then sees 11\u003c/p>\u003cpre>counts from left to right are \u003cmark>[3, 1, 2, 1, 1, 0]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The stack stores heights rather than indices because the output is written at the current index and a height comparison is all that is needed. The first taller person is counted but retained: it may be visible to a later observer farther left.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def can_see_persons_count(heights):\n    answer = [0] * len(heights)\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(len(heights) - 1, -1, -1):\n        while stack and stack[-1] &lt; heights[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n            answer[i] += 1\n        if stack:\n            answer[i] += 1\n        stack.append(heights[i])\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each height is pushed once and popped at most once. Despite the nested loop, the total time is O(n). The stack can contain n heights, so auxiliary space is O(n); the returned array is another O(n). A strictly increasing input exhibits a large stack in this scan direction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count the first taller person but do not pop it; that person blocks all remaining candidates for the current observer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count every shorter candidate popped, rather than only the last one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The final person sees nobody. An increasing line gives one visible neighbour per position except the last; a decreasing line does too, because the immediate taller-than-the-rest neighbour blocks those behind it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Distinct heights are essential to this exact comparison: duplicate heights require a separate treatment consistent with strict visibility.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the indices of visible people instead of counts; the output itself can be larger than linear.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count visibility in the opposite direction by reversing the traversal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute the next greater element for each person instead of the complete visibility count.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:328":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reorder a singly linked list so that nodes originally at odd positions come first, followed by nodes originally at even positions. Positions are one-based; preserve the relative order within each group. Rewire the existing nodes and return the new head. Thus \u003ccode>1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5\u003c/code> becomes \u003ccode>1 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 4\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The grouping depends on position, not whether a node's value is odd or even. The list may be empty or have only one node, and node values can repeat. The intended solution uses constant extra space and preserves the actual nodes rather than constructing replacement nodes. Every \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> update must avoid losing the remainder of the list or forming a cycle.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reorder a linked list so the nodes in odd positions come first, then the even ones, in place\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Walk once and grow two separate chains. What must you remember in order to join them at the end?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Pointer rewiring on a linked list (Linked lists, stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep the odd tail, the even tail and the even head; repeatedly set odd.next = even.next and even.next = odd.next, advancing both, until even or even.next is None. Then attach the even head after the odd tail.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse the list and save its node references in an array; then relink odd-position references in their original order, followed by the even-position references. This uses O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space. Saving references makes the reordering easy but exceeds the constant-space goal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep tails for the growing odd-position chain and even-position chain, and save the first even node for the final connection. Initially the first and second nodes are those tails. While an even tail has a successor, link the odd tail to that successor and advance the odd tail. Then link the even tail to the next node after the new odd tail and advance the even tail. The unprocessed suffix remains reachable through the even tail until it ends. Finally append the saved even head to the odd tail.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">1 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 4 -&gt; 5; odd = 1, even = 2, saved even head = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>link 1 to 3, then 2 to 4; tails become 3 and 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">link 3 to 5, then 4 to None; append saved 2 after 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>1 -&gt; 3 -&gt; 5 -&gt; 2 -&gt; 4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The even tail terminates the even chain as it advances. A two-node list does not enter the loop and the final link simply preserves its order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def odd_even_list(head):\n    if head is None:\n        return None\n    odd = head\n    even = even_head = head.next\n    while even is not None and even.next is not None:\n        odd.next = even.next\n        odd = odd.next\n        even.next = odd.next\n        even = even.next\n    odd.next = even_head\n    return head\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every node is visited a constant number of times, so time is O(n) and auxiliary space is O(1). The output reuses the input nodes; no node-value copies or auxiliary arrays are required.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Save the even head before moving the even tail; otherwise the second group is lost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Stop when either the even tail or its successor is absent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Group by original position, not by value. Empty and one-node lists remain unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Stably partition nodes by a predicate on their values using two head-and-tail pairs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Group nodes by position modulo three, preserving the order inside each group.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2300":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given positive integer spell strengths, positive potion strengths, and a positive success threshold, return for each spell how many potions produce a product at least the threshold. A potion may be reused for each independent spell. With spells \u003ccode>[5, 1, 3]\u003c/code>, potions \u003ccode>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]\u003c/code>, and success 7, the answer is \u003ccode>[4, 0, 3]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Spells and potions can each be numerous, and their original orders matter only for the spell answers. Values and threshold can be large, so exact integer comparisons are preferable to floating-point rounding. Strengths are strictly positive. Repeated strengths count as separate potions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each spell, count the potions whose product with it reaches a success threshold\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For one spell, which potions work? If the potions were in order, what shape would that group have?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort once, then binary search per query (Binary search and searching the answer)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort the potions once. For spell s the working potions are exactly those &gt;= ceil(success / s), a suffix of the sorted list, so the count is m - bisect_left(potions, (success + s - 1) // s).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O((n + m) log m) time, O(m) for the sorted copy\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each spell, multiply it by every potion and count products reaching the threshold. With n spells and m potions, this costs O(nm) time and O(1) auxiliary space beyond the O(n) output. It does not reuse any work between spells.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort a copy of the potions. Multiplying by a positive spell preserves their order, so all successful potions form a suffix of that sorted array. The smallest sufficient potion has value at least \u003ccode>ceil(success / spell)\u003c/code>, computed exactly as \u003ccode>(success + spell - 1) // spell\u003c/code>. Binary search for the first potion at least that value; all positions from there through the end are successful. This also handles a threshold larger than every potion or satisfied by all potions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">potions [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], success 7, spell 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>minimum potion is ceil(7 / 5) = 2; first qualifying index is 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">there are 5 - 1 = 4 successes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>spells 1 and 3 give 0 and 3; return \u003cmark>[4, 0, 3]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The lower-bound search places all qualifying duplicates in the suffix, including values whose product is exactly equal to success.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each answer is appended in the original spell order. Sorting only the potions means there is no need to remember original potion indices or to restore their order afterward.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from bisect import bisect_left\n\ndef successful_pairs(spells, potions, success):\n    ordered = sorted(potions)\n    answer = []\n    for spell in spells:\n        minimum = (success + spell - 1) // spell\n        first = bisect_left(ordered, minimum)\n        answer.append(len(ordered) - first)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting takes O(m log m); the n binary searches take O(n log m), with the usual constant-time handling of an empty list. The sorted copy needs O(m) auxiliary space, plus O(n) for the returned counts. Python integers avoid fixed-width product or ceiling overflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use a lower bound, not an upper bound: a product equal to the threshold succeeds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute a ceiling rather than floor division. Floating-point division can lose precision for large integers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One potion may succeed for many different spells; do not consume it between queries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sort spells as well and advance a monotone potion pointer, restoring answer order afterward.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Binary-search directly on the product comparison instead of computing a ceiling.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1019":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each node of a singly linked list, return the value of the first later node whose value is strictly greater. If no such node exists, return zero for that position. For \u003ccode>2 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 5\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[5, 5, 0]\u003c/code>. The returned array follows the list's original order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The official list contains positive values, so zero unambiguously indicates no later greater value. Equal values are not greater, and a closer smaller node does not prevent a later larger node from being the answer. Random access is unavailable in the linked list, but the result needs one entry per node anyway.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For every node of a linked list, the value of the first later node that is strictly larger\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Copy the values into a list. Which earlier nodes are still waiting for their answer, and how are their values ordered?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack for the next greater value (Monotonic stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Walk the list keeping a stack of indices whose answer is unknown; their values decrease from bottom to top. A new value pops and answers every smaller value on top, then waits itself. Indices left on the stack keep the answer 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each node, walk forward until finding the first greater value or reaching the end. On a nonincreasing list, every scan reaches the end, producing O(n²) time. Beyond the O(n) output, this approach uses O(1) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a stack of unresolved nodes as \u003ccode>(index, value)\u003c/code> pairs while traversing from left to right. When a new value is strictly greater than the stack top, it is the first greater value for that top: any intervening larger value would already have resolved it. Pop and fill its answer, repeating while the new value is greater. Then push the new node, which has not yet seen any later values. Remaining stack entries keep the initial zero answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">list 2 -&gt; 1 -&gt; 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push (0, 2), then (1, 1); neither has an answer yet\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">5 pops 1 and 2, filling both answers with 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push 5; it stays unresolved: \u003cmark>[5, 5, 0]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The unresolved values are nonincreasing from bottom to top. Equal values may coexist on the stack, since neither resolves the other, and a later larger value resolves both.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The list itself is traversed only once. The stored pair supplies the old value needed for a comparison and the output position needed when a matching later node finally appears.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def next_larger_nodes(head):\n    answer = []\n    stack = []\n    node = head\n    while node is not None:\n        value = node.val\n        while stack and stack[-1][1] &lt; value:\n            index, _ = stack.pop()\n            answer[index] = value\n        stack.append((len(answer), value))\n        answer.append(0)\n        node = node.next\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is pushed and popped at most once, so time is O(n). The unresolved stack uses O(n) auxiliary space in the worst case, and the answer uses O(n) output space. The list is not modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use a strict comparison so an equal later value is not reported as greater.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store indices as well as values to write answers in original order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A single node returns \u003ccode>[0]\u003c/code>; an empty list returns an empty list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the number of nodes until the next greater value instead of that value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the next smaller node by reversing the comparison.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1248":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count nonempty contiguous subarrays containing exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> odd numbers. For \u003ccode>[1, 1, 2, 1, 1]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 3\u003c/code>, the answer is 2. Two intervals with different boundaries count separately, even if they contain the same odd values.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Even values can be included at either end without changing an interval's odd count. Different choices of endpoints are counted separately. The official k is positive, but the implementation also handles zero.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the subarrays that contain exactly k odd numbers\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only parity matters. Replace each number by 1 if it is odd and 0 if even: what are you counting now?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums with a count map after mapping to parity (Prefix sums and difference arrays)\u003c/p>\u003cp>It becomes \"count subarrays with sum exactly k\" on a 0/1 array: a Counter of running totals seeded with {0: 1}, adding count[total - k] at each step; or atMost(k) - atMost(k - 1) with a window.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) with the window version)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every starting position, extend the end to the right, incrementing a count when the new value is odd. Add one whenever that count equals k. This examines O(n²) intervals, taking O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space. It is an effective reference for small arrays.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Only the parity of each integer affects whether an interval qualifies. Map each value to one if odd and zero if even. A subarray has exactly k odds when its mapped sum is k. If the running odd count at the current end is total, any earlier prefix with odd count \u003ccode>total - k\u003c/code> defines a valid start. Keep a frequency table of previous prefix counts; different previous positions with the same count create different intervals. Seed count zero once for the empty prefix and query before adding the current prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [2, 1, 2], k = 1; prefix counts initially {0: 1}\u003c/p>\u003cpre>after first even, zero has frequency 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">odd gives total 1 and contributes 2; final even contributes another 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer = \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Using the parity remainder also works for negative Python integers. The approach counts intervals, not just groups of consecutive odd positions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For the trace, the four intervals are the odd element alone, its extension by either even neighbour, and the interval including both neighbours. Each has a different pair of boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\ndef number_of_nice_subarrays(nums, k):\n    frequencies = defaultdict(int)\n    frequencies[0] = 1\n    total = answer = 0\n    for value in nums:\n        total += value % 2\n        answer += frequencies[total - k]\n        frequencies[total] += 1\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expected O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space for the frequency table. At most n + 1 distinct prefix odd counts occur, and the result can be quadratic in n when k is zero and all elements are even.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Seed the empty prefix or intervals beginning at index zero are lost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep counts of repeated prefix totals; even numbers cause repeats.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Query before inserting the current prefix to avoid counting an empty interval when k is zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Subtract the counts of intervals with at most k and at most k - 1 odd numbers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace parity with any Boolean predicate and count intervals containing exactly k matches.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1283":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given positive integers and a threshold, find the smallest positive divisor such that the sum of each number divided by that divisor and rounded up is at most the threshold. For \u003ccode>[1, 2, 5, 9]\u003c/code> and threshold 6, the answer is 5: the rounded quotients sum to 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The numbers are positive and the official threshold is at least the array length, ensuring that a valid divisor exists. Each rounded quotient is at least one, so a threshold below n would be impossible. Individual values can be large; checking every potential divisor can be too slow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest divisor such that the sum of the rounded-up quotients stays within a threshold\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>As the divisor grows, what happens to the sum? What does that let you do with the whole range of divisors?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Binary search on the answer (Binary search and searching the answer)\u003c/p>\u003cp>The sum never increases as the divisor grows, so \"sum &lt;= threshold\" is False...False True...True over d in [1, max(nums)]. Find the first True with the check sum((x + d - 1) // d) &lt;= threshold, which costs O(n) per probe.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log max(nums)) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try divisors in increasing order from one through the maximum input value, compute every rounded-up quotient, and return the first feasible divisor. For n numbers and maximum M, this takes O(nM) time and O(1) auxiliary space. Its simple enumeration is useful for checking small examples.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>As the divisor increases, no positive rounded-up quotient increases, so the feasibility predicate \u003ccode>sum &lt;= threshold\u003c/code> is false for an initial range and true thereafter. A divisor equal to the maximum input value M gives a sum of n and is therefore feasible. Binary search the first feasible divisor in \u003ccode>[1, M]\u003c/code>. When the middle is feasible, keep it as a possible answer and discard larger values; otherwise discard it and all smaller values. Compute each ceiling exactly as \u003ccode>(x + d - 1) // d\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, 2, 5, 9], threshold = 6; search [1, 9]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>d = 5 gives sum 5, so keep [1, 5]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">d = 3 gives sum 7, so keep [4, 5]; d = 4 gives sum 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first feasible divisor is \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The right endpoint always remains feasible, and every divisor below the left endpoint is known to be infeasible. When the endpoints meet, both facts identify the minimum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a single positive number and threshold one, the minimum divisor is the number itself. When the threshold is at least the sum of all inputs, divisor one is already feasible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def smallest_divisor(nums, threshold):\n    left, right = 1, max(nums)\n    while left &lt; right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        total = sum((value + mid - 1) // mid for value in nums)\n        if total &lt;= threshold:\n            right = mid\n        else:\n            left = mid + 1\n    return left\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each feasibility check scans n numbers and there are O(log M) checks, for O(n log M) time. The generator and search variables use O(1) auxiliary space. If M is one, there are no checks and the known feasible endpoint is returned.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Round each quotient up before summing, not the sum of ordinary quotients.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep a feasible midpoint when looking for the first true value.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The stated contract requires a nonempty array and threshold at least its length.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Stop computing a feasibility sum early once it exceeds the threshold.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the largest feasible value in a different monotone predicate by reversing the binary-search boundary choices.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2966":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Divide the array into groups of exactly three values, using every occurrence once, so that the difference between the maximum and minimum within each group is at most \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. Return any valid list of triples or an empty list if no division exists. The array length is a multiple of three. For \u003ccode>[1, 3, 4, 8, 7, 9]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 2\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[[1, 3, 4], [7, 8, 9]]\u003c/code> is invalid because the first span is 3; no valid grouping exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Numbers may repeat, and occurrences rather than distinct values must be used. Group order and the order inside each triple do not matter. The bound is inclusive and applies to every pair, equivalently to the maximum minus minimum. A large array makes enumeration of partitions impractical.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split the numbers into groups of three where every group spans at most k\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which numbers should share a group? Would putting far-apart values together ever help?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort, then group neighbours (Sorting, partitioning and selection)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort and cut into consecutive triples. If some consecutive triple spans more than k, no grouping works: any valid grouping can be rearranged into consecutive triples without increasing any group's span (an exchange argument from the smallest element upwards).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) for the output\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose three remaining occurrences for the first group, reject it if its span is too large, and recursively partition the rest. The number of partitions grows super-exponentially with the number of groups; recursion and copied remaining arrays can use O(n²) space along a path. This can still serve as an oracle for very small inputs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort the numbers and form consecutive triples. To justify this, take the three smallest remaining occurrences a, b, c in a valid partition. The valid group containing a has two other members at least as large as b and c, so \u003ccode>c - a &lt;= k\u003c/code>. Replace that group by \u003ccode>[a, b, c]\u003c/code>, exchanging any missing b or c for a displaced member x from a's group. Each such x is at least the occurrence it replaces and at most a + k. In the recipient group, if x is no larger than its old maximum, the span cannot increase; otherwise x becomes the maximum and every member is at least a, so the span is still at most k. Repeat on the remaining values. Thus failure of a consecutive triple proves impossibility.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums [1, 3, 4, 8, 7, 9], k = 3; sorted [1, 3, 4, 7, 8, 9]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first triple [1, 3, 4] spans 3 and is valid\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">second triple [7, 8, 9] spans 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>[[1, 3, 4], [7, 8, 9]]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The proof uses the smallest remaining values at each step, including duplicates. The result uses a sorted copy and does not change the supplied array.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def divide_array(nums, k):\n    values = sorted(nums)\n    answer = []\n    for i in range(0, len(values), 3):\n        if values[i + 2] - values[i] &gt; k:\n            return []\n        answer.append(values[i:i + 3])\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting costs O(n log n) time; the final pass costs O(n). The sorted copy and returned triples each use O(n) space. A failed result may still allocate the sorted copy and previously constructed groups.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A span exactly k is valid; reject only a strictly larger span.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat duplicates as separate occurrences and include every occurrence once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The code assumes length is divisible by three, as promised; an empty input produces an empty result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Partition into groups of another fixed size with the same maximum-span condition.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the minimum possible largest group span for a fixed group size.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1209":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Repeatedly remove any run of exactly \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> equal adjacent characters from a string until no such group remains. Return the final string. For \u003ccode>s = \"deeedbbcccbdaa\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>k = 3\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>\"aa\"\u003c/code>. Removing a group may bring equal letters together and trigger further deletions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The official input uses lowercase letters and \u003ccode>k &gt;= 2\u003c/code>. A run longer than k can be reduced by repeatedly removing k characters. The string can be long, so repeatedly rebuilding it after each deletion may be costly. The order of the surviving characters must remain unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Repeatedly delete k equal adjacent letters until no such block remains\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A deletion can make the letters on either side meet. What do you need to remember about what you have kept so far?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Stack of runs (Linked lists, stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a stack of [letter, run length] for the output so far. A letter equal to the top's letter increments its count, and the pair is popped when the count reaches k, which exposes the previous run exactly as a cascade needs; otherwise push [letter, 1]. Join the runs at the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Search the whole current string for a block of k equal neighbours; delete the first one found by slicing and concatenation, then start again. There can be O(n/k) deletions, each requiring O(n) scanning and copying, so a straightforward implementation takes O(n²) time and O(n) space. It provides a direct small-input simulation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process the characters from left to right, keeping the fully reduced prefix as a stack of character and run-length pairs. If the next character matches the top run, increment that run; otherwise start a new one. When the top count reaches k, remove that run. The exposed previous run can then merge with future characters, naturally handling cascades. At every step the stack is exactly the reduced form of the processed prefix: the only new removable group must end at the newly added character.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">s = \"abbba\", k = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>push a:1; then b counts 1, 2, 3 and is removed\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">last a joins the exposed a run to make a:2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>no count reaches 3; return \u003cmark>\"aa\"\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A run is removed immediately on reaching k, so a stored count is always below k. Reconstruct the result once after the scan rather than repeatedly concatenating in the loop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_duplicates(s, k):\n    stack = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if stack and stack[-1][0] == ch:\n            stack[-1][1] += 1\n            if stack[-1][1] == k:\n                stack.pop()\n        else:\n            stack.append([ch, 1])\n    return ''.join(ch * count for ch, count in stack)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each character causes constant stack work and the final join emits at most n characters, giving O(n) time. The stack and output use O(n) space in the worst case. The temporary strings built for the join contain O(n) characters in total.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Remove a run as soon as its count reaches k, then allow future input to meet the exposed run.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not use repeated immutable string concatenation inside the main scan.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A string shorter than k remains unchanged; a run of 2k identical characters disappears completely.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For k = 2, use a character stack and cancel matching adjacent pairs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return both the reduced string and the number of removed groups.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:572":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given two nonempty binary trees, decide whether the second appears as a complete subtree of the first. A match starts at a node and includes every descendant, including the absence of children; matching only some values or branches is not enough. For example, a single node 4 matches a leaf 4 but not a node 4 with a child.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The larger input has at most 2,000 nodes and the candidate has at most 1,000. Values range from -10,000 to 10,000 and may repeat. Either shape can be skewed, so tree height can be linear in node count. The function below accepts \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> for an empty tree as well, interpreting an empty candidate as a subtree of any tree.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does one tree appear inside another as some node together with all its descendants?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split it into two questions: where could the copy start, and how do you check that two trees are identical?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Tree recursion with an equality check (Binary trees and tree recursion)\u003c/p>\u003cp>For every node of the large tree, run an \"identical trees\" check against the small one (both recursive). That is O(mn) in the worst case; serialising both trees with markers for missing children and searching for one string inside the other (KMP) makes it O(m + n).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m·n) time simply, O(m + n) with serialisation; O(height) recursion space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At every node, compare the whole candidate with the subtree rooted there. Compare values and recursively compare both corresponding children, treating one missing child as a mismatch. With many repeated values, almost every comparison can examine most of the candidate, so this takes O(mn) time in the worst case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Encode each tree in preorder as a sequence of typed tokens, explicitly representing null children. A value token is distinct from a null token and from every other value, so neither a multi-digit value nor a missing branch can produce an accidental partial match. This encoding is self-delimiting: a value begins exactly two encoded child trees, while a null completes one child. Therefore an occurrence of the candidate sequence starts at a real node and describes exactly that node's subtree.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Search the candidate tokens in the larger sequence using the prefix-function form of KMP. On a mismatch, the prefix table tells us the longest prefix that might still match, without revisiting earlier text tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Main tree has root 3, left leaf 4, right leaf 5; candidate is leaf 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>main tokens: 3, 4, null, null, 5, null, null\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Candidate tokens are 4, null, null\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the first main token does not match\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The next three main tokens match in order\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Serialisation uses an explicit stack rather than recursion, so a long chain does not exceed Python's recursion limit. The prefix table and search each advance or fall back only a linear number of times.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, left, right\n\n\ndef is_subtree(root, sub_root):\n    if sub_root is None:\n        return True\n\n    def tokens(node):\n        result = []\n        stack = [node]\n        while stack:\n            current = stack.pop()\n            if current is None:\n                result.append((0,))\n            else:\n                result.append((1, current.val))\n                stack.append(current.right)\n                stack.append(current.left)\n        return result\n\n    text, pattern = tokens(root), tokens(sub_root)\n    prefix = [0] * len(pattern)\n    j = 0\n    for i in range(1, len(pattern)):\n        while j and pattern[i] != pattern[j]:\n            j = prefix[j - 1]\n        if pattern[i] == pattern[j]:\n            j += 1\n        prefix[i] = j\n    j = 0\n    for token in text:\n        while j and token != pattern[j]:\n            j = prefix[j - 1]\n        if token == pattern[j]:\n            j += 1\n        if j == len(pattern):\n            return True\n    return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For m and n nodes, serialisation and KMP take O(m + n) time and O(m + n) auxiliary space for the token sequences, table and traversal stack. Each binary tree contributes one null token per missing child, so token count is linear.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Values alone cannot distinguish a left child from a right child. Keep null markers and token boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A leaf cannot match a node that has extra descendants. Repeated values are allowed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The empty-candidate convention is explicit; both original inputs are nonempty.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the matching root rather than a boolean by retaining token-to-node positions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Check exact tree equality by comparing the two complete serialisations.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:692":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a list of words and an integer k, return the k distinct words that appear most often. Order results by decreasing frequency and break equal-frequency ties in ascending alphabetical order. For \u003ccode>[\"tea\", \"eat\", \"tea\", \"eat\", \"ice\"]\u003c/code> and k = 2, the answer is \u003ccode>[\"eat\", \"tea\"]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 500 words, each containing one to ten lowercase English letters. The supplied k is between one and the number of distinct words. Repeated words contribute separately to frequency; the output contains each selected word only once. The ranking order is part of the answer, not merely a set of winners. A follow-up asks for a solution whose selection cost depends logarithmically on k.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The k most frequent words, highest count first, ties in alphabetical order\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can you express the whole ranking rule as one sort key?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Counting plus a size-k heap with a composite key (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Count with a Counter, then rank by the key (-count, word). sorted(...)[:k] costs O(u log u) for u distinct words; heapq.nsmallest(k, counts, key=lambda w: (-counts[w], w)) costs O(u log k), the follow-up's target.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + u log k) time, O(u) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the frequency of each distinct word by repeatedly scanning the full input, then sort all distinct words using their counts and spelling. This is straightforward, but the repeated scans cost O(nu) for n words and u distinct words. Even if counts are built efficiently, sorting all u candidates costs O(u log u), regardless of whether k is small.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count in one pass. A word's complete ranking key is \u003ccode>(-frequency, word)\u003c/code>: smaller keys are better because a larger count becomes a smaller negative number, and equal counts then use normal alphabetical order. Select the k smallest keys with a bounded heap rather than ordering every distinct candidate. Finally, the library returns the selected words in key order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The two parts of the key are essential. A heap that keeps only counts might retain an arbitrary word at the frequency boundary, and sorting the chosen words afterward cannot recover a better word already discarded. The composite ranking decides both membership and output order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Count tea twice, eat twice, and ice once\u003c/p>\u003cpre>keys: tea (-2, tea), eat (-2, eat), ice (-1, ice)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Select the two smallest keys\u003c/p>\u003cpre>eat precedes tea because their counts tie\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Return the selected words in ranking order\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>[eat, tea]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>heapq.nsmallest\u003c/code> maintains a bounded selection heap for k smaller than the candidate count. When k covers every candidate, its full-sort shortcut has the same asymptotic bound expressed in terms of k.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\nfrom heapq import nsmallest\n\n\ndef top_k_frequent(words, k):\n    counts = Counter(words)\n    return nsmallest(k, counts, key=lambda word: (-counts[word], word))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let n be the number of words and u their distinct count. Counting costs O(n), and selection plus output ordering costs O(u log(k + 1)) comparisons. Counts and the selection structure occupy O(u) auxiliary space. Comparing words also costs up to the word length; the standard length bound is constant.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse only the frequency, not the alphabetical tie-breaker.\u003c/li>\u003cli>k can equal u, in which case every distinct word must be returned in rank order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A tie at the selection boundary still needs alphabetical resolution.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return frequencies alongside the chosen words using the same counter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If all distinct words are required, sort all keys directly instead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1288":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given intervals \u003ccode>[left, right]\u003c/code>, count those not covered by another interval. Interval \u003ccode>[a, b]\u003c/code> is covered by \u003ccode>[c, d]\u003c/code> when \u003ccode>c &lt;= a\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>b &lt;= d\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>[[1, 4], [2, 3], [3, 6]]\u003c/code>, the middle interval is covered, so the answer is 2. Intervals are compared as intervals, not as individual points.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 1,000 intervals; endpoints are nonnegative integers up to 100,000, each left endpoint is strictly less than its right endpoint, and the original intervals are unique. Equal starts and equal ends are possible. A covering interval may have the same start or the same end, so both inequalities are inclusive. The input need not arrive in any useful order.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the intervals that are not contained in another interval\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In which order should you visit the intervals so that any interval that covers another is seen before it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sort, then sweep (Intervals and sweep line)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by start ascending and end descending; then an interval is covered exactly when its end is at most the largest end seen so far. Count the intervals that raise that maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each interval, compare it with every other interval and mark it covered if both endpoint inequalities hold. This directly implements the definition and costs O(n²) time. It uses O(1) additional space if the count is maintained directly, but repeats comparisons among intervals whose relative positions already rule out coverage.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort by increasing left endpoint, breaking ties by decreasing right endpoint. A possible covering interval then appears before the interval it covers. For an equal start, the longer interval must come first; otherwise a shorter interval would be incorrectly counted before its cover appears.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Maintain the largest right endpoint among intervals already processed. The current interval is covered exactly when its right endpoint is at most that maximum: some earlier interval attains the maximum and starts no later. Otherwise no earlier interval covers it, and no later interval can cover it because later starts are larger, or equal starts have no larger end. Count it and update the maximum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort [1,4], [2,3], [3,6]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>largest end initially absent\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit [1,4] and then [2,3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first is kept; 3 is at most 4, so the second is covered\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit [3,6]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>6 exceeds 4, so the count becomes \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The stored maximum includes covered intervals too, although a covered interval cannot increase it. Thus the same invariant holds after every processed prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_covered_intervals(intervals):\n    ordered = sorted(intervals, key=lambda interval: (interval[0], -interval[1]))\n    largest_end = float('-inf')\n    count = 0\n    for left, right in ordered:\n        if right &gt; largest_end:\n            count += 1\n            largest_end = right\n    return count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting takes O(n log n) time, and the scan takes O(n). This non-mutating version allocates O(n) space for the sorted copy. It also leaves the caller's interval order intact. An in-place sort avoids that copy, though Python's sorting implementation can still use O(n) temporary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Ascending ends for equal starts produce a false survivor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Equality of right endpoints still means coverage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One interval survives; the implementation also returns zero for an empty list.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the surviving intervals instead of just their count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If duplicates are allowed and coverage requires a distinct interval, decide explicitly whether to remove all copies.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:103":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the values of a binary tree grouped by depth, starting at the root. Report the first level from left to right, the next from right to left, and continue alternating. For a root 1 with children 2 and 3 and grandchildren 4 and 5 under 2, the output is \u003ccode>[[1], [3, 2], [4, 5]]\u003c/code>. An empty tree produces an empty list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The tree contains up to 2,000 nodes, with integer values between -100 and 100. Values may repeat and are not identifiers. The shape can be uneven or a long chain. A level consists of every node at the same depth, even when parents are missing children. Only the order of reported values alternates; the definition of a child or a depth does not change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List the tree's values level by level, alternating left to right and right to left\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Visit the tree one level at a time. What changes between consecutive levels, and what does not?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Breadth-first traversal by levels (Binary trees and tree recursion)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Plain level-order traversal with a queue, one level per loop iteration; reverse every second level's list (or fill it with appendleft on a deque). The direction never changes which nodes form a level, only the order you report them in.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(width) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each depth, traverse the tree again and collect nodes at that depth, reversing the list on alternating levels. This correctly groups nodes but revisits upper levels repeatedly. On a chain of n nodes, there are n levels and the total work is O(n²); even a balanced tree repeats work across its height.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a queue of nodes awaiting processing. At the start of each iteration, its current length is exactly the number of nodes at the next depth. Remove exactly that many nodes, collecting their values while appending their existing children in normal left-to-right order. Their children form the next level, not part of the one currently being collected.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Reverse the completed value list on every other level. The queue stays in natural order regardless of output direction, avoiding complicated changes to the child insertion order. Each node enters and leaves the queue once, and each value is reversed at most once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Queue starts with root 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>report [1], enqueue 2 then 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process exactly two nodes at depth 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values [2,3] reverse to [3,2]; enqueue 4 then 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process the next two nodes in natural order\u003c/p>\u003cpre>output \u003cmark>[[1], [3,2], [4,5]]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The fixed level length is crucial: otherwise children appended during the iteration would be consumed prematurely. Alternating a boolean after finishing a whole level makes the root level left to right.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\nclass TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, left, right\n\n\ndef zigzag_level_order(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n    queue = deque([root])\n    answer = []\n    left_to_right = True\n    while queue:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):\n            node = queue.popleft()\n            level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left:\n                queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right:\n                queue.append(node.right)\n        if not left_to_right:\n            level.reverse()\n        answer.append(level)\n        left_to_right = not left_to_right\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each node is enqueued and removed once, and reversals touch at most n values in total, so time is O(n). The queue holds at most two adjacent levels, O(w) space for maximum width w; output storage is O(n). A chain needs only a small queue even though it has n levels.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Toggle direction per level, not per node.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Take the queue length before processing that level.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty root returns \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>; repeated values must all remain in the output.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Report ordinary level order by omitting the reversal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Produce a flat alternating sequence by extending a single result with each completed level.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1029":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are \u003ccode>2n\u003c/code> people and two cities. Person i has a cost to fly to each city. Send exactly n people to each city and minimize total cost. For \u003ccode>[[10,20], [30,200], [400,50], [30,20]]\u003c/code>, sending the first two to A and the other two to B costs 110. Choosing each person's cheaper city independently may violate the required quota.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original input has an even number of people, between 2 and 100, and each positive cost is at most 1,000. Costs and cost differences can tie; multiple assignments may therefore achieve the same minimum. Both quotas are exact, and every person must be assigned once. We return only the cost, not the chosen assignments.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Send exactly half of 2n people to each of two cities at minimum total cost\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Suppose everyone starts in city B. What does moving one person to city A cost or save?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Greedy by cost difference after sorting (Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Starting with everyone in B, moving person i to A changes the total by a_i - b_i. Choose the n people with the smallest a_i - b_i: sort by that difference and send the first half to A. Any other choice trades a smaller difference for a larger one.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every subset of n people for city A and assign everyone else to B. Compute each assignment's cost and take the minimum. This is correct because every feasible assignment corresponds to one such subset, but there are \u003ccode>C(2n, n)\u003c/code> choices, which grows exponentially. Merely choosing the lowest absolute A costs is not sufficient because the alternative B costs matter too.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Imagine everyone assigned to B first. Moving person i to A changes the total by \u003ccode>A_i - B_i\u003c/code>; this difference can be negative, zero, or positive. Exactly n people must move. Therefore select the n smallest differences and add those changes to the baseline. Sorting differences places those people first.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For an exchange proof, consider any feasible selection containing a larger difference while leaving out a smaller one. Swapping the two preserves both city quotas and does not increase cost. Repeating such swaps reaches the selection of the n smallest differences. Ties are harmless because exchanging equal differences leaves the total unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Assign everyone to B\u003c/p>\u003cpre>baseline is 20 + 200 + 50 + 20 = 290\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Differences A minus B are -10, -170, 350, 10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>choose the two smallest: -170 and -10\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Apply those changes to the baseline\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>110\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The algorithm sorts a new list of numeric differences, so the input remains untouched. Negative differences are savings, but the exact quota still requires selecting n people even if fewer than n moves save money.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def two_city_cost(costs):\n    baseline = sum(b for a, b in costs)\n    changes = sorted(a - b for a, b in costs)\n    return baseline + sum(changes[:len(costs) // 2])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Computing the baseline and differences is O(n), and sorting 2n differences takes O(n log n) time. The list of differences, slice, and Python's sorting workspace use O(n) auxiliary space. The cost does not depend on the magnitude of the prices.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sort by the difference, not by either city cost alone.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Select exactly half, even when all differences are positive.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Equal costs and equal differences may yield many optimal assignments.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the chosen people as well by sorting indexed differences.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the quotas differ, select exactly the required number for A rather than half.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1233":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a collection of absolute folder paths, remove every path that is a subfolder of another path in the collection. Return the remaining paths in any order. For \u003ccode>[\"/a\", \"/a/b\", \"/ab\", \"/c/d\"]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[\"/a\", \"/ab\", \"/c/d\"]\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>/a/b\u003c/code> is below \u003ccode>/a\u003c/code>, while \u003ccode>/ab\u003c/code> is a different folder rather than a child of \u003ccode>/a\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 40,000 distinct paths, each of length at most 100. Paths start with a slash and contain lowercase folder names separated by single slashes; no input is the root path itself. A descendant relationship must cross a slash boundary after the parent path. A path's parent need not itself appear in the input, so it must be retained unless some listed ancestor covers it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Remove every folder path that lies inside another listed folder\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When is \"/a/b\" inside \"/a\", and why is \"/ab\" not? In sorted order, where does a sub-folder sit relative to its parent?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix structure: sorted prefix checks or a trie of path parts (Tries)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every sub-folder of p starts with p + \"/\", and since \"/\" sorts before every letter, all of them come right after p in sorted order. So sort, and keep a path unless it starts with the last kept path + \"/\". A trie of path components that stops at nodes ending a folder works too.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(nL log n) time to sort n paths of length L, O(nL) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each path, check every other path as a possible ancestor. A path is removable if it starts with the other path followed immediately by \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>. This exactly implements the component-boundary rule but may perform O(n²) prefix comparisons. Each comparison can inspect up to L characters, giving O(n²L) time for maximum path length L.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort the paths lexicographically. A path precedes its descendants, because it is a prefix of each. All strings beginning with \u003ccode>parent + \"/\"\u003c/code> form one contiguous block, and \u003ccode>/\u003c/code> sorts before the lowercase letters that might extend the final folder name. Consequently that block follows the parent before paths like \u003ccode>/ab\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan the sorted list, retaining a path only when it is not below the most recently retained one. If a parent was discarded because it was itself a descendant, its retained ancestor also covers the child's entire descendant block. Once another non-descendant is retained, no later path can return to the previous ancestor's contiguous block. Thus one retained path is enough for the check.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort /a, /a/b, /ab, /c/d\u003c/p>\u003cpre>keep /a first\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">/a/b begins with /a plus a slash\u003c/p>\u003cpre>discard it; /ab does not, so keep /ab\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">/c/d is not under /ab\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>[/a, /ab, /c/d]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The precise character alphabet matters to the one-previous-path argument. For arbitrary path characters or a different collation, use a component trie or explicitly verify the ordering property rather than assuming it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def remove_subfolders(folders):\n    answer = []\n    for path in sorted(folders):\n        if not answer or not path.startswith(answer[-1] + '/'):\n            answer.append(path)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting costs O(n log n) string comparisons, each up to O(L), and the scan makes O(n) prefix checks of length at most L. Time is O(nL log n); the sorted copy and answer require O(n) references, plus O(L) for a temporary prefix. The original strings are not copied.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Test \u003ccode>parent + \"/\"\u003c/code>, not merely \u003ccode>parent\u003c/code>; \u003ccode>/a\u003c/code> does not cover \u003ccode>/ab\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A discarded intermediate folder can still have descendants.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A lone path is retained, even if its own parent is not listed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use a trie of path components and stop exploring below any listed folder.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return removed paths as well, remembering the ancestor responsible for each.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1382":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Rebuild a binary search tree so it has the same values but is height-balanced: at every node, the heights of its two child subtrees differ by at most one. Return the new root. A chain containing 1, 2, 3 can become root 2 with children 1 and 3. Any correctly balanced tree with the same values is acceptable.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original tree has between 1 and 10,000 nodes with distinct positive values up to 100,000. It obeys the binary search tree ordering, but it may be a chain of linear height. The result may use new nodes; it must not omit values or merely balance the root while leaving a lower subtree unbalanced.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Rebuild a binary search tree so that it is height-balanced, with the same values\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which traversal lists the values in sorted order? Which value of a sorted list should become the root?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> In-order to a sorted list, then build from the middle (Binary search trees and ordered maps)\u003c/p>\u003cp>In-order traversal yields the sorted values; build recursively with the middle element as the root and the two halves as its subtrees. At every node the halves differ in size by at most one, so the tree is balanced.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try possible tree shapes with the same values and check both ordering and height balance at every node. The number of ordered binary-tree shapes grows exponentially, so exhaustive construction is infeasible. Alternatively, inserting values in the original order into a fresh ordinary search tree can simply reproduce the original skew and provides no balance guarantee.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An in-order traversal of a search tree lists its values in increasing order. After collecting this list, choose a middle element as the root, recursively use the left half for its left subtree and the right half for its right subtree. All left values are smaller and all right values are larger, so the search-tree property holds at each node.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The two slices have sizes differing by at most one. Applying the same construction within each slice yields child heights differing by at most one, and induction gives balance at every node. Using index boundaries avoids copying sublists. Iterative in-order traversal handles a severely skewed input, while the construction recursion has logarithmic depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Input search tree is a chain 1 → 2 → 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>in-order values are [1,2,3]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Choose the middle value 2 as the new root\u003c/p>\u003cpre>left range contains 1; right range contains 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build each one-element range as a leaf\u003c/p>\u003cpre>balanced tree has root \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark> and children 1 and 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The snippets use \u003ccode>TreeNode\u003c/code>, defined below, with the conventional \u003ccode>val\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> attributes. They work with any input node exposing those same attributes; output nodes are instances of this class.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):\n        self.val, self.left, self.right = val, left, right\n\n\ndef balance_bst(root):\n    values, stack = [], []\n    node = root\n    while node or stack:\n        while node:\n            stack.append(node)\n            node = node.left\n        node = stack.pop()\n        values.append(node.val)\n        node = node.right\n\n    def build(lo, hi):\n        if lo &gt;= hi:\n            return None\n        mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n        return TreeNode(values[mid], build(lo, mid), build(mid + 1, hi))\n\n    return build(0, len(values))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traversal and construction visit each of n nodes once, for O(n) time. The values list and traversal stack require O(n) auxiliary space in the worst case; construction recursion is O(log n) deep. The new output tree itself uses O(n) nodes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Choosing only a new root without rebuilding both subtrees does not guarantee balance.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Avoid recursive traversal of the potentially skewed input.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A single node remains a valid balanced tree; this implementation also accepts an empty input.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reuse existing nodes collected in order rather than allocate new nodes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a sorted array instead of a tree, begin directly with the middle-element construction.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1353":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each event is available on every integer day in its inclusive interval \u003ccode>[start, end]\u003c/code>. Attend at most one event per day, with each event attended at most once. Return the largest possible number attended. For \u003ccode>[[1,2], [2,3], [3,4]]\u003c/code>, three events can be attended on days 1, 2, and 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 100,000 events, with \u003ccode>1 &lt;= start &lt;= end &lt;= 100,000\u003c/code>. Intervals can overlap, be identical, or last only one day. Attending an event consumes one day, not its entire interval. An event ending today is still available today. Large inputs make checking every remaining event every day too slow.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Attend as many events as possible, at most one per day, each on some day inside its range\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Go day by day. Among the events you could attend today, which one is the most urgent?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sweep the days with a min-heap of end days (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by start; sweep the days, pushing the end day of every event that starts today onto a min-heap, discarding ends already past, and attending the event with the earliest end. Taking the soonest-ending event is safe: any schedule that uses a later-ending one today can swap them.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O((n + D) log n) time for D days, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try assigning each event either to one available day or to no day, rejecting assignments that reuse a day. Choose the largest valid assignment. This enumerates all possibilities and is correct but can grow exponentially in the number of events and available days. Another direct approach scans every event at each day to find an eligible one, costing O(nD) over D days even when the choice is correct.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process events by their start day and keep the end days of currently available, unattended events in a min-heap. On each day, remove expired events and attend the event with the earliest end. If no event is available, jump to the next start day rather than scanning an empty gap.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The choice is safe by exchange. If an optimal schedule attends a later-ending event today, swap it with the earliest-ending event: the latter is available today, and the later-ending event remains valid on the earliest event's former day. If the earliest event was not scheduled, replace today's event with it. If today was idle, move that event to today, or add it if it was unscheduled. In all cases the count does not decrease. Repeating this argument fixes the greedy choice without reducing an optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Day 1: add event [1,2]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attend it; count is 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Day 2: add [2,3]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>attend it; count is 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Day 3: add [3,4] and attend it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>When the heap empties, advancing to the next start avoids a dependency on the largest day number. Removing all ends strictly less than today preserves events whose final eligible day is today.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\n\ndef max_events(events):\n    ordered = sorted(events)\n    heap = []\n    i = count = day = 0\n    while i &lt; len(ordered) or heap:\n        if not heap and i &lt; len(ordered):\n            day = max(day, ordered[i][0])\n        while i &lt; len(ordered) and ordered[i][0] &lt;= day:\n            heapq.heappush(heap, ordered[i][1])\n            i += 1\n        while heap and heap[0] &lt; day:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if heap:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n            count += 1\n            day += 1\n    return count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting takes O(n log n), and each event enters and leaves the heap once, also O(n log n) total. Empty gaps are jumped, and every other iteration removes or attends an event, so the sweep adds O(n) iterations. The ordered copy and heap require O(n) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Remove only events with end strictly less than the current day.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add every event that has already started before selecting one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An empty list returns zero; repeated intervals are separate events.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Record the chosen event IDs and days by storing \u003ccode>(end, id)\u003c/code> in the heap.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If an event consumes its entire duration rather than one day, this greedy argument no longer applies.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:695":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the largest area of an island in a binary grid. An island is a maximal group of land cells connected horizontally or vertically, and its area is the number of cells in that group. Diagonal contact alone does not connect islands. For \u003ccode>[[1,0,1], [1,0,1]]\u003c/code>, the answer is 2; if there is no land, the answer is zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The grid has between 1 and 50 rows and columns, and each cell is either zero for water or one for land. An island can touch an edge or fill the whole grid. The input may contain several islands of the same size. Cells are identified by their row and column, not by their values. The function should leave the input unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Size of the largest group of land cells connected up, down, left or right\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Start from an unvisited land cell and walk to everything connected to it. Can the walk report how many cells it covered?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Flood fill on a grid graph (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Flood fill (iterative DFS or BFS) from each unvisited land cell, marking cells visited and counting them; the answer is the largest count. Every cell is visited once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each land cell, independently explore all land cells reachable from it and take the maximum size found. Each exploration computes a valid island area, but every cell in an island starts the same exploration again. A grid containing one large island can therefore require O((rc)²) work for r rows and c columns.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Once one exploration has discovered an entire island, none of its cells needs to start another exploration. Scan the grid, and whenever an unvisited land cell appears, walk all its reachable land neighbours using a stack. Mark a cell when adding it to the stack, so two neighbours cannot add the same cell twice. The number removed from the stack is exactly that island's area.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every cell reached belongs to the starting island because each step crosses a valid land adjacency. Conversely, every cell in the island is reachable by a sequence of such steps and will eventually be discovered. After the walk, update the best area and continue scanning for a different island.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grid rows are [1,0,1] and [1,0,1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>start at the upper-left land cell\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Visit it and the land directly below it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first island area is 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The right column forms another area-2 island\u003c/p>\u003cpre>largest area is \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The explicit stack avoids recursion depth problems when a connected region winds through many cells. Visiting water during the outer scan has constant cost and never starts a walk.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_area_of_island(grid):\n    if not grid or not grid[0]:\n        return 0\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    seen = set()\n    best = 0\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if not grid[r][c] or (r, c) in seen:\n                continue\n            stack = [(r, c)]\n            seen.add((r, c))\n            area = 0\n            while stack:\n                x, y = stack.pop()\n                area += 1\n                for dx, dy in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n                    a, b = x + dx, y + dy\n                    if 0 &lt;= a &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= b &lt; cols and grid[a][b] and (a, b) not in seen:\n                        seen.add((a, b))\n                        stack.append((a, b))\n            best = max(best, area)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are rc cells and each land cell is pushed at most once, with four neighbour checks. Time is O(rc); the visited set and stack take O(rc) auxiliary space in the worst case. The input grid is not modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not connect diagonal cells.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark on insertion, not only on removal, to avoid duplicate work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>All water returns zero; all land returns the full grid area.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the number of islands by counting each new walk.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include diagonal neighbours if the definition explicitly permits eight-directional connectivity.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2316":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An undirected graph contains n labelled nodes and a list of edges. Count unordered pairs of distinct nodes for which no path connects the two nodes. For n = 5 and edges \u003ccode>[[0,1], [2,3]]\u003c/code>, the unreachable pairs are \u003ccode>{0,2}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{0,3}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{0,4}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{1,2}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{1,3}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{1,4}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>{2,4}\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>{3,4}\u003c/code>, giving 8. Each pair is counted once, not once in each direction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There can be up to 100,000 nodes and 200,000 edges. Nodes are numbered from zero through n minus one. Isolated nodes are valid components, and an edge connects its endpoints in both directions. The answer can exceed a 32-bit signed integer; Python integers expand as needed. Enumerating all pairs directly is too expensive when n is large.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count the pairs of nodes that have no path between them\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two nodes are unreachable from each other exactly when they lie in different groups. If you knew the group sizes, how would you count the pairs?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Connected components with union-find or DFS (Union-find and minimum spanning trees)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Find the connected components (union-find or DFS) and their sizes; pairs across components are the unreachable ones. Add size * (nodes counted so far) per component, or compute (n^2 - sum of size^2) / 2.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E α(n)) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each pair of nodes, run a graph search from one endpoint to determine whether the other can be reached. This checks the definition directly, but there are O(n²) pairs and each search can cost O(n + m) for m edges. Even computing reachability once per source still takes O(n(n + m)) time and repeats much of the same work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reachability in an undirected graph partitions nodes into connected components. Two nodes form an unreachable pair exactly when they lie in different components. Traverse each component once to find its size. Maintain the number of nodes in components already processed; a new component of size s contributes \u003ccode>s * previous\u003c/code> pairs, one for each choice of a new node and an earlier node.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Each cross-component pair is counted when the later of its two components is processed, and no within-component pair is counted. An isolated node simply has size one. This avoids needing all pairs or dividing a count by two.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Components have sizes 2, 2, 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first component contributes 2 × 0 = 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second component contributes 2 × 2 = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>four nodes have now been processed\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Last component contributes 1 × 4 = 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>total unreachable pairs \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>An explicit stack discovers each component without relying on the Python recursion limit. Marking nodes when they are pushed prevents repeated stack entries when the graph has cycles.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_unreachable_pairs(n, edges):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v in edges:\n        graph[u].append(v)\n        graph[v].append(u)\n    seen = [False] * n\n    previous = answer = 0\n    for start in range(n):\n        if seen[start]:\n            continue\n        seen[start] = True\n        stack = [start]\n        size = 0\n        while stack:\n            u = stack.pop()\n            size += 1\n            for v in graph[u]:\n                if not seen[v]:\n                    seen[v] = True\n                    stack.append(v)\n        answer += size * previous\n        previous += size\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Constructing the adjacency lists and traversing them takes O(n + m) time for m edges. The graph, visited array, and stack use O(n + m) auxiliary space. Each undirected edge is stored twice and inspected at most twice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count unordered pairs only once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Include isolated nodes as components of size one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A connected graph returns zero; an edgeless graph returns \u003ccode>n * (n - 1) // 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compute components with union-find, then combine their sizes in the same way.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count reachable pairs instead by summing \u003ccode>s * (s - 1) // 2\u003c/code> over components.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1466":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cities are connected by one-way roads whose underlying undirected graph is a tree. Reverse the fewest roads so that every city can reach city 0 following directions. For roads \u003ccode>[[0,1], [2,1], [2,3]]\u003c/code>, reversing 0→1 and 2→3 suffices, so the answer is 2. A reversal changes one road's direction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are between 2 and 50,000 cities labelled from 0 to n minus one and exactly n minus one roads. Ignoring direction, the roads form a connected tree, so each city has exactly one undirected route to city 0. Roads can initially point either way. The answer counts roads, not paths; a road may serve many cities after its direction is corrected.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Re-orient the fewest one-way roads of a tree so that every city can reach city 0\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Walk outwards from city 0 ignoring direction. Which roads point the wrong way?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Traversal with edge directions as weights (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store each road both ways, marking the original direction with cost 1 and the reverse with cost 0; traverse from 0 and add the cost of every edge you use. An original road pointing away from 0 must be flipped, one pointing towards 0 is fine, and in a tree each road is used exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every subset of roads to reverse and, for each resulting orientation, search from every city to see whether it reaches zero. The first valid subset with the fewest roads gives the answer. However, there are \u003ccode>2^(n-1)\u003c/code> subsets, and repeatedly verifying reachability adds further work. This approach ignores the unique-route structure of a tree.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Walk outward from city 0 while ignoring the original road direction. For a tree edge from a visited parent to its child, all cities below the child must cross that edge toward the parent to reach zero. Therefore an originally outward road must be reversed, whereas an originally inward road must not be reversed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store both directions of each road in an adjacency list. The original direction gets cost one when traversed outward from the root, and the reverse direction gets cost zero. Summing these costs during a traversal counts exactly the roads that need reversal. Every such reversal is necessary by uniqueness of the route, and making all of them is sufficient for every route to point toward zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start at 0: road 0→1 points outward\u003c/p>\u003cpre>reverse it; count is 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">From 1 visit 2 over original road 2→1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>this road already points toward 0; count stays 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">From 2 visit 3 over original road 2→3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>reverse it; answer \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The parent check is enough because the undirected graph is a tree. No separate visited set is needed, and an iterative stack avoids a deep recursive call chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_reorder(n, connections):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for u, v in connections:\n        graph[u].append((v, 1))\n        graph[v].append((u, 0))\n    answer = 0\n    stack = [(0, -1)]\n    while stack:\n        u, parent = stack.pop()\n        for v, cost in graph[u]:\n            if v != parent:\n                answer += cost\n                stack.append((v, u))\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building and traversing the adjacency lists takes O(n) time because a tree has n minus one roads. The graph and traversal stack use O(n) auxiliary space. Every road is inspected in both directions but contributes at most once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count edges pointing away from zero, not toward it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The parent-only traversal relies on the tree guarantee; general graphs need visited tracking and a different proof.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If all roads already point toward zero, the answer is zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the roads to reverse by storing their identifiers alongside the cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a different destination as the root and apply the same reasoning.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:851":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each person, return the index of the quietest person who is at least as rich as that person. Each pair \u003ccode>[a,b]\u003c/code> says a is strictly richer than b, and richness is transitive. Everyone is considered at least as rich as themselves. If \u003ccode>richer = [[1,0],[2,1]]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>quiet = [3,2,1]\u003c/code>, the result is \u003ccode>[2,2,2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 500 people and up to n(n-1)/2 richer pairs. The stated relations are consistent with a strict ordering and therefore contain no directed cycle. Quietness values are distinct integers from 0 through n minus one, so the quietest choice is unique. Not every pair of people has a known relationship; only relations implied by the input are usable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each person, the quietest person among those known to be at least as rich\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The richer-than relation never loops. If you knew the answer for everyone directly richer than x, could you build x's answer?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> DP over a DAG in topological order (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Direct edges from richer to poorer: the graph is acyclic. answer[x] is the quietest of x and answer[y] over everyone y directly richer than x; compute it with a memoised DFS over the richer lists, or push answers along a topological order from the richest.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each person, traverse all chains of richer people and choose the smallest quietness among those reached, including the person. This is correct but repeats searches through the same richer subgraph for many people. In the worst case it takes O(n(n + m)) time, where m is the number of stated relationships.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Direct every edge from richer to poorer. Process the resulting directed acyclic graph in topological order, starting with people who have no incoming richer edge. Initially each person's best candidate is themselves. When processing a richer person, pass their best candidate to each directly poorer neighbour if it is quieter than that neighbour's current candidate.\u003c/p>\u003cp>All incoming richer neighbours are processed before a person leaves the queue. Their propagated candidates include everyone richer along paths ending at those neighbours. Thus, when the person's indegree reaches zero, their candidate is the quietest among all known richer-or-equal people. This invariant also covers disconnected groups, each of which has its own starting nodes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Relations are 2 richer than 1, and 1 richer than 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>initial candidates [0,1,2], with 2 ready first\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process 2 and pass candidate 2 to person 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>person 1 becomes ready and passes candidate 2 to person 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>[2,2,2]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Indegrees count unprocessed incoming edges. They are decremented even if a candidate does not improve, because readiness concerns processed relationships rather than successful updates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef loud_and_rich(richer, quiet):\n    n = len(quiet)\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indegree = [0] * n\n    for rich, poor in richer:\n        graph[rich].append(poor)\n        indegree[poor] += 1\n    answer = list(range(n))\n    queue = deque(i for i in range(n) if indegree[i] == 0)\n    while queue:\n        u = queue.popleft()\n        for v in graph[u]:\n            if quiet[answer[u]] &lt; quiet[answer[v]]:\n                answer[v] = answer[u]\n            indegree[v] -= 1\n            if indegree[v] == 0:\n                queue.append(v)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The graph, indegrees, and queue require O(n + m) auxiliary space. Each node is processed once and each edge once, giving O(n + m) time. The result uses O(n) additional entries and is included in this bound.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Include each person as their own candidate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Propagate from richer to poorer; reversing the edge answers a different question.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With no relations the answer is \u003ccode>[0,1,...]\u003c/code>; cycles violate the problem's consistency guarantee.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use memoised search through directly richer neighbours when recursion depth is safe.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the quietness values instead of indices by mapping through the result.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:542":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary matrix, return a matrix of the same dimensions in which each entry is the distance to the nearest zero. Distance is the number of horizontal or vertical moves. For \u003ccode>[[0,1,1], [1,1,1]]\u003c/code>, the answer is \u003ccode>[[0,1,2], [1,2,3]]\u003c/code>. A zero has distance zero to itself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The matrix has at least one row and column, at most 10,000 cells in total, and contains at least one zero. Each cell contains either zero or one. Moves cross grid edges, not diagonals, and every cell is traversable; ones are not obstacles. Multiple zeros can be equally close, but only the minimum distance is needed. The implementation returns a new matrix without changing the input.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Distance from every cell to the nearest 0 cell\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One search per cell is far too slow. Could all the zeros start searching at the same moment?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Multi-source BFS (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Multi-source BFS: put every 0 in the queue at distance 0 and expand outwards; the first time a cell is reached gives its distance, because BFS reaches cells in order of distance from the whole set of sources.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every cell, compare its Manhattan distance to every zero and take the minimum. Because there are no obstacles, Manhattan distance is exactly the shortest number of grid moves. However, with r times c cells and a similar number of zeros, this costs O((rc)²) time. Searching independently from each cell repeats much of the same work as well.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Put all zero cells in one queue at distance zero, then expand outward one grid step at a time. This is equivalent to introducing a virtual source with zero-cost links to every zero and starting the ordinary breadth-first wave from all of them simultaneously. A cell's first discovery is its minimum distance from the entire set of zeros.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The queue processes cells in nondecreasing distance. Every newly discovered neighbour is one step farther than its predecessor, so a shorter route would have reached it in an earlier layer. Mark the distance as soon as the neighbour is enqueued; that prevents duplicate additions from different equally close paths.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Matrix is [[0,1,1], [1,1,1]]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>queue starts with the zero at (0,0), distance 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Expand distance 0, then distance 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cells (0,1) and (1,0) get 1; the next layer gets 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The farthest cell (1,2) gets 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>[[0,1,2], [1,2,3]]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The sentinel -1 denotes an undiscovered cell. The presence of at least one zero ensures that every matrix cell is eventually assigned a finite distance.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef update_matrix(mat):\n    rows, cols = len(mat), len(mat[0])\n    dist = [[-1] * cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    queue = deque()\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if mat[r][c] == 0:\n                dist[r][c] = 0\n                queue.append((r, c))\n    while queue:\n        r, c = queue.popleft()\n        for dr, dc in ((1, 0), (-1, 0), (0, 1), (0, -1)):\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; rows and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; cols and dist[nr][nc] == -1:\n                dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + 1\n                queue.append((nr, nc))\n    return dist\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each cell is queued at most once and examines four neighbours, so time is O(rc). The distance output and queue use O(rc) space. A matrix containing only zeros is already fully seeded and returns all zeros.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Seed every zero before expanding; processing zeros one at a time is not the same search.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark when enqueuing, not after removal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not treat ones as blocked cells. The input guarantee excludes a matrix without zeros.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>With obstacles, keep the same multi-source search but skip blocked neighbours.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Record a parent when discovering each cell to recover a shortest route to a zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1786":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In an undirected weighted graph, define \u003ccode>distance(u)\u003c/code> as the shortest distance from u to node n. Count paths from node 1 to node n for which this distance strictly decreases at every step. Return the count modulo 1,000,000,007. For edges 1–2 weight 1, 2–3 weight 1, and 1–3 weight 4, both 1–2–3 and 1–3 qualify.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 20,000 nodes and 40,000 undirected edges, with positive integer weights at most 100,000. The original graph is connected, and nodes are numbered from 1 through n. A qualifying path need not itself be a shortest path: it is the shortest distance to n at each vertex that must decrease. Equal distances cannot be joined by an allowed step.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count paths from 1 to n along which the shortest distance to n strictly decreases at every step\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Work out the distances to n first. Once you have them, what kind of graph do the allowed moves form?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Dijkstra, then DP in distance order (Weighted shortest paths)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Dijkstra from n gives dist; an allowed move goes from u to a neighbour v with dist[v] &lt; dist[u], so moves can never cycle. Count paths with DP in increasing order of dist: ways[n] = 1 and ways[u] is the sum of ways[v] over allowed neighbours, modulo 10^9 + 7.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(E log V) time, O(V + E) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First compute all distances to n, then enumerate simple paths from 1 to n and test the strict-decrease condition. This directly counts qualifying paths but can take exponential time because a graph can contain exponentially many simple paths. Recomputing shortest distances separately for each path would add even more repeated work.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use Dijkstra's algorithm from n to find all shortest distances. Now orient every usable step from a larger distance to a smaller one. Strict decrease forbids cycles, so the allowed steps form a directed acyclic graph. Let \u003ccode>ways[n] = 1\u003c/code>, representing the empty suffix at the destination. For every other node, sum \u003ccode>ways[v]\u003c/code> over neighbours v with smaller distance.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compute this recurrence in increasing order of distance. Every required neighbour has already been processed, while equal-distance neighbours are deliberately ignored. The recurrence counts each qualifying path once by its first step; different first neighbours give disjoint path sets. Reduce each total modulo the required modulus.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Distances to node 3 are: d[3]=0, d[2]=1, d[1]=2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ways[3] = 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 2 has smaller-distance neighbour 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ways[2] = 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Node 1 can step to 2 or directly to 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>ways[1] = 1 + 1 = \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The direct edge of weight 4 is allowed even though it is not on a shortest path. Dijkstra computes distances, but the path-counting step tests only the strict comparison between endpoint distances.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\n\ndef count_restricted_paths(n, edges):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for u, v, weight in edges:\n        graph[u].append((v, weight))\n        graph[v].append((u, weight))\n    dist = [float('inf')] * (n + 1)\n    dist[n] = 0\n    heap = [(0, n)]\n    while heap:\n        cost, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if cost != dist[u]:\n            continue\n        for v, weight in graph[u]:\n            candidate = cost + weight\n            if candidate &lt; dist[v]:\n                dist[v] = candidate\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (candidate, v))\n    ways = [0] * (n + 1)\n    ways[n] = 1\n    for u in sorted(range(1, n + 1), key=lambda x: dist[x]):\n        if u != n:\n            ways[u] = sum(ways[v] for v, _ in graph[u] if dist[v] &lt; dist[u]) % 1_000_000_007\n    return ways[1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With n vertices and m edges, lazy Dijkstra costs O(n + m log(m + 1)); sorting nodes costs O(n log n), and the DP scans all adjacency entries in O(m). Space is O(n + m), including the graph and heap. Python's integers avoid distance overflow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A restricted path is not necessarily a shortest path.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use strict inequality; equal distances cannot be traversed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Start the destination with one empty suffix, not zero; ignore stale heap entries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count paths from every starting node by returning the \u003ccode>ways\u003c/code> array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace path counts with another DAG aggregate, such as a minimum number of allowed steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:909":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A numbered snakes-and-ladders board has n² squares. Starting on square 1, roll a die to move one through six squares without passing n². If the landing square has a snake or ladder, move immediately to its destination once. Return the fewest rolls needed to reach n², or -1 if impossible. A destination that itself has a snake or ladder is not followed again on the same roll.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The board is an n by n array with \u003ccode>2 &lt;= n &lt;= 20\u003c/code>. Square numbering begins at the bottom-left, runs across the bottom row, and alternates direction on successive rows upward. Each entry is -1 or a destination square number; the first and last squares have no jump. Jumps may go backward and create cycles, so revisiting positions needs care. Only the dice roll counts as a move.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest dice rolls to finish a snakes and ladders board\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every roll costs the same. What are the states, and what are the up to six moves from each?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> BFS on an implicit graph (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>BFS over squares 1..n^2: from square s try s + 1 to s + 6, follow a snake or ladder if one starts there (once only), and record the roll count when a square is first reached. The fiddly part is converting a square number to (row, col), with rows numbered from the bottom and alternating direction.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^2) time and space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all sequences of die outcomes and simulate each until reaching the finish. Without a length bound this can loop forever on boards with backward jumps, and even bounded sequences branch up to six ways per roll. Checking all sequences through depth d takes O(6^d) time before accounting for repeated board positions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Model each square reached after any single landing jump as a state. A roll gives up to six equally costly transitions, each followed by at most one jump. Breadth-first search from square 1 discovers each state in order of increasing roll count, so the first discovery of the final square is optimal. A visited set eliminates cycles and repeated states.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To decode square s, let \u003ccode>(row_from_bottom, offset) = divmod(s - 1, n)\u003c/code>. The array row is \u003ccode>n - 1 - row_from_bottom\u003c/code>. The column is offset on an even row from the bottom and \u003ccode>n - 1 - offset\u003c/code> on an odd row. This handles the alternating numbering independently of the search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Use a 3×3 board with no jumps, starting at square 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>first roll can reach squares 2 through 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Square 3 can roll to square 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>the finish is reached after two rolls\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">No first roll reaches 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>minimum rolls \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Mark the destination after applying a jump, not the intermediate landing square. A jump to a different jump square ends the current move there; its own jump is not chained.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\ndef snakes_and_ladders(board):\n    n = len(board)\n    finish = n * n\n    queue = deque([(1, 0)])\n    seen = {1}\n    while queue:\n        square, rolls = queue.popleft()\n        if square == finish:\n            return rolls\n        for landing in range(square + 1, min(square + 6, finish) + 1):\n            row, offset = divmod(landing - 1, n)\n            col = offset if row % 2 == 0 else n - 1 - offset\n            jump = board[n - 1 - row][col]\n            destination = landing if jump == -1 else jump\n            if destination not in seen:\n                seen.add(destination)\n                queue.append((destination, rolls + 1))\n    return -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n² states and at most six transitions per state. Time is O(n²), and the queue and visited set use O(n²) auxiliary space. The board itself is read but not modified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Reverse column direction on alternate rows counted from the bottom.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Follow only one snake or ladder per roll, even when its destination has another.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Bound a roll by the final square and use visited states to stop cycles.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store predecessors and chosen die values to reconstruct one shortest sequence of rolls.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the die size by replacing six with the supplied maximum roll.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2492":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Roads connect cities in both directions and each road has a positive distance. The score of a route is the smallest road distance appearing on that route. Find the minimum possible score of a route from city 1 to city n. A route may revisit cities and reuse roads. For roads 1–2 of length 7, 2–4 of length 5, 1–3 of length 3, and 3–4 of length 8, with n = 4, the route 1–3–4 has score 3, which is the smallest possible score.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 100,000 cities and 100,000 roads; cities are labelled from 1 through n, and road distances are positive. The problem guarantees a route from 1 to n. Revisiting and backtracking are permitted. Only the score is requested, not the route itself.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest possible weight of the lightest road on any route from 1 to n, where roads may be reused\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Since a route may go back and forth, which roads can a route from 1 to n touch?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Connected component plus a minimum over its edges (Union-find and minimum spanning trees)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Because revisits are allowed, a route can detour through any road of the component that contains city 1 (and n lies in it). The answer is the smallest weight among those roads: find the component with union-find or BFS from 1 and take the minimum weight over its edges.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + E α(n)) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerating simple paths from 1 to n and taking their lowest score is not correct because the optimal route may need a detour and repeated vertices. A correct finite baseline for tiny graphs can explore states \u003ccode>(city, smallest_distance_seen)\u003c/code> and keep every reachable state; the number of distance states per city can grow with the number of roads. It does substantially more work than necessary.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The answer is the smallest road weight anywhere in the connected component of city 1. No route starting at 1 can use a road outside this component, so its score cannot be below that minimum. Conversely, for any road in the component, travel from 1 to one endpoint, cross the road, and then travel to n. The graph is undirected and 1 is connected to n, so these walks exist; revisits are allowed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Traverse the component from 1, inspecting each incident road and updating a running minimum. This inspects every road in that component, including branches not on a simple path to the destination. The lower bound and the constructed detour prove equality.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For n = 4, use roads 1–2 of weight 7, 2–4 of weight 5, and 2–3 of weight 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>from 1 follow the road of weight 7 to 2\nsmallest inspected weight is 7\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">From 2 inspect roads to 4 with weight 5 and to 3 with weight 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>component minimum becomes 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Walk 1→2→3→2→4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>route score \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The minimum is taken over every incident edge, even when its other endpoint was visited earlier. Visited tracking controls traversal, not whether an edge's weight is considered.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def min_score(n, roads):\n    graph = [[] for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for u, v, weight in roads:\n        graph[u].append((v, weight))\n        graph[v].append((u, weight))\n    seen = {1}\n    stack = [1]\n    answer = float('inf')\n    while stack:\n        u = stack.pop()\n        for v, weight in graph[u]:\n            answer = min(answer, weight)\n            if v not in seen:\n                seen.add(v)\n                stack.append(v)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building the graph costs O(n + m), and the traversal examines each road in the reachable component twice, for O(n + m) total time. The adjacency lists, visited set, and stack use O(n + m) auxiliary space. The connectivity guarantee ensures a finite answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The score is a minimum edge weight, not a sum or maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not restrict attention to simple routes: detours may improve the score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Roads in other connected components cannot occur on a route starting at 1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a witnessing walk by retaining traversal paths to the minimum-weight edge and to n.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If repeated vertices are forbidden, this component-minimum proof no longer suffices.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1882":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Servers have weights, and task j arrives at time j with a positive duration. Tasks wait in arrival order. Whenever a task can start, assign it to the free server with smallest weight, breaking ties by smallest server index. A task starting at time t with duration d releases its server at time t+d. Return the assigned server index for each task.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For server weights \u003ccode>[3,1]\u003c/code> and durations \u003ccode>[2,1,2]\u003c/code>, the assignment is \u003ccode>[1,0,1]\u003c/code>: server 1 handles the first task, is still busy at time 1, and is free again at time 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 200,000 servers and tasks. Weights and durations are positive integers up to 200,000. Several tasks may be waiting when servers become free, and several servers can become free simultaneously. Waiting tasks retain their original order, but assignments at the same time must still use the weight and index tie rules. A server released at time t is available to a task starting at t.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Assign queued tasks to free servers by weight and then index, waiting whenever none is free\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You keep needing two different \"best\" servers: the best free one, and the one that becomes free soonest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Two heaps for an event simulation (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a min-heap of free servers keyed (weight, index) and a min-heap of busy servers keyed (free time, weight, index). For task j at time t = max(t, j), release every busy server free by t; if none is free, jump t to the earliest free time and release those. Pop the best free server and push it as busy until t + tasks[j].\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O((n + m) log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Simulate each time unit, scan all servers for every assignment, and choose the best free server. This follows the specification but can inspect many servers repeatedly and can spend enormous time in gaps while all servers are busy. Its cost depends on the elapsed time as well as the number of tasks and servers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain two priority queues. The free heap is ordered by \u003ccode>(weight, index)\u003c/code>, the exact assignment preference. The busy heap is ordered by \u003ccode>(finish_time, weight, index)\u003c/code> so its first entry identifies the next release. For each task in arrival order, advance the current time to at least its arrival and release all servers finishing by that time. If none are free, jump to the earliest finish and release every server finishing at that instant.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Then take the best free server and insert it into the busy heap with its new finish time. Releasing all simultaneous finishers before choosing is necessary: the earliest busy heap entry alone need not have the best weight among all servers available at that time. The current time never goes backward, preserving task order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At time 0 choose server 1, weight 1, for duration 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>server 1 becomes free at time 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At time 1 server 1 is busy, so server 0 receives the next task\u003c/p>\u003cpre>server 0 also becomes free at time 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At time 2 release both and choose lower weight server 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>assignment \u003cmark>[1,0,1]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The code processes both releases before assigning the third task, so the lower-weight server wins.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\n\ndef assign_tasks(servers, tasks):\n    free = [(weight, i) for i, weight in enumerate(servers)]\n    heapq.heapify(free)\n    busy = []\n    answer = []\n    time = 0\n    for arrival, duration in enumerate(tasks):\n        time = max(time, arrival)\n        while busy and busy[0][0] &lt;= time:\n            _, weight, i = heapq.heappop(busy)\n            heapq.heappush(free, (weight, i))\n        if not free:\n            time = busy[0][0]\n            while busy and busy[0][0] &lt;= time:\n                _, weight, i = heapq.heappop(busy)\n                heapq.heappush(free, (weight, i))\n        weight, i = heapq.heappop(free)\n        answer.append(i)\n        heapq.heappush(busy, (time + duration, weight, i))\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Heap construction is O(n), and each of m tasks inserts and later releases at most one server, with O(log n) per heap operation. Total time is O(n + m log(n + 1)); auxiliary space is O(n) for the heaps, plus O(m) for the returned assignment list. Time jumps do not scan empty time units.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Release servers finishing at the current time before assigning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Release all simultaneous finishers before comparing their weights and indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not allow later tasks to jump ahead of waiting tasks.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return each task's start time as well as its assigned server.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the free-heap key to support a different explicitly specified server preference.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2092":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Person 0 initially knows a secret and tells \u003ccode>first_person\u003c/code> at time zero. Meetings are triples \u003ccode>[x,y,time]\u003c/code>. A person who knows the secret at a meeting time tells the other participant, and transmission within a time instant is immediate: knowledge may travel through any chain of meetings all happening at that time. Return all people who eventually know the secret, in any order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>n = 5\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>first_person = 1\u003c/code>, and meetings \u003ccode>[[1,2,3], [3,4,3], [2,3,3]]\u003c/code>, all five people learn it. The input ordering of meetings at time 3 must not limit that instantaneous chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 100,000 people and 100,000 meetings, with positive meeting times up to 100,000. A person may attend several meetings at exactly the same time, and the same pair can meet at different times. Initial knowledge is permanent. A connection made during a meeting when nobody in that connected group knows the secret does not transfer knowledge later unless another meeting occurs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Meetings at different times must be processed chronologically; input order is not guaranteed. The result is a collection of people, not a record of who told whom or when.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A secret spreads through meetings, and people meeting at the same instant pass it on instantly\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Group the meetings by time. Within one time, who ends up knowing? What should happen to people who met but did not learn it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Union-find with a reset per time slot (Union-find and minimum spanning trees)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Process the times in increasing order. For one time, union the people who meet; afterwards everyone connected to person 0's set knows the secret, and everyone else from those meetings is reset to a singleton so later meetings do not inherit a false link. A BFS inside each time slot works the same way.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(M log M + M α(n)) time, O(n + M) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort the meetings by time, then repeatedly scan every meeting at a given time until an entire pass spreads no new knowledge. This computes the instantaneous closure: each successful update adds a person, so the process terminates. However, an adversarial ordering of a long chain can require one new person per pass. Across a time group with k meetings, this can take O(k²) work.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Another incorrect shortcut is to join all meetings into one timeless graph. A person who learns the secret tomorrow cannot retroactively tell someone they met yesterday. Chronological processing is necessary as well as closure within each time group.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each distinct time, build an undirected graph only from meetings at that time. Seed a queue with every participant who already knows the secret before this group. Traverse that temporary graph; every reached participant learns immediately, and is added to the permanent known set. Discard the temporary graph before moving on.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The invariant before processing time t is that the known set contains exactly the people who could learn from initial information and meetings strictly before t. Within a connected component of the time-t graph, if any participant is already known, a sequence of simultaneous meetings transmits the secret to the entire component. If no participant is known, nobody in that component can start transmission. Thus the traversal produces exactly the correct known set after time t.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This formulation uses no persistent links between past meeting partners. People keep their knowledge, not the ability to communicate outside the specified meeting times. Handling every meeting in a time group before beginning the traversal prevents input order within the group from changing the answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Initially persons 0 and 1 know the secret\u003c/p>\u003cpre>time 3 meetings connect 1–2, 3–4, and 2–3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build the entire time-3 graph and seed person 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>propagation reaches 2, then 3, then 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">No meetings remain\u003c/p>\u003cpre>known people \u003cmark>[0,1,2,3,4]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>If only 3–4 met at an earlier time, that meeting would not let person 4 learn from person 3 later without a new meeting. Conversely, the chronological ordering of different groups makes information gained earlier available as a seed in a later group.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict, deque\n\n\ndef find_all_people(n, meetings, first_person):\n    known = {0, first_person}\n    ordered = sorted(meetings, key=lambda meeting: meeting[2])\n    i = 0\n    while i &lt; len(ordered):\n        time = ordered[i][2]\n        graph = defaultdict(list)\n        while i &lt; len(ordered) and ordered[i][2] == time:\n            x, y, _ = ordered[i]\n            graph[x].append(y)\n            graph[y].append(x)\n            i += 1\n        queue = deque(person for person in graph if person in known)\n        reached = set(queue)\n        while queue:\n            u = queue.popleft()\n            for v in graph[u]:\n                if v not in reached:\n                    reached.add(v)\n                    queue.append(v)\n        known.update(reached)\n    return sorted(known)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For M meetings, sorting takes O(M log(M + 1)). Across all time groups, the temporary graphs contain O(M) total edge occurrences and at most O(M) participant occurrences, so all traversals together take O(M) time. Sorting the returned people costs O(n log(n + 1)) in the worst case; it is optional because output order is unrestricted. Space is O(n + M) for the known set, sorted input, a time group's graph, and its queue.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Process all meetings at one time as a group, not as sequential opportunities in input order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not retain connectivity from a time group whose participants did not know the secret.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With no meetings, exactly persons 0 and \u003ccode>first_person\u003c/code> know; duplicate simultaneous meetings do not change the result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Mark visited participants locally even if they already know the secret, so cycles and multiple seeds are safe.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the earliest learning time by recording the current time when a person is first reached.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Process each time group with a temporary union-find, discarding its components after propagating knowledge.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If communication takes positive time instead of being instantaneous, model those delays explicitly rather than using same-time closure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:279":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a positive integer n, return the fewest perfect-square positive integers whose sum is n. Squares may be used more than once. For n = 12, the answer is 3 because \u003ccode>4 + 4 + 4 = 12\u003c/code>; taking 9 first would require four terms, \u003ccode>9 + 1 + 1 + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The original range is \u003ccode>1 &lt;= n &lt;= 10,000\u003c/code>. The square 1 is always available, so every target has a solution. A square larger than the current amount cannot be its final summand. We need the minimum number, not the number of representations or a particular representation. This implementation also defines the natural extension that zero requires zero squares.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest perfect squares that add up to n\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Suppose you knew the last square used. What problem is left, and how many different leftovers can there be?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Unbounded coin-change DP (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>best[0] = 0 and best[i] = 1 + min(best[i - s*s]) over squares s*s &lt;= i: coin change with square coins, n amounts each trying about sqrt(n) coins. (Number theory says the answer is never more than four, which allows faster special-case solutions.)\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n sqrt n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Recursively try every square not exceeding the remaining amount, and take one plus the smallest answer among the resulting remainders. This is correct by considering the final square, but many different sequences reach the same remainder and recompute it. The search tree can grow exponentially. Always taking the largest possible square is not correct, as the example 12 demonstrates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store the optimal answer for every amount from zero through n. Set \u003ccode>best[0] = 0\u003c/code>. For amount x, consider each square s² no larger than x and take \u003ccode>1 + best[x - s²]\u003c/code>; the smallest such value is optimal. Every remainder is smaller than x, so an increasing iteration order guarantees that it has already been solved.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For correctness, every candidate adds one square to a valid solution of the remainder, so it is achievable. Conversely, remove the last square from an optimal representation of x: its remaining squares form a representation of one of the tested remainders, whose optimum cannot be larger. Therefore the smallest candidate equals the true optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Set best[0]=0; fill amounts in increasing order\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best[4]=1 because 4 itself is square\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For 8, square 4 leaves amount 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best[8]=2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">For 12, square 4 leaves amount 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>best[12]=\u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>; choosing 9 leaves 3 and needs 4 terms\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Precompute squares up to n once. The inner loop can stop at the first square greater than the current amount because the square list is increasing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from math import isqrt\n\n\ndef num_squares(n):\n    squares = [i * i for i in range(1, isqrt(n) + 1)]\n    best = [0] + [n + 1] * n\n    for amount in range(1, n + 1):\n        for square in squares:\n            if square &gt; amount:\n                break\n            best[amount] = min(best[amount], 1 + best[amount - square])\n    return best[n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n amounts and at most \u003ccode>floor(sqrt(n))\u003c/code> candidate squares per amount, giving O(n sqrt(n)) time. The table uses O(n) space and the square list O(sqrt(n)), for O(n) auxiliary space total. The bound also includes the initial list construction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>The largest square first is not always optimal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reuse squares freely; the same square can occur multiple times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A perfect square needs one term; the explicit zero extension returns zero.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store the chosen square per amount to reconstruct a minimum representation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Treat sums as states in a breadth-first search, where each edge adds one square.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:40":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given candidate numbers and a target, return all distinct combinations whose sum equals the target. Each input occurrence may be used at most once, even when multiple occurrences have the same value. Combinations differing only in order are the same. For candidates \u003ccode>[1,1,2,5]\u003c/code> and target 3, the only answer is \u003ccode>[[1,2]]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 100 candidates, each between 1 and 50; the target is between 1 and 30. Values can repeat. The answer can contain many combinations, but duplicate value lists are forbidden. Positivity means that once a chosen value exceeds the remaining target, no larger value can help; it also means a completed combination need not be extended with zero-valued candidates.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List every combination that sums to the target, each element used at most once, from input with duplicates, with no repeated combinations\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two equal numbers can produce the same combination twice. At one level of the choices, when is picking a value pointless?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Backtracking with sort-and-skip deduplication (Complete search and backtracking)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort, then recurse choosing the next element from index start onwards; skip a value equal to the previous one at the same depth (i &gt; start and a[i] == a[i - 1]), and stop the loop once a[i] exceeds the remaining target, since the rest are larger. Recurse with i + 1 because each element is used once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> Exponential in the worst case (output size times n); the small target keeps it fast\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all subsets of input indices, keep those whose values sum to the target, sort each value list, and deduplicate the results. This is complete because every legal combination uses a subset of occurrences. However, there are 2^n subsets, many of which collapse to the same value combination when the input contains duplicates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort candidates and build each combination in nondecreasing order. At a recursion level, try each possible next index at most once per distinct value: if the same value was tried immediately before at that level, choosing it again would produce identical value combinations from equivalent remaining occurrences. After selecting index i, recurse from i+1, so that occurrence cannot be reused.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Equal values at deeper levels remain allowed. For example, two copies of 1 can both appear if the input contains them: the second is chosen in a different recursive call. As all numbers are positive and sorted, stop the loop when the next value exceeds the remaining target. Reaching zero records one complete combination.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort [1,1,2,5] and start with remainder 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>choose the first 1; remainder becomes 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At the next depth, a second 1 is allowed, but cannot complete the target\u003c/p>\u003cpre>choosing 2 records [1,2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">At the root skip the second 1 as a duplicate choice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>answer \u003cmark>[[1,2]]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every combination has a nondecreasing representation, and the earliest available equal value represents each choice at a given depth. Skipping later equals therefore removes duplicates without losing distinct value combinations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def combination_sum2(candidates, target):\n    values = sorted(candidates)\n    answer = []\n    path = []\n\n    def search(start, remaining):\n        if remaining == 0:\n            answer.append(path.copy())\n            return\n        for i in range(start, len(values)):\n            if i &gt; start and values[i] == values[i - 1]:\n                continue\n            if values[i] &gt; remaining:\n                break\n            path.append(values[i])\n            search(i + 1, remaining - values[i])\n            path.pop()\n\n    search(0, target)\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting costs O(n log n). The search has at most exponentially many index subsets and may copy O(n) values per result, so a safe worst-case time bound is O(n 2^n + n log n). The sorted copy, current path, and recursion use O(n) auxiliary space, excluding the potentially exponential output.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Skip equal values only at the same depth, not globally.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Advance to i+1 to use each input occurrence at most once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Copy the path when saving it; otherwise later backtracking changes saved results.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Allow unlimited reuse by recurring from i instead, while retaining same-depth deduplication.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Count combinations rather than returning them, taking care not to count duplicate value lists.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1035":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two rows contain integers. Draw lines between equal values in different rows, using each position at most once, without any two lines crossing. Return the largest possible number of lines. Positions matter even if their values repeat. For example, \u003ccode>[1, 4, 2]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4]\u003c/code> allow two lines, not three.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each row contains 1 to 500 elements, with values from 1 to 2000. Explicitly listing all line selections is too expensive. The two rows may have different lengths and can contain repeated numbers. Only the number of lines, not their actual endpoints, is requested.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Draw the most non-crossing lines joining equal numbers in two rows\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Two lines do not cross exactly when their endpoints come in the same order in both rows. What are you choosing from each row, then?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Longest common subsequence in disguise (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>A set of non-crossing lines is a common subsequence of the two arrays and vice versa, so the answer is their longest common subsequence: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1 on a match, else max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(min(m, n)) space with two rows\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all subsets of positions in the first row and all equally sized subsets in the second row, matching chosen positions in order. Check whether each aligned pair has the same value and retain the largest valid size. This explores exponentially many subsets and repeats work for identical prefixes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Noncrossing lines have endpoints in the same relative order in both rows. Their values therefore form a common subsequence, and every common subsequence gives noncrossing lines. The problem is exactly to find the largest such sequence. For two prefixes, if their last values match, an optimal sequence can include that pair: any solution not using both can replace its last occurrence with these later positions without reducing its length. Otherwise at least one of the final positions is unused, so take the best answer after removing either one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A rolling row stores answers for the previous first-row prefix. Build a fresh row for the next element: the left entry already describes the current prefix, and the previous row supplies the upper and diagonal entries. Swapping the inputs makes the stored row the shorter one. The zero row handles an empty prefix naturally.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For rows \u003ccode>[1, 4, 2]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[1, 2, 4]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The empty prefix has row [0, 0, 0, 0].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First two elements\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Processing 1 gives [0, 1, 1, 1]; processing 4 gives [0, 1, 1, 2].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Processing 2 gives [0, 1, 2, 2], so the result is 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def uncrossed_lines(a, b):\n    if len(b) &gt; len(a):\n        a, b = b, a\n    prev = [0] * (len(b) + 1)\n    for x in a:\n        row = [0] * (len(b) + 1)\n        for j, y in enumerate(b, 1):\n            row[j] = prev[j - 1] + 1 if x == y else max(prev[j], row[j - 1])\n        prev = row\n    return prev[-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For lengths m and n, the nested scan takes O(mn) time and O(min(m, n)) extra space. The input arrays are not changed. Reconstructing the actual matching pairs would require retaining additional information or recomputing portions of the table.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Matching equal values greedily from left to right can sacrifice more useful later matches. Repeated values are separate positions, not one shared item. A row with no values in common returns zero, and an all-equal input returns the shorter length. In the mismatch case both removal choices must be considered; using only the diagonal loses valid lines.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return one set of matching position pairs by retaining predecessor choices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Give each matched value a weight and maximize the total weight instead of the count.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1594":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start at the top-left of an integer grid and move only right or down to its bottom-right corner. Multiply every value on the chosen path, including both endpoints. Return the largest nonnegative path product modulo 1,000,000,007, or -1 if every path has a negative product. For \u003ccode>[[1,-2],[3,4]]\u003c/code>, the best product is 12.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 15 rows and columns, and values lie between -4 and 4. A cell can reverse the order of products when it is negative. Python integers preserve exact intermediate values, which is essential because residues do not preserve the ordering of signed products.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Largest non-negative product along a right-and-down path in a grid that contains negative numbers\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A very negative product can become the best one after one more negative cell. What do you need to keep for every cell?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Grid DP with max and min states (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>For every cell keep both the largest and the smallest product of a path reaching it; from the top and left neighbours, multiply both by the cell's value and take the max and the min. The answer is the corner's max modulo 10^9 + 7, or -1 if it is negative; take the modulus only at the end.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(n) space with one row\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate each sequence of right and down moves, multiply the cells on the resulting path, then compare full products before applying the modulus. The number of routes is a binomial coefficient, exponential in the combined grid dimensions in the worst case. Many routes share a destination cell and repeat the same continuation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At each cell preserve both the smallest and largest exact product among paths ending there. When the current value is positive, multiplying a maximum gives the best candidate; when it is negative, the minimum gives the best candidate instead. Zero maps both extremes to zero. Every path arrives from above or left, and multiplication by a fixed number attains its extremes at an endpoint of the predecessor range. Thus comparing both extremes from both available predecessors gives exact new extremes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A row of pairs suffices. Before processing a cell, its entry belongs to the previous row; its left neighbor has already been updated for the current row. The starting cell is a special base case, and inaccessible neighbors are omitted instead of inventing a zero product.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[[1,-2],[3,4]]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First row\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The reachable product pairs are (1, 1) and (-2, -2).\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second row\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The left cell has pair (3, 3). The destination candidates are -8 and 12.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Result\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The destination extrema are (-8, 12); the nonnegative maximum is 12.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_product_path(grid):\n    mod = 1_000_000_007\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    dp = [None] * cols\n    for r, row in enumerate(grid):\n        for c, value in enumerate(row):\n            if r == 0 and c == 0:\n                dp[c] = (value, value)\n                continue\n            candidates = []\n            for previous in (dp[c], dp[c - 1] if c else None):\n                if previous is not None:\n                    candidates.extend((previous[0] * value, previous[1] * value))\n            dp[c] = (min(candidates), max(candidates))\n    answer = dp[-1][1]\n    return answer % mod if answer &gt;= 0 else -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For m rows and n columns, each cell examines at most four products: O(mn) arithmetic operations and O(n) auxiliary space. Python's integer multiplication has a size-dependent cost, although the stated bounds keep these integers modest. The input grid remains unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not discard the minimum: two negatives can make a large positive. Do not reduce modulo while comparing, since that changes order and loses sign. A zero path is a valid nonnegative result, even if all other products are negative. A one-cell grid returns its value or -1 according to its sign.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store predecessor choices to recover a path attaining the best product.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Allow blocked cells and explicitly represent cells that are unreachable.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:93":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a string of decimal digits, return every valid IPv4 address obtained by inserting exactly three dots without rearranging or removing digits. An address has four decimal parts, each between 0 and 255 inclusive, with no leading zero unless the part is exactly \u003ccode>0\u003c/code>. For \u003ccode>\"1010\"\u003c/code>, the only result is \u003ccode>\"1.0.1.0\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input has 1 to 20 digits. Each part uses one to three digits, so any input longer than 12 or shorter than four has no answer. Every output has the same digits in the same order as the input, with exactly three additional dots. Return all valid addresses rather than choosing a single one.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every way to split a digit string into a valid IPv4 address\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Exactly four parts, each 1 to 3 digits with value 0 to 255 and no leading zero. How many ways can there possibly be?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Backtracking with pruning (Complete search and backtracking)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Try each part length 1, 2 or 3 recursively, rejecting parts above 255 or with a leading zero, and prune when the remaining digits cannot fill the remaining parts (fewer than one or more than three per part). There are at most 3^4 = 81 length choices.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1): at most 81 candidate splits, each checked in O(length)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every combination of three distinct cut positions in the string, create four pieces, and validate their lengths, numeric bounds, and leading zeros. There are O(n³) combinations before validation, and each candidate may scan O(n) characters. This is feasible for small input but makes many cuts that cannot form valid parts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build the address from left to right. At each position, try the next part with length one, two, or three. Every valid address has exactly one such sequence of lengths, so this search is complete and does not produce duplicates. Reject a multi-digit piece beginning with zero and any piece above 255 immediately; neither can become valid by adding more digits.\u003c/p>\u003cp>If r parts remain and d digits remain, a solution requires r &lt;= d &lt;= 3r. Check that bound before branching. Once four parts have been chosen, only an exhausted string is acceptable. Keep the chosen pieces on a path, append a joined copy on success, and undo each choice before trying another length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>\"1010\"\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First choice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Choose 1; three digits remain for three parts, so each must have length one.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Continue\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Choose 0, then 1, then 0. All parts are valid and no digits remain.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Other choices\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Longer first pieces leave too few digits for the remaining parts, so the sole address is 1.0.1.0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def restore_ip_addresses(s):\n    result = []\n    def search(pos, parts):\n        remaining = 4 - len(parts)\n        if not remaining &lt;= len(s) - pos &lt;= 3 * remaining:\n            return\n        if remaining == 0:\n            result.append('.'.join(parts))\n            return\n        for size in range(1, 4):\n            piece = s[pos:pos + size]\n            if len(piece) != size:\n                break\n            if size &gt; 1 and piece[0] == '0':\n                break\n            if int(piece) &gt; 255:\n                break\n            parts.append(piece)\n            search(pos + size, parts)\n            parts.pop()\n    search(0, [])\n    return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are at most 3⁴ length sequences and each piece has at most three characters, so time and recursion space are O(1) under the fixed IPv4 format, excluding the bounded output. The length check also rejects overlong input immediately. Output order is not important.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>0\u003c/code> is legal but \u003ccode>00\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>01\u003c/code> are not. The value 255 is allowed, but 256 is not. Exactly four parts must consume every input digit; accepting a partial address is incorrect. The recursive path must be popped after each attempt so a sibling branch does not inherit it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count valid addresses instead of returning their strings.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generalize to a specified number of parts and maximum part value.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2140":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Questions appear in a fixed order. Question i has a point reward and a brainpower value: solving it earns the reward but forces you to skip that many following questions. Skipping it earns nothing and advances by one. Return the maximum total points obtainable. For \u003ccode>[[3,2],[4,3],[4,4],[2,5]]\u003c/code>, the answer is 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are up to 100,000 questions; rewards and brainpower values are positive and can be large. A search through every combination is impractical, and recursion may exceed Python's call stack. A solved question may skip past the end, in which case there are no further questions to answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Answer questions in order; solving one earns points but skips the next brainpower questions\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A decision only affects what comes after it. From which end is it easier to start?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Take-or-skip DP from the back (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Solve from the back: dp[i] = max(dp[i + 1], points[i] + dp[i + brainpower[i] + 1]), with dp = 0 past the end. Each question is a take-or-skip choice whose consequences all lie later.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every question branch between skipping and solving, then follow the forced skips. Evaluate all legal decision sequences and retain the largest sum. The tree can have exponentially many branches, with the same suffix reached repeatedly through different earlier choices. Its recursion depth may also be linear.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>dp[i]\u003c/code> be the best reward from question i onward. Every legal plan either skips i, giving \u003ccode>dp[i+1]\u003c/code>, or solves it, giving its points plus the best suffix beginning at \u003ccode>i + brainpower + 1\u003c/code>. These choices cover all plans and do not overlap in their first decision. Taking their maximum is optimal because any better continuation could replace the one selected after that first decision.\u003c/p>\u003cp>All referenced suffixes start later, so scan backwards. A jump beyond the array contributes zero; clamping the destination to n lets a single extra zero entry represent every such jump. The answer for the entire input is the first state.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[[3,2],[4,3],[4,4],[2,5]]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Suffixes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>At the last question, taking it yields 2. At index 2, taking yields 4, beating 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Index 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Taking yields 4 and skipping also yields 4, so dp[1] is 4.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Index 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Taking gives 3 plus dp[3] = 5; skipping gives 4. Return 5.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def most_points(questions):\n    n = len(questions)\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        points, brainpower = questions[i]\n        following = min(n, i + brainpower + 1)\n        dp[i] = max(dp[i + 1], points + dp[following])\n    return dp[0]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each question is processed once with constant work, giving O(n) time and O(n) auxiliary space. Python integers support totals larger than a fixed 32-bit integer. The algorithm leaves the question list unchanged, and the sentinel also makes an empty extension return zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The solved question itself is followed by the skipped questions, so the next usable index includes the extra one. A jump past the end is valid and simply has no future reward. Do not force the last question to be skipped because its brainpower exceeds the number remaining. The largest immediate reward need not lead to the largest total.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Save the chosen branch at each index to reconstruct which questions are solved.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the score to allow negative rewards; skipping still provides a zero baseline.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:375":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A hidden integer is chosen from 1 through n. After an incorrect guess, you pay the value guessed and learn whether the hidden integer is higher or lower; a correct guess costs nothing. Return the least amount of money sufficient to guarantee finding the number regardless of the hidden choice. For n = 2, guessing 1 guarantees a cost of 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The range has 1 to 200 possible values. The opponent can choose the most expensive consistent outcome, so minimizing an average cost is not enough. The guaranteed amount must cover every possible hidden number, including either endpoint of the range.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Least money that guarantees a win at higher-or-lower guessing when a wrong guess x costs x\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You choose the guess; the side is chosen against you. What does the guaranteed cost of a range depend on?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Interval DP with a minimax step (Advanced dynamic programming; memoised recursion over ranges is enough)\u003c/p>\u003cp>cost(l, r) = min over guesses g in [l, r] of g + max(cost(l, g - 1), cost(g + 1, r)), with cost 0 on ranges of size 0 or 1. Fill by increasing range length or memoise: a minimax over sub-ranges.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^3) time, O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every possible decision tree of guesses. For each tree, evaluate the maximum cost over all hidden numbers, then select the tree with the smallest maximum. There are exponentially many trees and many repeated subintervals; choosing only a median without evaluating costs also fails because larger incorrect guesses cost more.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Define the guarantee for an interval as the minimum over first guesses of the maximum cost forced by a remaining side. Guess g either succeeds for free or fails and costs g, after which the answer lies in the lower or upper interval. For an interval containing multiple candidates, at least one side is nonempty, and the adversary can select the more costly continuation. Thus the candidate guarantee is \u003ccode>g + max(left, right)\u003c/code>. A zero- or one-element interval costs zero, because its answer is already determined.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Fill intervals by increasing length so both smaller sides are known. Any strategy has some first guess, and its remaining strategies cannot do better than the corresponding optimal subinterval costs. Conversely, picking the minimizing guess and using those optimal strategies achieves the stated guarantee, proving both bounds.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For n = 3:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Length two\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Intervals [1,2] and [2,3] cost 1 and 2, respectively.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Try 2 first\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Either remaining side contains only one value, so the worst incorrect cost is 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Guess 1 costs 1 + 2 = 3; guess 3 costs 3 + 1 = 4. The answer is 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def guaranteed_guess_cost(n):\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 2) for _ in range(n + 2)]\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for left in range(1, n - length + 2):\n            right = left + length - 1\n            dp[left][right] = min(\n                guess + max(dp[left][guess - 1], dp[guess + 1][right])\n                for guess in range(left, right + 1)\n            )\n    return dp[1][n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n²) intervals, and each considers O(n) guesses: O(n³) time and O(n²) space. Extra rows and columns represent empty intervals at either end. The computation is iterative and does not depend on recursion depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Pay only for wrong guesses; in a singleton interval no money is needed. The adversary chooses the worse remaining side, whereas the player chooses the best first guess: reversing min and max changes the problem. A guess at an endpoint has one empty side and must still be considered. For n = 1 return zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store the minimizing guess for every interval and reconstruct the guaranteed strategy.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace guess cost with an arbitrary nonnegative cost per value and use the same interval recurrence.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:646":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You are given pairs \u003ccode>[a, b]\u003c/code> with a &lt; b. A pair may follow another only if its first value is strictly greater than the previous pair's second value. Choose and order pairs to make the longest chain, and return its length. For \u003ccode>[[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]]\u003c/code>, the answer is 2: the first and last pairs can be chained.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 1000 pairs and endpoints are between -1000 and 1000. Equality at the boundary is not sufficient. Input order is not a restriction: pairs may be rearranged. Negative endpoints are allowed. The answer counts pairs rather than the distance covered by the chain.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Longest chain of pairs where each pair starts after the previous one ends\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which pair should start the chain? Think about which first choice leaves the most room for the rest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Greedy by earliest end (Greedy algorithms and exchange arguments)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort by end and take each pair whose start is greater than the end of the last pair taken (activity selection). The pair that ends first can replace the first pair of any optimal chain, so taking it is safe. An O(n^2) DP also passes but is not needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try subsets and all their orders, test the strict chain condition, and keep the maximum length. Even selecting subsets is exponential, and permutations add more work. A quadratic dynamic program over sorted pairs is also possible, but comparing every compatible predecessor is not necessary for the count.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Among available pairs, choose one ending earliest. In any optimal nonempty chain, its first pair can be replaced by this earliest-ending pair: the replacement ends no later, so every later pair that followed the original first pair still fits. This exchange preserves the number of pairs. Apply the same argument to the remaining pairs whose starts exceed the selected end. Therefore repeatedly taking the compatible pair with the earliest end produces an optimal chain.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort pairs by end and scan once. If a pair starts after the last selected end, select it; otherwise it cannot follow the current choice. Use negative infinity as the initial end so even a pair beginning at the smallest allowed value is accepted. \u003ccode>sorted\u003c/code> makes a new list rather than changing the caller's input.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Sort\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The pairs are already ordered by end: 2, 3, 4.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Select\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Take [1,2]. The next start equals 2, so [2,3] cannot follow it.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The start 3 exceeds 2, so take [3,4]. The count is 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def longest_pair_chain(pairs):\n    end = float('-inf')\n    count = 0\n    for start, finish in sorted(pairs, key=lambda pair: pair[1]):\n        if start &gt; end:\n            count += 1\n            end = finish\n    return count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sorting n pairs takes O(n log n) time; the scan takes O(n). The copied sorted list and Python's sorting machinery use O(n) auxiliary space. Only the count is retained, although keeping selected pairs would recover a chain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use strict \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> rather than \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code>; touching endpoints do not chain. Sorting by start alone does not justify selecting the first compatible pair, because it may finish very late. A single pair returns one, including when both endpoints are negative. Duplicate pairs cannot follow each other under the strict rule.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the selected pairs as well as their count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Maximize total pair weights; earliest finishing alone is then insufficient and a weighted scheduling method is needed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:712":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given two strings, delete characters from either until the remaining strings are equal. Each deletion costs the deleted character's ASCII code. Return the smallest total cost. For \u003ccode>\"sea\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"eat\"\u003c/code>, deleting \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> costs 115 + 116 = 231. Characters that remain must keep their original order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each string has 1 to 1000 lowercase English letters, so the costs are positive ASCII codes. Only the total cost is requested, not the resulting common string or the indices of deleted characters. The two strings need not have the same length.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Smallest total of character codes deleted to make two strings equal\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The characters you keep must appear in the same order in both strings. What is the most valuable thing you can keep?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Weighted longest common subsequence (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Minimising the deleted value is maximising the kept value: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + ord(c) on a match, else the larger of dropping one character from either side. The answer is the codes of both strings minus twice the best kept value (or fill deletion costs directly with min).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) time, O(n) space with two rows\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all subsequences of each string, compare equal subsequences, and total the cost of deleting all other characters. This explores exponentially many subsets, even though many possibilities share the same prefixes. Alternatively, recursively try deleting the first character from either string without caching; that repeats the same suffix comparisons many times.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let the state for two prefixes be their minimum deletion cost. If the last characters match, keep both: there exists an optimum retaining that equal pair because replacing a kept occurrence of the same character with these later occurrences cannot increase cost. The state is then the diagonal predecessor. If they differ, at least one final character must be deleted, so compare deleting the first string's last character with deleting the second string's last character. Add that character's ASCII cost to the appropriate predecessor.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The empty-prefix boundary equals the sum of all codes in the other prefix. In a row scan, the previous row represents the preceding first-string prefix, and the current row's left cell is already computed. The final entry compares the full strings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>\"a\"\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>\"b\"\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Boundary\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The empty first prefix costs 0 against empty and 98 against b.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Process a\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Deleting a from a versus empty costs 97. The final characters differ.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Either 98 + 97 or 97 + 98 is 195, so both characters are deleted.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def minimum_delete_sum(a, b):\n    if len(b) &gt; len(a):\n        a, b = b, a\n    prev = [0]\n    for char in b:\n        prev.append(prev[-1] + ord(char))\n    for x in a:\n        row = [prev[0] + ord(x)]\n        for j, y in enumerate(b, 1):\n            if x == y:\n                row.append(prev[j - 1])\n            else:\n                row.append(min(prev[j] + ord(x), row[j - 1] + ord(y)))\n        prev = row\n    return prev[-1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For lengths m and n, the scan uses O(mn) time and O(min(m, n)) extra space. The row swap is valid because deletion cost is symmetric between the two input strings. Boundary sums are accumulated once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Minimizing the number of deletions does not necessarily minimize their ASCII sum. Count deletions from both strings and use \u003ccode>ord\u003c/code>, not the digit value or a zero-based alphabet index. Equal strings cost zero. If one string is empty, the cost is the sum of all character codes in the other.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Recover the characters deleted on both sides by storing predecessor decisions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replace ASCII codes with supplied nonnegative character weights.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1653":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A string contains only \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>b\u003c/code>. Delete as few characters as possible so that no \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> appears before an \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> in the remaining string. Equivalently, all remaining \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> characters precede all remaining \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> characters. Return the minimum number of deletions. For \u003ccode>\"baba\"\u003c/code>, deleting both b's or deleting the first b and last a costs 2.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The length is between 1 and 100,000, so enumerating subsequences is not viable. Each character has only two possible values, and the objective is a count rather than the resulting string. The empty string is balanced, and removing all occurrences of either letter always produces a valid result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fewest deletions so that no 'b' appears before an 'a'\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When you reach an 'a', there are two ways to make the prefix acceptable. What does each cost?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> One-pass DP over prefixes (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan with b_count, the number of b's so far, and best, the answer for the prefix. On 'b', b_count += 1; on 'a', either delete this 'a' (best + 1) or delete every 'b' so far (b_count): best = min(best + 1, b_count). Equivalently, try every split point: b's before it plus a's after it.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try every subset of characters to delete, check whether the survivors contain a \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> before any later \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>, and select the fewest deletions. This takes exponential time in the length. A simpler polynomial baseline tests every split position and counts b's before it plus a's after it, taking O(n²) if each count scans its side.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain the best deletion count for the prefix and the number of b's seen. A new b can always be appended to a balanced result without further deletion. For a new a, either delete that a, costing one more than the previous optimum, or keep it and delete every earlier b. The latter leaves only earlier a's and the new a, costing exactly the count of earlier b's. These are exhaustive: if a kept a has a preceding kept b, the result is invalid.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Taking the smaller option maintains the optimal prefix count by induction. The raw b count tracks the cost of the second option even if a different optimal prefix had already deleted some characters. Neither a split list nor an explicit resulting string is needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>\"baba\"\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First b\u003c/p>\u003cpre>There is one b and the best deletion count is 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First a and next b\u003c/p>\u003cpre>For a, min(delete it, remove earlier b) = 1. The next b raises the seen count to 2, without changing the best.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Final a\u003c/p>\u003cpre>min(1 + 1, 2) = 2, so return 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def minimum_balanced_deletions(s):\n    b_count = 0\n    best = 0\n    for char in s:\n        if char == 'b':\n            b_count += 1\n        else:\n            best = min(best + 1, b_count)\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The scan visits n characters once: O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. The input is not modified. The stored count is at most n, and no recursion is involved.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Already balanced strings, including all a's or all b's, need zero deletions. A balanced result may contain only one letter or be empty. Counting every inversion is wrong because one deletion can remove many inversions. \u003ccode>b_count\u003c/code> counts all original b's seen, not just those kept by one particular optimal prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return a split position and the actual indices to delete.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Assign different deletion costs to characters and compare weighted prefix and suffix totals.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1140":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Players alternate taking stones from the front of a row of piles. Initially M is 1; on a turn a player takes X piles, where 1 &lt;= X &lt;= 2M and at most the number remaining, then M becomes max(M, X). Both players play optimally to maximize their own stones. Return the first player's total. For \u003ccode>[2,7,9,4,4]\u003c/code>, the answer is 10.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 100 piles, each with 1 to 10,000 stones. The total number of stones is fixed and all piles are positive. Each turn must take at least one pile, and taking all remaining piles is legal when the current limit permits it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Players take all the stones in the first X piles, 1 &lt;= X &lt;= 2M, after which M becomes max(M, X); maximise the first player's total\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whatever has happened, the rest of the game depends only on where the remaining piles start and on M. The total of the remaining piles is fixed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Game DP over (position, M) (Advanced dynamic programming; memoisation is enough)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let f(i, M) be the most the player to move can collect from piles i onwards. Then f(i, M) = suffix[i] - min over x in 1..2M of f(i + x, max(M, x)), and f(i, M) = suffix[i] when i + 2M &gt;= n. Memoise over (i, M).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^3) time, O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Generate every legal sequence of moves until all piles are taken, alternating players and choosing the best outcome for the current player. The branching factor and depth can grow with the number of piles, producing an exponential game tree. Repeated states do not depend on the exact earlier moves once the next index and M are fixed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>best(i, m)\u003c/code> be the maximum stones the player about to move can obtain from the remaining suffix. The suffix total is fixed. After taking x piles, the opponent obtains \u003ccode>best(i+x, max(m,x))\u003c/code>; everything else belongs to the current player. Therefore choose x minimizing the opponent's optimal share, or equivalently maximize suffix total minus that share. If at most 2m piles remain, the current player can take all of them and this is optimal because every pile is positive.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Precompute suffix sums, then memoize the two-argument recurrence. Each call either returns the remaining total immediately or advances the index, so the recursion is acyclic. The same definition applies on both players' turns, avoiding separate turn-specific cases.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[1,2,3]\u003c/code> with M = 1:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Take one\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The opponent faces [2,3] with M = 1 and can take both, leaving the first player 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Take two\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The opponent receives the remaining 3, leaving 1 + 2 = 3 for the first player.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Choose\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The larger guaranteed share is 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from functools import lru_cache\n\ndef stone_game_ii(piles):\n    n = len(piles)\n    suffix = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        suffix[i] = suffix[i + 1] + piles[i]\n    @lru_cache(None)\n    def best(i, m):\n        if 2 * m &gt;= n - i:\n            return suffix[i]\n        return suffix[i] - min(best(i + x, max(m, x))\n                               for x in range(1, 2 * m + 1))\n    return best(0, 1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(n²) possible \u003ccode>(i, m)\u003c/code> states and each tries at most O(n) moves, for O(n³) time in this direct bound. Memoization and suffix sums use O(n²) space, including O(n) recursion depth. The actual number of reachable states may be smaller.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The opponent is also optimizing, so taking the largest immediate pile total is not always best. Update M with the number of piles taken, not their stone sum. A single pile is taken in full. The shortcut relies on positive piles and on being allowed to take all remaining piles.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store the best move at each state and reconstruct one optimal play sequence.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the score difference rather than the first player's total.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:688":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A knight starts at a specified cell on an n by n chessboard and makes exactly k independent moves. Each move chooses one of its eight standard offsets with equal probability, even if it leaves the board. Once off the board it cannot return. Return the probability that it is still on the board after k moves. On a 1 by 1 board with one move, the probability is 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The board size is 1 to 25, k is 0 to 100, and the starting cell is valid. There are exponentially many possible move sequences; leaving the board on any intermediate move counts as failure. A floating-point answer is expected with tolerance; no random simulation is needed for the computed result.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Probability that a knight is still on an n x n board after k uniformly random moves\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Instead of following single paths, spread probability over the board one move at a time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Probability DP over steps (DP on grids, strings and knapsacks)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a board of probabilities. For each move, every cell sends one eighth of its probability to each of its eight knight targets that are still on the board (mass that leaves is dropped). After k moves, sum the board.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(k n^2) time (8 moves per cell), O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all 8^k move sequences, test whether every prefix remains on the board, and divide the number of survivors by 8^k. This is exact but infeasible for large k. A random estimate avoids enumeration but cannot guarantee the requested precision and introduces sampling error.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A state stores the probability mass at each board cell after a given number of moves, counting only paths that never left. Initially the start cell holds probability one. At each step, send one eighth of each cell's mass to every legal knight destination; the remaining shares disappear off the board. Different paths arriving at the same cell can be added because their future behavior depends only on that cell and the number of moves left.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a new board for each step, so mass arriving during a step is not moved again in that same step. Summing all cells after k steps gives the probability of survival. When k is zero the initial mass remains intact, including on a one-cell board.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For n = 3, one move from the center \u003ccode>(1,1)\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Initial mass\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The center has probability 1 and every other cell has probability 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Distribute\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Each of the eight knight offsets leaves the board, so no mass reaches a legal destination.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Total\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The new board sums to 0; survival probability is 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def knight_probability(n, k, row, column):\n    offsets = [(dr, dc) for dr, dc in ((1,2),(1,-2),(-1,2),(-1,-2),\n                                        (2,1),(2,-1),(-2,1),(-2,-1))]\n    current = [[0.0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n    current[row][column] = 1.0\n    for _ in range(k):\n        following = [[0.0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n        for r in range(n):\n            for c in range(n):\n                for dr, dc in offsets:\n                    nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n                    if 0 &lt;= nr &lt; n and 0 &lt;= nc &lt; n:\n                        following[nr][nc] += current[r][c] / 8.0\n        current = following\n    return sum(map(sum, current))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are k rounds over n² cells and eight fixed moves, so time is O(kn²) and auxiliary space is O(n²) for two boards. Floating-point rounding can cause tiny errors; compare results using a tolerance rather than exact equality.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not divide by the number of legal moves: an off-board choice still has probability one eighth and is lost. Updating a board in place would allow extra moves in one round. Zero moves has answer 1. Position coordinates are zero-based, and all eight signed offsets must be included exactly once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Compute survival probabilities from every starting cell for a fixed number of moves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the move distribution to nonuniform probabilities that sum to one.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1442":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For an integer array, count index triples i &lt; j &lt;= k such that the XOR of \u003ccode>arr[i:j]\u003c/code> equals the XOR of \u003ccode>arr[j:k+1]\u003c/code>. Both parts are nonempty. For \u003ccode>[2,3,1,6,7]\u003c/code>, four triples satisfy the condition. Return the number of triples, not their index lists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The array has 1 to 300 elements, each between 1 and 100,000,000. Quadratic work is comfortably feasible. The two ranges must both contain at least one element. Different split positions within the same outer interval count as different triples.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count triples i &lt; j &lt;= k where the XOR of a[i..j-1] equals the XOR of a[j..k]\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If two values are equal, what is their XOR? What does that say about the whole stretch from i to k?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix XOR (Bits and number theory, with prefix sums)\u003c/p>\u003cp>a == b exactly when a ^ b = 0, that is when the XOR of a[i..k] is 0, which with running XORs means prefix[i] == prefix[k + 1]. Then every j in (i, k] works: k - i choices. Sum over equal-prefix pairs in O(n^2), or in O(n) with dicts holding the count and the index sum per prefix value.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time with dicts (O(n^2) is plenty for n &lt;= 300), O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try all O(n³) triples and explicitly XOR the two selected parts, costing up to O(n) per triple and O(n⁴) overall. Prefix XORs reduce each comparison to constant time but still leave O(n³) choices. The split index can instead be counted in bulk once the outer interval is known.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The two part XORs are equal exactly when their combined XOR is zero: XORing equal quantities gives zero, and if \u003ccode>a XOR b\u003c/code> is zero then a equals b. For a fixed interval from i through k whose XOR is zero, every j in \u003ccode>i+1\u003c/code> through k is valid. There are exactly k-i such splits, provided k &gt; i.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Enumerate each start i and extend its end k while maintaining the XOR of the current interval. Whenever that XOR is zero, add k-i. This counts every triple exactly once because its outer endpoints determine a unique interval, and its split belongs to precisely the counted range. A length-one zero interval would contribute zero automatically.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[1,3,2]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start at 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The running XORs are 1, 2, then 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Zero interval\u003c/p>\u003cpre>For i = 0 and k = 2, j can be 1 or 2, so add 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Other starts\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Neither remaining interval has XOR zero; the answer is 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def count_xor_triplets(arr):\n    answer = 0\n    for i in range(len(arr)):\n        value = 0\n        for k in range(i, len(arr)):\n            value ^= arr[k]\n            if value == 0:\n                answer += k - i\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n(n+1)/2 intervals, each extended in constant time: O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space. This matches the small input constraint. The count can be much larger than the array length; Python integers retain the exact result.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The split j may equal k, leaving one element on the right, but it may not equal i. Adding k-i+1 would count an empty left part. XOR is not ordinary addition, and a zero interval contributes multiple triples rather than one. A one-element input returns zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use prefix XOR frequencies and index sums to count contributions in linear time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the valid triples themselves, accepting output-sensitive cost.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1823":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Number n friends from 1 through n around a circle. Starting at friend 1, count k people including the current person, remove the kth, and continue from the next remaining person. Repeat until one friend remains; return that friend's original number. For n = 5 and k = 2, the survivor is 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Both n and k are between 1 and 500. Direct list removal is possible at this scale but can shift O(n) elements per deletion. The identity of the survivor depends on the circular position, so zero-based indexing makes wraparound arithmetic especially simple.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Friends sit in a circle and every k-th one leaves; who remains?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>After the first friend leaves, the game restarts with n - 1 friends from a new position. How are the two answers related?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Josephus recurrence (Bits and number theory; recursion)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Number from 0. The survivor among n is the survivor among n - 1 shifted by k: f(1) = 0, f(n) = (f(n - 1) + k) % n, and the answer is f(n) + 1. Simulating with a list and pop((i + k - 1) % len) is O(n^2), also fine for n &lt;= 500.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep the current circle in a list. Repeatedly move \u003ccode>(k-1)\u003c/code> positions forward modulo the list length, remove that element, and continue at the same list index modulo the new length. This simulation is intuitive but requires O(n²) time in Python because removing from the middle shifts elements. It uses O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Suppose the survivor's zero-based position in a circle of size s-1 is known, where counting starts immediately after a removal. In the original circle of size s, that reduced circle begins at position k modulo s. Shifting the survivor by k and wrapping modulo s maps it back to the original numbering. Therefore \u003ccode>winner(s) = (winner(s-1) + k) % s\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>winner(1) = 0\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build the answer from size one upward. The reduction and reverse shift are valid even when k exceeds the circle size, because only its remainder determines the removed position. Convert the final zero-based position back to the requested one-based friend number.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For n = 5, k = 2:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Base\u003c/p>\u003cpre>At size 1 the survivor index is 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Grow\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Sizes 2, 3, and 4 produce indices 0, 2, and 0 respectively.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish\u003c/p>\u003cpre>At size 5 the index is (0 + 2) % 5 = 2, corresponding to friend 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def circular_winner(n, k):\n    winner = 0\n    for size in range(2, n + 1):\n        winner = (winner + k) % size\n    return winner + 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The loop performs n-1 constant-time arithmetic operations: O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. It does not construct the circle or the elimination order. Python arithmetic naturally handles larger k as well.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Counting includes the current person, so the first removal in a zero-based list is at k-1, not k. The recurrence shifts by k because the next start is one position beyond that removed person. With k = 1 the last numbered friend survives; with n = 1 the answer is 1 regardless of k.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Simulate the circle to return the complete elimination order.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use an order-statistics structure to efficiently retrieve eliminated positions for very large n.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1352":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design a stream supporting \u003ccode>add(num)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>getProduct(k)\u003c/code>. The latter returns the product of the last k added numbers. For additions 3, 0, 2, 5, querying the last two returns 10, while querying the last three returns 0. Queries always refer to at least one and no more than the number of inserted values.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Numbers are between 0 and 100, and there are at most 40,000 operations. The problem guarantees queried products fit in a signed 32-bit integer. The stream may contain zeros, including consecutive zeros, and each query refers to the current end of the stream. Exact integer arithmetic is available in Python.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A stream of numbers with queries for the product of the last k\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Running products let you divide out the part you do not want. What does a zero do to that idea?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix products with a reset at zero (Designing data structures, with prefix sums)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a list P of running products since the last zero, starting [1]. On add(0) reset P to [1]; otherwise append P[-1] * x. A query k returns 0 if k &gt;= len(P) (a zero lies among the last k) and P[-1] // P[-1 - k] otherwise.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep all inserted numbers and multiply the last k entries on each query. An addition takes O(1), but a query takes O(k), making a long sequence of large queries expensive. It is nevertheless a useful reference implementation because it treats zeros and repeated values naturally.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Within the suffix since the most recent zero, keep cumulative products starting with a sentinel 1. Adding a nonzero number multiplies the last prefix product and appends the result. If k is less than the prefix list length, the requested window lies wholly inside that zero-free suffix. Its product is the newest prefix divided exactly by the prefix immediately before the window.\u003c/p>\u003cp>When a zero is added, discard previous prefix products and restart at \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>. If k is at least the new list length, the requested window reaches that zero, so its answer is zero. This also handles consecutive zeros. The division is exact because each prefix is a product of the same initial zero-free sequence; integer division avoids floating-point rounding.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>After adding 3, 0, 2, 5:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Reset\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Adding 0 resets the prefix list to [1].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">New suffix\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Adding 2 and 5 produces [1, 2, 10].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Queries\u003c/p>\u003cpre>k = 2 gives 10 // 1 = 10. k = 3 reaches the preceding zero, so returns 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class ProductOfNumbers:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.prefix = [1]\n\n    def add(self, num):\n        if num == 0:\n            self.prefix = [1]\n        else:\n            self.prefix.append(self.prefix[-1] * num)\n\n    def getProduct(self, k):\n        if k &gt;= len(self.prefix):\n            return 0\n        return self.prefix[-1] // self.prefix[-k - 1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each operation uses O(1) arithmetic operations amortized, and the list uses O(a) space after a additions without zero. Resetting can release O(a) stored entries at once, charged to their earlier additions. Python integer multiplication and division have size-dependent costs if cumulative products grow large, even if queried products fit the problem guarantee. Resetting releases the old list.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A window ending just after a zero must include that zero when k equals the current prefix list length. A window of size one returns the latest value, including zero. Do not divide by a prefix containing zero, and do not use floating-point division. The API assumes valid positive k as specified.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return products modulo a prime, taking care that division requires invertible factors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Support arbitrary range products by tracking zero counts and prefix products per segment.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2101":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each bomb has integer coordinates and a blast radius. Detonating a bomb immediately detonates every other bomb whose center lies within or on its radius; those bombs can trigger more bombs. Choose one initial bomb and return the largest number that eventually detonate. For \u003ccode>[[0,0,2],[2,0,1],[3,0,1]]\u003c/code>, starting at the first triggers all three.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 100 bombs; coordinates and radii can be as large as 100,000. A blast relation is directional because radii differ. A bomb is included in its own final detonation count. Coordinates need not be distinct, and touching the blast boundary is sufficient.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Set off one bomb; every exploding bomb sets off all bombs within its own radius; maximise the total\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Does \"a reaches b\" imply \"b reaches a\"? What structure does that suggest?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Directed reachability by a search from each source (Graph modelling and traversal)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build a directed graph with an edge i -&gt; j when (xi - xj)^2 + (yi - yj)^2 &lt;= ri^2 (integers, no square roots), then run BFS or DFS from every bomb and take the largest reachable set. Union-find is wrong here, because reach is not symmetric.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n^3) time (n searches over up to n^2 edges), O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each possible initial bomb, repeatedly scan all detonated and undetonated bombs, adding any bomb currently in range, until no further changes occur. A straightforward implementation can perform O(n) rounds of O(n²) comparisons per starting bomb, for O(n⁴) time. It is a useful direct simulation but repeatedly checks the same relationships.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Construct a directed edge from bomb i to bomb j exactly when squared distance between centers is at most radius i squared. A chain of detonations corresponds to a directed path; every bomb on such a path eventually detonates, and every detonated bomb has a path from the initial bomb. Thus the number of bombs triggered is precisely the reachable vertex count.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Run a depth-first search from each vertex, marking a bomb when it is first added to the stack. The visited set prevents cycles and duplicate counting. Take the largest count. Directed reachability, rather than undirected connected components, is essential when a large-radius bomb reaches a small-radius bomb that cannot reach back.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[[0,0,2],[2,0,1],[3,0,1]]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Edges\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The first bomb reaches the second on its boundary; the second reaches the third.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Search\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Starting at the first visits it, then the second, then the third.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Result\u003c/p>\u003cpre>All three bombs are reachable, so the maximum is 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def maximum_detonation(bombs):\n    n = len(bombs)\n    graph = [[] for _ in bombs]\n    for i, (x, y, radius) in enumerate(bombs):\n        for j, (xx, yy, _) in enumerate(bombs):\n            if i != j and (x - xx) ** 2 + (y - yy) ** 2 &lt;= radius ** 2:\n                graph[i].append(j)\n    answer = 0\n    for start in range(n):\n        seen = {start}\n        stack = [start]\n        while stack:\n            for nxt in graph[stack.pop()]:\n                if nxt not in seen:\n                    seen.add(nxt)\n                    stack.append(nxt)\n        answer = max(answer, len(seen))\n    return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building the graph takes O(n²) time and space. Each of n searches costs O(n + e), giving O(n³) worst-case time for a dense graph and O(n²) total auxiliary space. Python integers avoid overflow in squared distances.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Include bombs exactly on the radius boundary. A blast need not be reciprocal, and transitive triggering can reach bombs outside the initial radius. Mark visited nodes before pushing them to avoid repeated stack entries. A single bomb always yields one, even if it reaches nothing else.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return an initial bomb achieving the maximum as well as the count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compute reachability for all starts using a transitive-closure matrix.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:382":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A nonempty singly linked list is provided to a sampler. Each call to \u003ccode>getRandom()\u003c/code> must return a node's value, with every node equally likely, without assuming the list length is known. Repeated values may occur: the probability requirement applies to nodes, not distinct values. For a three-node list, each node must be chosen with probability one third.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The list has 1 to 10,000 nodes, node values range from -10,000 to 10,000, and at most 10,000 calls are made. The follow-up asks for constant extra space and permits a list too large to store. The algorithm therefore cannot rely on an indexed array of all nodes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Return a uniformly random value from a linked list, possibly of unknown length\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can you decide on the fly, as the nodes go past, whether the current node should replace your pick?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Reservoir sampling over a stream (Bits and number theory: randomness and counting)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Simplest: copy the values into a list once and use random.choice (O(n) space, O(1) per call). Without extra space, reservoir sampling: keep the i-th node with probability 1/i; node i survives every later step with probability (1/i)·(i/(i+1))···((n-1)/n) = 1/n.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) per call and O(1) space with the reservoir, or O(1) per call and O(n) space with a list\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Traverse the list once, copy all values into an array, and choose a uniformly random array index on each query. Construction takes O(n) time and space, while a query is O(1). This works for a fixed list but violates the constant-extra-space follow-up and uses memory proportional to the list length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan the list once per query. When visiting the ith node, replace the current candidate with probability 1/i, using an unbiased integer draw. For any earlier node, its selection probability after i-1 nodes is 1/(i-1), and it survives the new replacement with probability (i-1)/i. Their product is 1/i. The new node is selected with probability 1/i as well, establishing uniformity by induction without knowing the final length.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The first node is always selected. Only the current node, a counter, and one candidate value are stored. A new random scan on each call gives an independent selection when the underlying random draws are independent. If equal values appear on several nodes, their returned value's probability is the sum of those nodes' probabilities.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a list A -&gt; B -&gt; C:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First node\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Choose A with probability 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Second node\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Replace A by B with probability 1/2, leaving each with probability 1/2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Third node\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Replace by C with probability 1/3; A and B each survive with probability 1/2 times 2/3 = 1/3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\nclass RandomNode:\n    def __init__(self, head):\n        self.head = head\n\n    def getRandom(self):\n        node = self.head\n        chosen = None\n        count = 0\n        while node is not None:\n            count += 1\n            if random.randrange(count) == 0:\n                chosen = node.val\n            node = node.next\n        return chosen\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For n nodes each call traverses the list once, taking O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space. Initialization stores only the head in O(1) time and space. The list is assumed nonempty and unchanged during a call.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choosing every node with the same fixed replacement probability biases toward later nodes. Sampling a value uniformly among distinct values is also wrong when duplicates occur. A single-node list always returns that value. Do not use modulo on a biased random integer source; \u003ccode>randrange(count)\u003c/code> directly supplies a uniform choice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Sample k distinct nodes uniformly using a reservoir of size k.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Precompute an array for a static list when O(n) memory is acceptable and calls must be fast.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1856":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each nonempty contiguous subarray of positive integers, multiply its minimum element by its sum. Return the largest such min-product modulo 1,000,000,007. Choose the largest exact product before reducing it modulo the constant. For \u003ccode>[1,2,3,2]\u003c/code>, subarray \u003ccode>[2,3,2]\u003c/code> gives 2 times 7, or 14.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The array contains 1 to 100,000 positive values, each at most 10,000,000. The true answer is guaranteed to fit a signed 64-bit integer, though intermediate arithmetic in Python remains exact. The subarray must be contiguous and nonempty. The modulo affects the returned representation, not which subarray is considered largest.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Maximise min(subarray) × sum(subarray) over all subarrays\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the element that acts as the minimum. How far can its subarray stretch?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Monotonic stack bounds plus prefix sums (Monotonic stacks and queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each i, the best subarray with a[i] as its minimum spans from just after the previous smaller element to just before the next smaller one (values are positive, so wider is better). Find both bounds with a monotonic stack and get range sums from prefix sums; maximise before taking the modulus.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate all O(n²) subarrays, incrementally maintaining their minimum and sum, then compare their products. This takes O(n²) time and constant extra space, but is too slow for the largest input. The same candidate minimum appears in many overlapping intervals, so considering its maximal span is enough.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fix a position as a minimum. Its best interval extends until a strictly smaller value blocks it on each side: all included values are at least the chosen value, and positivity makes the widest valid interval at least as good as any narrower one. Prefix sums give the sum of an interval in constant time.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A stack of indices with nondecreasing values finds these spans in one scan. When a smaller value appears, pop each larger stack top; the current position is its first strictly smaller right boundary. The new stack top is a previous value no greater than the popped value. Equal values are allowed to remain: the leftmost equal minimum eventually obtains the whole span, so at least one representative covers every optimal interval. A final sentinel flushes the stack. Compare exact products before applying the modulus.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[1,2,3,2]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First decline\u003c/p>\u003cpre>At the last 2, pop 3: its span is only [3], giving 9.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Flush\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The later 2 covers [3,2], while the earlier 2 covers [2,3,2], giving 14.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Compare\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The 1 covers the whole array and gives 8. The maximum is 14.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def max_sum_min_product(nums):\n    prefix = [0]\n    for value in nums:\n        prefix.append(prefix[-1] + value)\n    stack = []\n    best = 0\n    for right in range(len(nums) + 1):\n        while stack and (right == len(nums) or nums[stack[-1]] &gt; nums[right]):\n            index = stack.pop()\n            left = stack[-1] + 1 if stack else 0\n            total = prefix[right] - prefix[left]\n            best = max(best, nums[index] * total)\n        stack.append(right)\n    return best % 1_000_000_007\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every index is pushed and popped at most once, so time is O(n). Prefix sums and the stack occupy O(n) auxiliary space. The end sentinel is appended only after the final flush and is never dereferenced.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The maximal-span argument relies on positive inputs; it fails for arbitrary negative values. Equal minima need a consistent tie rule, not accidental omission of all full spans. Do not compare reduced residues. A single element produces its square, and an all-equal array uses its entire sum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the boundaries of a maximizing subarray along with its product.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Find the largest rectangle in a histogram using a related stack of limiting heights.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2349":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design a system supporting \u003ccode>change(index, number)\u003c/code>, which assigns or replaces the number at an index, and \u003ccode>find(number)\u003c/code>, which returns the smallest index currently assigned that number, or -1 if none. For changes \u003ccode>(2,10)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>(1,10)\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>(1,20)\u003c/code>, finding 10 returns 2 and finding 20 returns 1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Indices and numbers are positive integers up to 10⁹, and there are at most 100,000 operations. Most possible indices may never be assigned, and a query may ask about a number that has never been assigned. Reassignments must be reflected in queries, including repeated assignments of the same number.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Change the number stored at an index; query the smallest index currently holding a given number\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Changes leave old entries behind. Do you have to clean them up straight away?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Heap with lazy deletion (Designing data structures)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep index -&gt; number in a dict and number -&gt; min-heap of indices. On change, update the dict and push the index onto the new number's heap. On find, pop from the top while the top index no longer holds that number (lazy deletion), then return the top or -1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(log n) amortised per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store only a dictionary from index to current number. To find a number, scan all assignments and return the smallest matching index. Changes are O(1), but each query costs O(d) for d assigned indices, which can make a long operation sequence quadratic.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep the authoritative current number for each index, and separately keep a min-heap of candidate indices for each number. On change, update the dictionary and push the index into the new number's heap. Old heap entries are allowed to remain. On find, repeatedly remove the smallest heap entry while the dictionary says that index currently holds a different number. The first valid entry, if any, is the smallest current index because every current assignment was inserted and a heap presents candidates in increasing order.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A reassigned index can return to an earlier number, leaving duplicate valid entries. They do not affect the minimum and become removable if the index later changes again. Each pushed entry is popped at most once, so cleanup is amortized across operations, although one find may perform many removals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>change(2,10)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>change(1,10)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>change(1,20)\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Changes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The heap for 10 contains indices 1 and 2, but the current mapping says index 1 holds 20.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Find 10\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Remove stale top 1; index 2 is current and is returned.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Find 20\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Its heap contains valid index 1, so return 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\nclass NumberContainers:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.current = {}\n        self.heaps = {}\n\n    def change(self, index, number):\n        self.current[index] = number\n        heapq.heappush(self.heaps.setdefault(number, []), index)\n\n    def find(self, number):\n        heap = self.heaps.get(number, [])\n        while heap and self.current.get(heap[0]) != number:\n            heapq.heappop(heap)\n        return heap[0] if heap else -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For q operations, each change pushes once and each entry is removed at most once. Total heap work is O(q log q), or O(log q) amortized per operation, with O(q) space. A single find may remove many stale entries, so its worst-case latency is not O(log q).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A heap alone cannot determine whether an old assignment is still current. Do not return its top without validating against the authoritative dictionary. A never-seen number returns -1. Repeated changes to the same number may create duplicates; these are harmless but consume space until removed or the structure is discarded.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use ordered sets to delete old assignments eagerly and bound a query's cleanup work.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return the largest assigned index using max-heaps and the same validity check.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2402":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are n rooms numbered from 0. Meetings have distinct original start times and half-open intervals \u003ccode>[start, end)\u003c/code>. Process meetings in order of original start. A meeting uses the smallest-numbered room free at its start. If none is free, delay it until the earliest room becomes free, preserving its duration; if several rooms free at that time, choose the smallest number. Earlier original starts have priority over later starts when waiting. Return the room hosting the most meetings, breaking count ties by smallest room number.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For two rooms and meetings \u003ccode>[[0,10],[1,5],[2,7],[3,4]]\u003c/code>, the third meeting waits until time 5 and the fourth waits until time 10. Room 0 hosts two meetings and room 1 hosts two, so return 0.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 100 rooms and up to 100,000 meetings. Start times are unique, durations are positive, and the original end is at most 500,000. Delayed finish times can greatly exceed original times, so arithmetic must accommodate accumulated durations. The input order is not guaranteed to match the order of original start times. A room whose meeting ends exactly at a start is free for that start.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Meetings take the lowest-numbered free room or wait for the room that frees first, keeping their length; which room hosts the most?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At each meeting you need either the lowest-numbered free room or the room that becomes free soonest: two different orders.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Two heaps for room allocation (Heaps and priority queues)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Sort the meetings by start. Keep a min-heap of free room numbers and a min-heap of (end time, room) for busy rooms. Before each meeting release the rooms with end &lt;= start; if a room is free take the lowest, else pop the earliest (end, room) and run the meeting from that end for its original length. Count meetings per room.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(m log m + m log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Sort by original start, keep each room's next available time, and examine all n rooms for each meeting. If any available time is at most the start, choose the smallest such index; otherwise choose the minimum pair \u003ccode>(available time, room index)\u003c/code>. Update that room and increment its count. This direct simulation is O(m log m + mn) time and O(n) state, excluding the sorted copy. It is an excellent oracle for small cases, but scans rooms even when only one is relevant.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are two distinct orderings to maintain. Among currently free rooms, only the smallest room number matters. Among occupied rooms, the earliest ending time matters first and the room number breaks ties. Use a min-heap of free indices and a min-heap of \u003ccode>(actual end, room)\u003c/code> pairs for busy rooms. At the start of each original meeting, move every room with actual end at most the original start into the free heap. If any are free, choose the smallest and schedule at the original start. Otherwise pop the earliest busy pair and schedule at its end, retaining the original duration.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Processing by original start enforces the waiting priority: any previously delayed meeting has already reserved its room before a later request is processed. A busy heap entry can represent a meeting scheduled in the future, not just one active at the current original time. Its end still correctly represents when its room next becomes available. All rooms whose prior end is at most the current start must be released before choosing, since a room that finished earlier but has a larger number must not automatically beat a lower-numbered free room.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For correctness, each room is in exactly one heap after a meeting is assigned. Releasing completed entries preserves the set of free rooms. If the free heap is nonempty, its minimum implements the first rule. Otherwise, the lexicographically smallest busy pair implements the earliest possible start and room tie rule. Replacing the chosen entry with its new end preserves this invariant for the next meeting. Induction over sorted meetings proves every assignment and count is correct.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For two rooms and \u003ccode>[[0,10],[1,5],[2,7],[3,4]]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First two meetings\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Room 0 is busy until 10 and room 1 until 5; each count is 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Meeting [2,7)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>No room is free at 2. Room 1 frees first at 5, so duration 5 moves it to [5,10); its count becomes 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Meeting [3,4)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Both rooms next free at 10. The tie chooses room 0, whose delayed meeting ends at 11. Both counts are 2, so return 0.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\ndef most_booked(n, meetings):\n    free = list(range(n))\n    heapq.heapify(free)\n    busy = []\n    counts = [0] * n\n    for start, end in sorted(meetings):\n        while busy and busy[0][0] &lt;= start:\n            _, room = heapq.heappop(busy)\n            heapq.heappush(free, room)\n        if free:\n            room = heapq.heappop(free)\n            finish = end\n        else:\n            available, room = heapq.heappop(busy)\n            finish = available + end - start\n        counts[room] += 1\n        heapq.heappush(busy, (finish, room))\n    return max(range(n), key=lambda room: (counts[room], -room))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For m meetings, sorting costs O(m log m). Each meeting inserts and removes at most one busy and one free heap entry, taking O(m log n) total heap time. The heaps and counts use O(n) space, and the sorted copy adds O(m). Python's unbounded integers avoid overflow in delayed end times. Returning the maximum count scans the n rooms once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code> when releasing rooms because intervals are half-open. Do not select the earliest-ending room when any room is already free: the lowest-numbered free room wins even if it freed more recently. A delayed meeting keeps its duration, not its original end. When multiple busy rooms end together, tuple ordering must break ties by room number. Process by original start, not delayed start, to preserve waiting priority. One room simply serializes every meeting; unused rooms have count zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return each meeting's assigned room and actual start and end times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the tie rule to prioritize another room attribute, adjusting both heaps and the final count comparison.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1964":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every position in an array of obstacle heights, find the maximum length of a nondecreasing subsequence that ends at that exact position. Selected obstacles must preserve their original order, but need not be adjacent. Equal heights may follow each other. Return one answer per position. For \u003ccode>[1,2,3,2]\u003c/code>, the answers are \u003ccode>[1,2,3,3]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are 1 to 100,000 obstacles, each between 1 and 10⁷. The answer for each position depends only on the prefix through that position. A course ending at one position need not include the immediately preceding obstacle. The result contains one length for every input position.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For each position, the longest non-decreasing course that ends exactly there\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep, for every course length seen so far, the lowest height a course of that length can end with. How does a new obstacle change that list?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Patience-style longest non-decreasing subsequence (Dynamic programming: from recursion to tables)\u003c/p>\u003cp>The list of lowest ending heights is non-decreasing, so binary search it: the new obstacle extends the longest course whose ending height is &lt;= it (bisect_right, because equal heights may follow), replaces the entry at that position, and its answer is that position + 1.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n log n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each position i, examine every earlier position j whose height is at most height i, and set the answer to one plus the largest answer at such a j, or one if none exists. This directly implements the definition and costs O(n²) time and O(n) space. Enumerating all subsequences would be exponential and is unnecessary even as a baseline.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain \u003ccode>tails[length-1]\u003c/code>, the smallest possible final height among nondecreasing subsequences of that length in the processed prefix. A smaller tail is at least as useful as a larger tail for extending a subsequence later. The tails are nondecreasing: removing the last item of a minimum-tail length L course gives a valid shorter course with a tail no greater than the longer course's final value.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For new height x, find the first tail strictly greater than x. All preceding tails are at most x, so the longest of them can be extended by x. If the insertion position is p, this yields a course of length p+1 ending at x. No longer course ending at x exists: removing x from one would produce a length p+1 course with tail at most x, contradicting the position of the first greater tail. Thus p+1 is exactly the required answer for this position.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Replace \u003ccode>tails[p]\u003c/code> with x, or append x if p is at the end. Replacement cannot discard a better future possibility: x is smaller than the previous tail at this length and therefore can be followed by everything that could follow the old tail. The preceding tail is at most x, and the following tails are at least the old value, so sorted order survives. Binary search for the first strictly greater element is \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code>; searching for the first greater-or-equal element would mistakenly disallow equal heights.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The array of tails is a collection of separate optimal witnesses. Its entries need not themselves describe one subsequence, and replacing an entry does not retroactively change the answers already returned. The procedure records the new element's own extension length, rather than the length of the longest course anywhere in the prefix. This distinction matters when a low obstacle arrives after a long increasing run.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>[1,2,3,2]\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">First three\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Heights 1, 2, 3 append in turn. Tails become [1,2,3], with answers 1, 2, 3.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Last height 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The first tail strictly greater than 2 is 3 at position 2. Replace it with 2.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Finish\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Tails are [1,2,2], and the last answer is 3 from course [1,2,2]. The full output is [1,2,3,3].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from bisect import bisect_right\n\ndef longest_obstacle_course(obstacles):\n    tails = []\n    answers = []\n    for height in obstacles:\n        position = bisect_right(tails, height)\n        if position == len(tails):\n            tails.append(height)\n        else:\n            tails[position] = height\n        answers.append(position + 1)\n    return answers\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each of n obstacles performs one binary search in a list of at most n tails, for O(n log n) time. The tail list and returned answers use O(n) space. The input is not modified. Appending and replacing a list entry are amortized O(1); no insertion in the middle is performed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>bisect_right\u003c/code>, because equal heights can extend one another. A decreasing array produces all ones, while an all-equal array produces 1 through n. Return the position-specific length even when a previous obstacle ended a longer course. Replacing a tail rather than inserting it is essential: inserting would change the meaning of the later indices. A single obstacle yields \u003ccode>[1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Require strictly increasing heights, using the first tail greater than or equal to the current height.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track predecessor indices to reconstruct a longest course ending at each requested position.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1146":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design an array initialized to zeros with \u003ccode>set(index, val)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>snap()\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>get(index, snap_id)\u003c/code>. A snapshot returns an increasing identifier starting at 0; a later get returns the value at that index when that snapshot was taken. Setting an index multiple times before a snapshot keeps only its last value in that snapshot. For a length-one array, set 5, snap, then set 6; get at snapshot 0 returns 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The length is 1 to 50,000, values are 0 to 10⁹, and at most 50,000 operations occur. Each get refers to an existing snapshot and a valid index. A snapshot can be taken without any intervening changes, and unchanged positions retain their prior values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An array with snapshots; read any index as it was at any snapshot\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Copying the array at every snapshot is too expensive. What is the least you could store per index?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Per-key version history with binary search (Designing data structures)\u003c/p>\u003cp>Per index keep a list of (snap_id, value), appended on set (overwrite the last entry if it has the same snap_id). get(i, s) binary-searches that list for the last entry with snap_id &lt;= s; an index never set before s returns 0.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) set, O(log S) get, O(n + number of sets) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep the current array and append a full copy whenever snap is called. A get is then a direct lookup in the stored copy. This simple simulation costs O(length) time and space per snapshot, which is wasteful when snapshots are frequent and updates sparse.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store a chronological change history separately for each index. A history starts with \u003ccode>(0,0)\u003c/code>, representing its initial value. The current snapshot identifier denotes the version being edited. On set, append \u003ccode>(current id, value)\u003c/code> unless the latest record already has that id, in which case replace it. A snap returns the current id and increments it; no per-index copying is needed.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For get at version s, find the last record whose identifier is at most s. Every earlier change has been superseded, and every later change happened after the snapshot. Histories have increasing identifiers because same-version writes are coalesced, so binary search locates this predecessor. Searching just identifiers avoids assuming a maximum value for tuple tie-breaking.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For a length-one array:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write and snap\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Set index 0 to 5; history is [(0,5)]. Snap returns 0 and advances the current id to 1.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Write again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>Set index 0 to 6; history becomes [(0,5),(1,6)].\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Read old version\u003c/p>\u003cpre>The latest record at or before 0 is (0,5), so get(0,0) returns 5.\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class SnapshotArray:\n    def __init__(self, length):\n        self.history = [[(0, 0)] for _ in range(length)]\n        self.version = 0\n\n    def set(self, index, val):\n        records = self.history[index]\n        if records[-1][0] == self.version:\n            records[-1] = (self.version, val)\n        else:\n            records.append((self.version, val))\n\n    def snap(self):\n        result = self.version\n        self.version += 1\n        return result\n\n    def get(self, index, snap_id):\n        records = self.history[index]\n        lo, hi = 0, len(records)\n        while lo &lt; hi:\n            mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n            if records[mid][0] &lt;= snap_id:\n                lo = mid + 1\n            else:\n                hi = mid\n        return records[lo - 1][1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Initialization costs O(length) time and space. Set and snap are O(1) amortized; get takes O(log h) for h recorded versions at that index. Total space is O(length + number of sets), since each set adds at most one record. Same-version writes may use less.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A get must use the latest change at or before the snapshot, not just an exact identifier match. Unchanged indices remain zero across arbitrarily many snapshots. Replacing a record is safe only while its snapshot has not yet been taken. Repeated snaps without writes do not require new history entries. The API assumes existing snapshot ids.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Add a method that enumerates indices changed between two snapshots, using a separate change log.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store a persistent tree when snapshots must also support efficient range aggregates.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
